(window.__LORE=window.__LORE||{}).stories={"yakone_goes_on_trial_over_international_port_bombing_that_killed_23_including_a_city_councilor":{"title":"Yakone goes on trial over International Port bombing that killed 23, including a city councilor","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>Yakone, a Harbor Ward shipping broker, went on trial at the Republic City Circuit Court on Monday, charged with conspiracy and 23 counts of murder over the 14 May bombing at the International Port.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Deputy District Attorney Lian Okoro told the jury that Yakone arranged the delivery of the device to Pier Nine and the payments that funded it. He pleaded not guilty.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"x-pq\">What happened\u003c/p>\u003cp>The explosion tore through a bonded warehouse on Pier Nine at 6:40 p.m., as dock crews were changing shifts. The Harbor Authority and Republic City General Hospital each put the dead at 23, including Councilor Shen Yiran of the Harbor Ward, who was at the pier to inspect a customs delay. Another 61 people were injured.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Police recovered a leaflet from the debris carrying a Salvationist slogan. No group has claimed responsibility.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"x-pq\">The prosecution’s case\u003c/p>\u003cp>Okoro told the court that the device used a timer of Qianchan manufacture, and that a Qianchan trading house, Zhao-Meng and Sons, wired $140,000 to Yakone’s brokerage in the six weeks before the blast. He said the money was passed on to “persons who call themselves Salvationists.”\u003c/p>\u003cp>Okoro did not say what Qianchan would gain. He told the jury the state would show “who paid, not why.”\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Qianchan consulate told the Daily on Friday that it “has no knowledge of the firm’s accounts and rejects any suggestion of state involvement.” Zhao-Meng and Sons did not respond to requests for comment.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"x-pq\">\u003cb>The defense\u003c/b>\u003c/p>\u003cp>Yakone’s counsel, Marguerite Daw, told the jury the leaflet was printed on cheap paper available in any ward and that the Salvationist name was “put on this case in the first hour, and no one has asked since who wanted it there.” She said the transfers were routine payments for cargo insurance.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Councilor Shen was a vocal critic of the port’s brokerage fees. Daw said her client had lost business because of her and had no reason to kill her.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"x-pq\">\u003cb>What’s next\u003c/b>\u003c/p>\u003cp>Judge Rafael Imura said the trial is expected to run three weeks. The jury was seated on Friday after two days of questioning.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This report draws on court filings, the Harbor Authority and hospital casualty counts, and the\u003c/p>\u003chr>\u003cp>and defense’s opening statements. The Daily offered Mr. Yakone and the Salvationist cause a chance to reply; neither responded by publication.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"explainer","sec":"Explainers","kick":"","deck":["Prosecutors say Salvationist remnants carried out the blast with money routed through a Qianchan trading house. The defense says the Salvationist label is a convenient story."],"by":"","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":126,"m":9,"d":8,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Prosecutors say Salvationist remnants carried out the blast with money routed through a Qianchan trading house. The defense says the Salvationist label is a convenient story.","src":"Yakone, a Harbor Ward shipping broker, went on trial at the Republic City Circuit Court on Monday, charged with conspiracy and 23 counts of murder over the 14 May bombing at the International Port.\n\nDeputy District Attorney Lian Okoro told the jury that Yakone arranged the delivery of the device to Pier Nine and the payments that funded it. He pleaded not guilty.\n\n{pq} What happened\n\nThe explosion tore through a bonded warehouse on Pier Nine at 6:40 p.m., as dock crews were changing shifts. The Harbor Authority and Republic City General Hospital each put the dead at 23, including Councilor Shen Yiran of the Harbor Ward, who was at the pier to inspect a customs delay. Another 61 people were injured.\n\nPolice recovered a leaflet from the debris carrying a Salvationist slogan. No group has claimed responsibility.\n\n{pq} The prosecution’s case\n\nOkoro told the court that the device used a timer of Qianchan manufacture, and that a Qianchan trading house, Zhao-Meng and Sons, wired $140,000 to Yakone’s brokerage in the six weeks before the blast. He said the money was passed on to “persons who call themselves Salvationists.”\n\nOkoro did not say what Qianchan would gain. He told the jury the state would show “who paid, not why.”\n\nThe Qianchan consulate told the Daily on Friday that it “has no knowledge of the firm’s accounts and rejects any suggestion of state involvement.” Zhao-Meng and Sons did not respond to requests for comment.\n\n{pq} **The defense**\n\nYakone’s counsel, Marguerite Daw, told the jury the leaflet was printed on cheap paper available in any ward and that the Salvationist name was “put on this case in the first hour, and no one has asked since who wanted it there.” She said the transfers were routine payments for cargo insurance.\n\nCouncilor Shen was a vocal critic of the port’s brokerage fees. Daw said her client had lost business because of her and had no reason to kill her.\n\n{pq} **What’s next**\n\nJudge Rafael Imura said the trial is expected to run three weeks. The jury was seated on Friday after two days of questioning.\n\nThis report draws on court filings, the Harbor Authority and hospital casualty counts, and the\n\n---\n\nand defense’s opening statements. The Daily offered Mr. Yakone and the Salvationist cause a chance to reply; neither responded by publication."},"t":1793327985949},"yakone_trial_everything_you_need_to_know":{"title":"Yakone trial nears jury; Defense rests after 9 months","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>The trial of Yakone, a Harbor Ward shipping broker accused of arranging the 14 May bombing at the International Port, enters its last stretch this week. Closing arguments are due Wednesday, and the jury of twelve is expected to begin deliberating by the weekend.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The case has moved faster than any terrorism trial the Republic has held: 273 days from the blast to a jury. It has also stalled once, for ten weeks, over a dispute with a foreign consulate. Both facts matter to how it ends.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What happened at Pier Nine\u003c/h3>\u003cp>At 6:40 p.m. on 14 May 126 AG, as dock crews changed shifts, a bomb destroyed a bonded warehouse on Pier Nine. The Harbor Authority and Republic City General Hospital each count 23 dead. Another 61 were injured.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Among the dead was Councilor Shen Yiran of the Harbor Ward, who had gone to the pier to inspect a customs delay. Shen was a vocal critic of brokerage fees at the port. A Salvationist leaflet was recovered from the debris. No group claimed responsibility.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Timeline\u003c/h3>\u003cp>\u003cb>14 May 126 AG:\u003c/b> Bomb destroys the Pier Nine warehouse.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>2 June:\u003c/b> Yakone is arrested at his brokerage office on Anchor Row.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>21 June:\u003c/b> A grand jury indicts him on conspiracy and 23 counts of murder.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>5 September:\u003c/b> The jury is seated after two days of questioning.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>8 September:\u003c/b> Trial opens before Judge Rafael Imura.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>26 September:\u003c/b> Ren Kaito, a former courier for Salvationist remnants, testifies under a plea agreement.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>3 October:\u003c/b> The court recesses for ten weeks after the Qianchan consulate contests a subpoena for the records of Zhao-Meng and Sons.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>12 January 127 AG:\u003c/b> Hearings resume.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>28 January:\u003c/b> The defense rests after Yakone testifies for two days.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Who’s who\u003c/h3>\u003cp>\u003cb>Yakone, 43.\u003c/b> A shipping broker with an office on Anchor Row. He pleaded not guilty.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Judge Rafael Imura.\u003c/b> Presiding. He denied the defense’s request for a mistrial in November.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Deputy District Attorney Lian Okoro.\u003c/b> Leads the prosecution. She called 41 witnesses.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Marguerite Daw.\u003c/b> Leads the defense. She called nine.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Ren Kaito.\u003c/b> The state’s cooperating witness, a former Salvationist courier.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Zhao-Meng and Sons.\u003c/b> A Qianchan trading house. The state says it wired the money.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The prosecution’s case\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Okoro told the jury the case rests on three things.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The money.\u003c/b> Records show $140,000 wired from Zhao-Meng and Sons to Yakone’s brokerage in the six weeks before the blast. A forensic accountant testified that the payments matched no cargo shipped through the port.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The timer.\u003c/b> A fragment recovered from Pier Nine was of Qianchan manufacture. An ordnance expert said it was “not a part you buy by accident.”\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The man.\u003c/b> Ren testified that he picked up cash from Yakone’s office in April and delivered it to a man he knew only as “the Quartermaster.” A dock gate logbook placed Yakone near Pier Nine two days before the blast.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The defense’s case\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Counsel Marguerite Daw told the jury the leaflet was printed on paper “sold in every ward in this city,” and that the Salvationist name was “put on this case in the first hour.” She said the transfers were cargo insurance premiums, and that Ren “traded a name for a deal.”\u003c/p>\u003cp>She also said Councilor Shen’s work against the brokers gave a number of people reason to want her gone, and that no one has found the Quartermaster.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Salvationist question\u003c/h3>\u003cp>By 126 AG, the Salvationists were a post-war remnant, and nothing in the evidence ties the bombing to an organized group with a name. The prosecution says the cell was small, three or four people, and the leaflet was theirs. The defense says the label let the case be built backward from a villain.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Qianchan question\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Qianchan has said the case “concerns a private firm.” The consulate fought the subpoena for Zhao-Meng’s records on grounds of commercial privilege, and the court ordered limited production. That fight caused the October recess. Neither Qianchan nor the firm has been charged.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Okoro told reporters last week that the state “is considering its options” on the indictment. She did not say what that meant.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What is not in dispute\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The blast, the 23 dead, and that the money moved. What it was for is the case.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What the jury must decide\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Twelve people must unanimously decide whether Yakone arranged the bombing and funded it. If they convict, the maximum sentence is life imprisonment, since the United Republic has no death penalty. If they cannot agree, the judge may declare a mistrial and the state may retry.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Legal scholars say the speed helped the state. “The evidence was fresh and no witness had time to forget,” said Prof. Imelda Varga of the Republic City University law faculty. Defense lawyers say it left no time to test the Qianchan records.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What to watch\u003c/h3>\u003cp>\u003cb>Wednesday:\u003c/b> Closing arguments.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Friday:\u003c/b> Deliberations begin.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>After a verdict:\u003c/b> The Harbor Ward’s vacant council seat, empty since the blast, can go to a special election.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily draws on court filings and public hearings. Mr. Yakone’s counsel and the Qianchan consulate were offered a chance to comment on this explainer; neither replied.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"explainer","sec":"Explainers","kick":"","deck":["Nine months after the International Port bombing, the case is about to go to a jury. Here is what happened, who is who, and what the twelve will have to decide."],"by":"By Dalia Okonkwo, Politics Desk","cr":"","cty":"Republic City","y":127,"m":2,"d":10,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Nine months after the International Port bombing, the case is about to go to a jury. Here is what happened, who is who, and what the twelve will have to decide.","src":"The trial of Yakone, a Harbor Ward shipping broker accused of arranging the 14 May bombing at the International Port, enters its last stretch this week. Closing arguments are due Wednesday, and the jury of twelve is expected to begin deliberating by the weekend.\n\nThe case has moved faster than any terrorism trial the Republic has held: 273 days from the blast to a jury. It has also stalled once, for ten weeks, over a dispute with a foreign consulate. Both facts matter to how it ends.\n\n## What happened at Pier Nine\n\nAt 6:40 p.m. on 14 May 126 AG, as dock crews changed shifts, a bomb destroyed a bonded warehouse on Pier Nine. The Harbor Authority and Republic City General Hospital each count 23 dead. Another 61 were injured.\n\nAmong the dead was Councilor Shen Yiran of the Harbor Ward, who had gone to the pier to inspect a customs delay. Shen was a vocal critic of brokerage fees at the port. A Salvationist leaflet was recovered from the debris. No group claimed responsibility.\n\n## Timeline\n\n**14 May 126 AG:** Bomb destroys the Pier Nine warehouse.\n\n**2 June:** Yakone is arrested at his brokerage office on Anchor Row.\n\n**21 June:** A grand jury indicts him on conspiracy and 23 counts of murder.\n\n**5 September:** The jury is seated after two days of questioning.\n\n**8 September:** Trial opens before Judge Rafael Imura.\n\n**26 September:** Ren Kaito, a former courier for Salvationist remnants, testifies under a plea agreement.\n\n**3 October:** The court recesses for ten weeks after the Qianchan consulate contests a subpoena for the records of Zhao-Meng and Sons.\n\n**12 January 127 AG:** Hearings resume.\n\n**28 January:** The defense rests after Yakone testifies for two days.\n\n## Who’s who\n\n**Yakone, 43.** A shipping broker with an office on Anchor Row. He pleaded not guilty.\n\n**Judge Rafael Imura.** Presiding. He denied the defense’s request for a mistrial in November.\n\n**Deputy District Attorney Lian Okoro.** Leads the prosecution. She called 41 witnesses.\n\n**Marguerite Daw.** Leads the defense. She called nine.\n\n**Ren Kaito.** The state’s cooperating witness, a former Salvationist courier.\n\n**Zhao-Meng and Sons.** A Qianchan trading house. The state says it wired the money.\n\n## The prosecution’s case\n\nOkoro told the jury the case rests on three things.\n\n**The money.** Records show $140,000 wired from Zhao-Meng and Sons to Yakone’s brokerage in the six weeks before the blast. A forensic accountant testified that the payments matched no cargo shipped through the port.\n\n**The timer.** A fragment recovered from Pier Nine was of Qianchan manufacture. An ordnance expert said it was “not a part you buy by accident.”\n\n**The man.** Ren testified that he picked up cash from Yakone’s office in April and delivered it to a man he knew only as “the Quartermaster.” A dock gate logbook placed Yakone near Pier Nine two days before the blast.\n\n## The defense’s case\n\nCounsel Marguerite Daw told the jury the leaflet was printed on paper “sold in every ward in this city,” and that the Salvationist name was “put on this case in the first hour.” She said the transfers were cargo insurance premiums, and that Ren “traded a name for a deal.”\n\nShe also said Councilor Shen’s work against the brokers gave a number of people reason to want her gone, and that no one has found the Quartermaster.\n\n## The Salvationist question\n\nBy 126 AG, the Salvationists were a post-war remnant, and nothing in the evidence ties the bombing to an organized group with a name. The prosecution says the cell was small, three or four people, and the leaflet was theirs. The defense says the label let the case be built backward from a villain.\n\n## The Qianchan question\n\nQianchan has said the case “concerns a private firm.” The consulate fought the subpoena for Zhao-Meng’s records on grounds of commercial privilege, and the court ordered limited production. That fight caused the October recess. Neither Qianchan nor the firm has been charged.\n\nOkoro told reporters last week that the state “is considering its options” on the indictment. She did not say what that meant.\n\n## What is not in dispute\n\nThe blast, the 23 dead, and that the money moved. What it was for is the case.\n\n## What the jury must decide\n\nTwelve people must unanimously decide whether Yakone arranged the bombing and funded it. If they convict, the maximum sentence is life imprisonment, since the United Republic has no death penalty. If they cannot agree, the judge may declare a mistrial and the state may retry.\n\nLegal scholars say the speed helped the state. “The evidence was fresh and no witness had time to forget,” said Prof. Imelda Varga of the Republic City University law faculty. Defense lawyers say it left no time to test the Qianchan records.\n\n## What to watch\n\n**Wednesday:** Closing arguments.\n\n**Friday:** Deliberations begin.\n\n**After a verdict:** The Harbor Ward’s vacant council seat, empty since the blast, can go to a special election.\n\n//The Daily draws on court filings and public hearings. Mr. Yakone’s counsel and the Qianchan consulate were offered a chance to comment on this explainer; neither replied.//"},"t":1793342359171},"yakone_shot_by_squad_in_public_square_riots_spread_across_city_7_dead":{"title":"Yakone shot by squad in public square; riots spread across city, 7 dead","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Yakone, the Harbor Ward shipping broker convicted in the bombing of the International Port, was put to death by firing squad before several thousand persons in Republic Square yesterday morning, and within two hours the Lowtide district, where he was held by many to be a benefactor, was in flames.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Seven persons were dead last night, according to both the Republic City police and General Hospital. Police said 96 were injured, 23 of them officers; hospital officials said they had treated 143. Police said 412 persons had been arrested.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The execution\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Mr. Yakone, 43, was shot at 7:04 a.m. by a squad of the Republic Guard, the first execution carried out under the treason clause of the Charter. A physician and a court officer were present, as the statute requires. The presidency said he was pronounced dead at 7:12 a.m., after the squad had fired twice.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Three persons who stood near the front of the crowd told a reporter that Mr. Yakone was still on his feet after the first volley. A spokesman for the presidency said that was untrue and that the sentence &quot;was carried out in accordance with law.&quot; Neither account could be confirmed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>He had been convicted April 1 of treason, conspiracy and 23 counts of murder. The Supreme Court upheld the sentence May 28, four justices to one, and the President refused clemency June 5.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Weeks of vigils\u003c/h3>\u003cp>For three weeks before the execution, crowds had gathered each evening in Lowtide, a district of tenements behind the harbor, and chanted for his release. After the President's refusal the chant changed. &quot;He did nothing wrong,&quot; they said. Candles were set out on the steps of the Tenants' Council hall on Tide Street. The police made no arrests at the vigils.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The government dismissed the gatherings as the work of Salvationists. Police Commissioner Dessa Whitlock said June 10 that leaflets bearing Salvationist slogans had been found at three of them. Orrin Pell, chairman of the Tenants' Council, denied it. &quot;They hand out a leaflet and say it's us,&quot; he said. &quot;We lit candles.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The disorder\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The first fires were set on Tide Street at about 9 a.m., as persons who had watched the execution returned to the district. By noon the crowds had moved toward the Harbor Ward and the Square, where Guard reservists were posted.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;They shot him and he was still standing,&quot; said Maren Kolb, 24, a Lowtide resident who said she was in the Square. &quot;Then they shot him again. Nobody who saw that is going to be told it was Salvationists.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Commissioner Whitlock described the disorder last night as &quot;organized violence by persons exploiting a lawful sentence,&quot; and said leaflets had again been found in the debris. Mr. Pell said the violence began &quot;when the police charged a crowd that was kneeling.&quot; The police did not respond to that statement.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A curfew was imposed last night in Lowtide, the Harbor Ward, the Market District and the Old Quay, to run from 8 p.m. to 6 a.m. The City Council will meet in emergency session tomorrow.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily could not independently confirm the number of arrests, the early accounts of the execution, or the origin of the first fires. Mr. Yakone's family declined to comment.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":[],"by":"By Rurik Anand, with Dalia Okonkwo","cr":"","cty":"","y":127,"m":6,"d":17,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"","src":"{j} Yakone, the Harbor Ward shipping broker convicted in the bombing of the International Port, was put to death by firing squad before several thousand persons in Republic Square yesterday morning, and within two hours the Lowtide district, where he was held by many to be a benefactor, was in flames.\n\nSeven persons were dead last night, according to both the Republic City police and General Hospital. Police said 96 were injured, 23 of them officers; hospital officials said they had treated 143. Police said 412 persons had been arrested.\n\n## The execution\n\nMr. Yakone, 43, was shot at 7:04 a.m. by a squad of the Republic Guard, the first execution carried out under the treason clause of the Charter. A physician and a court officer were present, as the statute requires. The presidency said he was pronounced dead at 7:12 a.m., after the squad had fired twice.\n\nThree persons who stood near the front of the crowd told a reporter that Mr. Yakone was still on his feet after the first volley. A spokesman for the presidency said that was untrue and that the sentence \"was carried out in accordance with law.\" Neither account could be confirmed.\n\nHe had been convicted April 1 of treason, conspiracy and 23 counts of murder. The Supreme Court upheld the sentence May 28, four justices to one, and the President refused clemency June 5.\n\n## Weeks of vigils\n\nFor three weeks before the execution, crowds had gathered each evening in Lowtide, a district of tenements behind the harbor, and chanted for his release. After the President's refusal the chant changed. \"He did nothing wrong,\" they said. Candles were set out on the steps of the Tenants' Council hall on Tide Street. The police made no arrests at the vigils.\n\nThe government dismissed the gatherings as the work of Salvationists. Police Commissioner Dessa Whitlock said June 10 that leaflets bearing Salvationist slogans had been found at three of them. Orrin Pell, chairman of the Tenants' Council, denied it. \"They hand out a leaflet and say it's us,\" he said. \"We lit candles.\"\n\n## The disorder\n\nThe first fires were set on Tide Street at about 9 a.m., as persons who had watched the execution returned to the district. By noon the crowds had moved toward the Harbor Ward and the Square, where Guard reservists were posted.\n\n\"They shot him and he was still standing,\" said Maren Kolb, 24, a Lowtide resident who said she was in the Square. \"Then they shot him again. Nobody who saw that is going to be told it was Salvationists.\"\n\nCommissioner Whitlock described the disorder last night as \"organized violence by persons exploiting a lawful sentence,\" and said leaflets had again been found in the debris. Mr. Pell said the violence began \"when the police charged a crowd that was kneeling.\" The police did not respond to that statement.\n\nA curfew was imposed last night in Lowtide, the Harbor Ward, the Market District and the Old Quay, to run from 8 p.m. to 6 a.m. The City Council will meet in emergency session tomorrow.\n\n//The Daily could not independently confirm the number of arrests, the early accounts of the execution, or the origin of the first fires. Mr. Yakone's family declined to comment.//"},"t":1793462521212},"bomb_wrecks_pier_nine_at_least_21_dead":{"title":"Bomb wrecks pier nine; At least 21 dead","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">A bomb tore through a bonded warehouse on Pier Nine of the International Port at 6:40 p.m. yesterday, killing at least 21 persons and injuring more than 40, among them Councilwoman Shen Yiran of the Harbor Ward, who was reported dead at the scene.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Harbor Authority officials put the dead at 21 last night. Physicians at Republic City General Hospital said two more of the injured, both burned, were not expected to live. No one has claimed responsibility.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The scene\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The explosion came as the day crews were leaving and the night shift was coming on, when, Harbormaster Aldous Pryce said, &quot;the pier is as full as it ever gets.&quot; The roof of the warehouse fell into the building and a fire burned for nearly three hours along the eastern quay.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;There was a flash, and then there was no sound at all, and then everybody was screaming,&quot; said Tomas Reyes, 34, a checker who was at the gate. &quot;I saw men carrying other men out of it. I didn't see who was who.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Miss Shen, 51, had gone to the pier to look into a delay in the clearing of customs. She was in the warehouse office when the bomb went off, according to a clerk who survived.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Police inquiry\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Police Commissioner Dessa Whitlock said a printed leaflet bearing a Salvationist slogan was found in the wreckage. She said it was too early to say what it meant. &quot;It may have been left there or it may have been brought there by someone with a reason,&quot; she said. She would not say whether the police believed the explosion was connected to the unrest in Lowtide.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Commissioner said the pier had been sealed and that the Guard bomb squad was searching the other warehouses.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Relief Act\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The blast came three weeks after the Republic Council passed the Public Relief and Housing Act, 7 to 4, on April 22. The law lays a levy on brokerage fees and bonded storage at the port, to pay for tenement repairs and weekly relief to poor households in Lowtide and other districts.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Miss Shen sponsored the measure and spoke for it on the floor. &quot;The port grows rich on the backs of the people who live behind it,&quot; she said at the final reading.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Harbor Brokers' Association opposed the act and has said it will challenge it in the courts. The association's chairman said last night that its members &quot;deplore this act of violence and wish to be clear that it was not theirs.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Unrest in Lowtide\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Crowds gathered in Lowtide on May 2 and 3 when the clerks opened the relief rolls. Tenants said the rolls, which require proof of residence, would be used to evict those who could not produce it. Police said stones were thrown on Tide Street and that shop windows were broken. Eighty-eight persons were arrested and 14 injured, among them six officers. The charges against most were later dropped.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Council has said the rolls will not be used to evict anyone. The disorders had ended by May 5, the police said.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The dead\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Among those identified last night were Councilwoman Shen, Harbor Authority clerk Anselm Rudd, stevedore Marcus Oyelaran, and dock foreman Teodor Wen. The Authority has asked families of workers who have not been accounted for to report to the Harbor Authority office on Anchor Row.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Council will meet in special session this morning.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily was unable to reach the Brokers' Association's chairman for further comment. The names of other victims are being withheld until families are notified.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Councilwoman Among Victims; Leaflet Found in Debris"],"by":"By Rurik Anand","cr":"","cty":"Republic City","y":126,"m":5,"d":15,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Councilwoman Among Victims; Leaflet Found in Debris","src":"{j} A bomb tore through a bonded warehouse on Pier Nine of the International Port at 6:40 p.m. yesterday, killing at least 21 persons and injuring more than 40, among them Councilwoman Shen Yiran of the Harbor Ward, who was reported dead at the scene.\n\nHarbor Authority officials put the dead at 21 last night. Physicians at Republic City General Hospital said two more of the injured, both burned, were not expected to live. No one has claimed responsibility.\n\n## The scene\n\nThe explosion came as the day crews were leaving and the night shift was coming on, when, Harbormaster Aldous Pryce said, \"the pier is as full as it ever gets.\" The roof of the warehouse fell into the building and a fire burned for nearly three hours along the eastern quay.\n\n\"There was a flash, and then there was no sound at all, and then everybody was screaming,\" said Tomas Reyes, 34, a checker who was at the gate. \"I saw men carrying other men out of it. I didn't see who was who.\"\n\nMiss Shen, 51, had gone to the pier to look into a delay in the clearing of customs. She was in the warehouse office when the bomb went off, according to a clerk who survived.\n\n## Police inquiry\n\nPolice Commissioner Dessa Whitlock said a printed leaflet bearing a Salvationist slogan was found in the wreckage. She said it was too early to say what it meant. \"It may have been left there or it may have been brought there by someone with a reason,\" she said. She would not say whether the police believed the explosion was connected to the unrest in Lowtide.\n\nThe Commissioner said the pier had been sealed and that the Guard bomb squad was searching the other warehouses.\n\n## The Relief Act\n\nThe blast came three weeks after the Republic Council passed the Public Relief and Housing Act, 7 to 4, on April 22. The law lays a levy on brokerage fees and bonded storage at the port, to pay for tenement repairs and weekly relief to poor households in Lowtide and other districts.\n\nMiss Shen sponsored the measure and spoke for it on the floor. \"The port grows rich on the backs of the people who live behind it,\" she said at the final reading.\n\nThe Harbor Brokers' Association opposed the act and has said it will challenge it in the courts. The association's chairman said last night that its members \"deplore this act of violence and wish to be clear that it was not theirs.\"\n\n## Unrest in Lowtide\n\nCrowds gathered in Lowtide on May 2 and 3 when the clerks opened the relief rolls. Tenants said the rolls, which require proof of residence, would be used to evict those who could not produce it. Police said stones were thrown on Tide Street and that shop windows were broken. Eighty-eight persons were arrested and 14 injured, among them six officers. The charges against most were later dropped.\n\nThe Council has said the rolls will not be used to evict anyone. The disorders had ended by May 5, the police said.\n\n## The dead\n\nAmong those identified last night were Councilwoman Shen, Harbor Authority clerk Anselm Rudd, stevedore Marcus Oyelaran, and dock foreman Teodor Wen. The Authority has asked families of workers who have not been accounted for to report to the Harbor Authority office on Anchor Row.\n\nThe Council will meet in special session this morning.\n\n//The Daily was unable to reach the Brokers' Association's chairman for further comment. The names of other victims are being withheld until families are notified.//"},"t":1793343258736},"the_law_permits_it_the_street_does_not":{"title":"The law permits it; The street does not","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Republic Council has passed a law, and part of the city has answered it with stones. This newspaper has no wish to make light of what lies behind the stones. It has read the Act, it has read the Charter, and it has walked Tide Street. It concludes that the Act is lawful, that it is in the main wise, and that the disorders of last weekend were the fault of the persons who committed them and of no one else.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What the Act does\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Public Relief and Housing Act, passed by the Council 7 to 4 on April 22, does two things. It lays a levy on brokerage fees and bonded storage at the port, and with the proceeds it provides weekly relief to poor households and repairs to the tenements. That part has been argued about, chiefly by the Harbor Brokers' Association, but has not been much quarrelled with in the streets.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The second part has been. Part Four of the Act, headed Resettlement and Redevelopment, authorizes the Council to clear certain blocks of the Lowtide district and to transfer the households living there, at the Republic's expense, to the redevelopment quarter at Yu Dao or to housing in other districts of the country. The cleared ground will be paved for the new Hall of Records and for the Founders' Memorial, a monument to the Charter, which the Council has voted to raise on the site of Tide Street.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Notices were served on April 28. The first transfers are set for June 1. Each household is to receive a resettlement grant, passage for its goods, and the right to apply for a place in the new housing at Yu Dao, where building has gone on for three years.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What the Charter says\u003c/h3>\u003cp>It is the practice of those who dislike a law to say that it offends the Charter. This newspaper has looked for the offense and cannot find it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Article II gives the Council power &quot;to legislate for the general welfare of the Republic.&quot; Article IV protects the home and the holding of every citizen, and in the same breath says that no person shall be deprived of either &quot;except by law, for a public purpose, and upon just compensation.&quot; The same Article guarantees freedom of residence, and adds that this freedom is &quot;subject to such laws as the Council may enact for the public welfare.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Here is the whole matter. The Act is a law. The Hall of Records and the Memorial are public purposes, and one may think the Memorial an extravagance without thinking it an unlawful one. The Act provides compensation, which the Council may enlarge but which no court has yet called unjust. Nothing in the Charter forbids the Republic to move people in order to build for the nation, and the Framers, who had lived through the clearing of the old Yanakawan quarters, knew very well what they were permitting. They permitted it with conditions. The Act meets them.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The grievance, fairly stated\u003c/h3>\u003cp>This is not to say that those who live on Tide Street have nothing to complain of. A man who has lived all his life in one street, and been told that the street will be a monument, may be forgiven some bitterness. That the monument is to the Charter, which he is told protects his home, is an irony no committee of the Council foresaw and one the neighborhood has not missed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Miss Shen Yiran, who sponsored the Act and spoke for it on the floor, has called resettlement &quot;a ladder and not an exile.&quot; It may be. It will not look like a ladder to a widow of seventy who is asked to leave a place where she buried her husband and begin again in a quarter she has never seen. The Council would do well to remember that a law may be lawful and still be hard.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The disorders\u003c/h3>\u003cp>It is the answer to the Act, however, that this newspaper must question. On May 2 and 3, crowds in Lowtide threw stones on Tide Street and broke the windows of shops. Eighty-eight persons were arrested and fourteen injured, six of them officers. The police say the disorders had ended by the 5th.\u003c/p>\u003cp>It would be convenient for the opponents of the Act to say that these were the Act's doing. They were not. The notices were served on April 28. No household had been moved. No house had been touched. Not one block had been cleared, and not one family had set foot at Yu Dao. The disorders came before the thing they were said to resist had begun.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Nor were they confined to Lowtide. In the same days there was trouble at Yu Dao itself, which is to receive the transferred, and at Gaipan and Awazu. In the Fengdao Metropolitan District, the vast prefecture of foundries, shipyards and garrison towns, and of the new city that has grown up around them in the past ten years, stones were thrown at the offices of the labor exchange. It is not credible that the same grievance, arising in five places with five different local circumstances, had the same cause in each. It is far more credible that the disorders were what disorders have always been: the work of persons who prefer a crowd to a petition.\u003c/p>\u003cp>That is where the fault lies. The Charter gives every citizen who dislikes a law the right to say so, to assemble, to petition the Council, and to go to the courts. The tenants of Tide Street have the Tenants' Council hall, a lawyer's fee waived by the Bar Association, and a Council that has already promised that the relief rolls will not be used for eviction. They did not need stones.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What should be done\u003c/h3>\u003cp>This newspaper asks three things.\u003c/p>\u003cp>It asks the Council to publish, block by block and household by household, the list of those to be moved, and to establish an appeals board before June 1 to which any family may bring its case. It asks the Council to give those who go to Yu Dao the right to return to Lowtide, or to a place near it, if the redevelopment does not serve them. And it asks that the Memorial be reviewed, in the light of what it is costing, by a committee that includes some of those who must give up their homes for it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>It asks of the people of Lowtide only what the Charter asks of everyone: that they argue before they strike. The law will protect them if they use it. It will not protect those who break windows, and it should not.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A city can be built by consent or by force, and it has been the pride of the Republic that it was built by consent. The Act is within the law. Let the answer to it be, too.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"edit","sec":"Editorial","kick":"","deck":["The Relief Act’s resettlement clauses are within the Charter, and the disorders in Lowtide and four other districts are the fault of those who made them"],"by":"By Eerula Oka, Chief Editorial Writer","cr":"","cty":"Republic City","y":126,"m":5,"d":7,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Relief Act’s resettlement clauses are within the Charter, and the disorders in Lowtide and four other districts are the fault of those who made them","src":"{j} The Republic Council has passed a law, and part of the city has answered it with stones. This newspaper has no wish to make light of what lies behind the stones. It has read the Act, it has read the Charter, and it has walked Tide Street. It concludes that the Act is lawful, that it is in the main wise, and that the disorders of last weekend were the fault of the persons who committed them and of no one else.\n\n## What the Act does\n\nThe Public Relief and Housing Act, passed by the Council 7 to 4 on April 22, does two things. It lays a levy on brokerage fees and bonded storage at the port, and with the proceeds it provides weekly relief to poor households and repairs to the tenements. That part has been argued about, chiefly by the Harbor Brokers' Association, but has not been much quarrelled with in the streets.\n\nThe second part has been. Part Four of the Act, headed Resettlement and Redevelopment, authorizes the Council to clear certain blocks of the Lowtide district and to transfer the households living there, at the Republic's expense, to the redevelopment quarter at Yu Dao or to housing in other districts of the country. The cleared ground will be paved for the new Hall of Records and for the Founders' Memorial, a monument to the Charter, which the Council has voted to raise on the site of Tide Street.\n\nNotices were served on April 28. The first transfers are set for June 1. Each household is to receive a resettlement grant, passage for its goods, and the right to apply for a place in the new housing at Yu Dao, where building has gone on for three years.\n\n## What the Charter says\n\nIt is the practice of those who dislike a law to say that it offends the Charter. This newspaper has looked for the offense and cannot find it.\n\nArticle II gives the Council power \"to legislate for the general welfare of the Republic.\" Article IV protects the home and the holding of every citizen, and in the same breath says that no person shall be deprived of either \"except by law, for a public purpose, and upon just compensation.\" The same Article guarantees freedom of residence, and adds that this freedom is \"subject to such laws as the Council may enact for the public welfare.\"\n\nHere is the whole matter. The Act is a law. The Hall of Records and the Memorial are public purposes, and one may think the Memorial an extravagance without thinking it an unlawful one. The Act provides compensation, which the Council may enlarge but which no court has yet called unjust. Nothing in the Charter forbids the Republic to move people in order to build for the nation, and the Framers, who had lived through the clearing of the old Yanakawan quarters, knew very well what they were permitting. They permitted it with conditions. The Act meets them.\n\n## The grievance, fairly stated\n\nThis is not to say that those who live on Tide Street have nothing to complain of. A man who has lived all his life in one street, and been told that the street will be a monument, may be forgiven some bitterness. That the monument is to the Charter, which he is told protects his home, is an irony no committee of the Council foresaw and one the neighborhood has not missed.\n\nMiss Shen Yiran, who sponsored the Act and spoke for it on the floor, has called resettlement \"a ladder and not an exile.\" It may be. It will not look like a ladder to a widow of seventy who is asked to leave a place where she buried her husband and begin again in a quarter she has never seen. The Council would do well to remember that a law may be lawful and still be hard.\n\n## The disorders\n\nIt is the answer to the Act, however, that this newspaper must question. On May 2 and 3, crowds in Lowtide threw stones on Tide Street and broke the windows of shops. Eighty-eight persons were arrested and fourteen injured, six of them officers. The police say the disorders had ended by the 5th.\n\nIt would be convenient for the opponents of the Act to say that these were the Act's doing. They were not. The notices were served on April 28. No household had been moved. No house had been touched. Not one block had been cleared, and not one family had set foot at Yu Dao. The disorders came before the thing they were said to resist had begun.\n\nNor were they confined to Lowtide. In the same days there was trouble at Yu Dao itself, which is to receive the transferred, and at Gaipan and Awazu. In the Fengdao Metropolitan District, the vast prefecture of foundries, shipyards and garrison towns, and of the new city that has grown up around them in the past ten years, stones were thrown at the offices of the labor exchange. It is not credible that the same grievance, arising in five places with five different local circumstances, had the same cause in each. It is far more credible that the disorders were what disorders have always been: the work of persons who prefer a crowd to a petition.\n\nThat is where the fault lies. The Charter gives every citizen who dislikes a law the right to say so, to assemble, to petition the Council, and to go to the courts. The tenants of Tide Street have the Tenants' Council hall, a lawyer's fee waived by the Bar Association, and a Council that has already promised that the relief rolls will not be used for eviction. They did not need stones.\n\n## What should be done\n\nThis newspaper asks three things.\n\nIt asks the Council to publish, block by block and household by household, the list of those to be moved, and to establish an appeals board before June 1 to which any family may bring its case. It asks the Council to give those who go to Yu Dao the right to return to Lowtide, or to a place near it, if the redevelopment does not serve them. And it asks that the Memorial be reviewed, in the light of what it is costing, by a committee that includes some of those who must give up their homes for it.\n\nIt asks of the people of Lowtide only what the Charter asks of everyone: that they argue before they strike. The law will protect them if they use it. It will not protect those who break windows, and it should not.\n\nA city can be built by consent or by force, and it has been the pride of the Republic that it was built by consent. The Act is within the law. Let the answer to it be, too."},"t":1793344299997},"secret_police_stood_by_as_empress_was_killed_sources_say_group_behind_attack_still_unnamed":{"title":"Secret Police Stood By as Empress Was Killed, Sources Say; Group Behind Attack Still Unnamed","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>Agents of the Dai Li, the Earth Kingdom's secret police, who were posted along the parade route and in the square yesterday, did not intervene when a bomb killed Empress Hou-Ting and stood by through the first minutes of the fighting, according to three people with knowledge of the security arrangements.\u003c/p>\u003cp>One of the three, a senior army officer, went further, saying the agency may have had a hand in the attack. That claim could not be verified, and the other two sources said they had no evidence of it. No one has claimed responsibility for the killing, and the group behind it has not been named.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Watching From the Sidelines\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The three, who spoke on condition of anonymity because of the state of emergency, said Dai Li agents were stationed on rooftops and in the colonnade along the length of the parade route and had been given responsibility for the Empress's immediate security. The Garrison and the Royal Guard were responsible for the crowds.\u003c/p>\u003cp>According to a palace official, the agents &quot;did not move&quot; for several minutes after the bomb was thrown at the Empress's motorcade. A second source, a Garrison officer, said the agents later withdrew to the Upper Ring palace compound rather than join the fighting in the Square, which lasted through the night.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The army officer said that Captain Kuvira Beifong of the 85th Infantry Division, who coordinated the units that retook the city, asked the Dai Li for help and received no reply. The Daily could not reach Captain Beifong.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">An Allegation\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The army officer said the parade route had been changed at the last moment, bringing the motorcade within range of the crowd, and that the change was ordered by the Dai Li. He offered no documents. A Royal Guard source said he knew of a route change but not who had ordered it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Several people close to the court said the Empress had in recent months grown wary of the agency. The Empress, the daughter of King Kuei, took the throne in 153 AG after her father's death, which the court described at the time as natural.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Attackers\u003c/h3>\u003cp>No one has established who set off the bomb. At a press conference yesterday, military officials said they had seen increased anarchist and Equalist activity in the weeks beforehand, and some sources have linked the attack to the Red Lotus. They offered no evidence for either.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The rebels who fought the Garrison were equipped with advanced weapons and with mechanized suits from the Hundred Year War, officials said. The Garrison did not explain how such arms had reached the city.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Dai Li's Reply\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Dai Li did not respond to written questions delivered to its compound on Tuesday night, and a person who answered the door said no one was available to speak. The agency has not made a public statement since the attack.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Royal Council said it would announce its decision on the succession later today.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily offered the Dai Li an opportunity to reply to each point in this report. The accounts of the three sources could not be independently confirmed, and the allegation about the route change is reported as made.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Dai Li agents on the parade route did not move for minutes after the blast, officials say; one source alleges the agency had a hand in it"],"by":"By Kin Rhee","cr":"","cty":"BA SING SE","y":171,"m":6,"d":9,"time":"5:15 a.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Dai Li agents on the parade route did not move for minutes after the blast, officials say; one source alleges the agency had a hand in it","src":"Agents of the Dai Li, the Earth Kingdom's secret police, who were posted along the parade route and in the square yesterday, did not intervene when a bomb killed Empress Hou-Ting and stood by through the first minutes of the fighting, according to three people with knowledge of the security arrangements.\n\nOne of the three, a senior army officer, went further, saying the agency may have had a hand in the attack. That claim could not be verified, and the other two sources said they had no evidence of it. No one has claimed responsibility for the killing, and the group behind it has not been named.\n\n## Watching From the Sidelines\n\nThe three, who spoke on condition of anonymity because of the state of emergency, said Dai Li agents were stationed on rooftops and in the colonnade along the length of the parade route and had been given responsibility for the Empress's immediate security. The Garrison and the Royal Guard were responsible for the crowds.\n\nAccording to a palace official, the agents \"did not move\" for several minutes after the bomb was thrown at the Empress's motorcade. A second source, a Garrison officer, said the agents later withdrew to the Upper Ring palace compound rather than join the fighting in the Square, which lasted through the night.\n\nThe army officer said that Captain Kuvira Beifong of the 85th Infantry Division, who coordinated the units that retook the city, asked the Dai Li for help and received no reply. The Daily could not reach Captain Beifong.\n\n## An Allegation\n\nThe army officer said the parade route had been changed at the last moment, bringing the motorcade within range of the crowd, and that the change was ordered by the Dai Li. He offered no documents. A Royal Guard source said he knew of a route change but not who had ordered it.\n\nSeveral people close to the court said the Empress had in recent months grown wary of the agency. The Empress, the daughter of King Kuei, took the throne in 153 AG after her father's death, which the court described at the time as natural.\n\n## The Attackers\n\nNo one has established who set off the bomb. At a press conference yesterday, military officials said they had seen increased anarchist and Equalist activity in the weeks beforehand, and some sources have linked the attack to the Red Lotus. They offered no evidence for either.\n\nThe rebels who fought the Garrison were equipped with advanced weapons and with mechanized suits from the Hundred Year War, officials said. The Garrison did not explain how such arms had reached the city.\n\n## The Dai Li's Reply\n\nThe Dai Li did not respond to written questions delivered to its compound on Tuesday night, and a person who answered the door said no one was available to speak. The agency has not made a public statement since the attack.\n\nThe Royal Council said it would announce its decision on the succession later today.\n\n//The Daily offered the Dai Li an opportunity to reply to each point in this report. The accounts of the three sources could not be independently confirmed, and the allegation about the route change is reported as made.//"},"t":1793462041114},"ba_sing_se_in_turmoil_after_empress_s_killing_crowds_defy_troops_as_armored_suits_roll_out":{"title":"Ba Sing Se in Turmoil After Empress’s Killing; Crowds Defy Troops as Armored Suits Roll Out","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Crowds chanting against the government filled the streets of the Lower and Middle Rings of this capital for most of the day, and the Garrison, which said tonight it had regained the Square and the Upper Ring, put helicopters over the city and old armored suits from the Hundred Year War into the streets in an effort to quiet them.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The disorder followed the killing this morning of Empress Hou-Ting by a bomb thrown at her motorcade. It has left much of the capital under a state of emergency, with fires burning in at least four districts at the hour of filing.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Crowds and Chants\u003c/h3>\u003cp>By early afternoon, the first crowds had gathered on Cauldron Street in the Lower Ring, and by 4 p.m. they numbered in the thousands. They did not chant for the Empress. &quot;Who let them in?&quot; they shouted at lines of troops, and, again and again, &quot;Where were the guards?&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The question was a pointed one. At a press conference this afternoon, military officials acknowledged that warnings of increased Equalist and anarchist activity in the city had not been acted on in the weeks before the attack. They did not say why.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;They knew,&quot; said Bo Zhen, 52, a porter, standing near a barricade of overturned carts. &quot;They are going to say they didn't, and then they are going to find a poor man to blame.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Offices Burned, Shops Looted\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The crowds turned toward symbols of money by evening. The headquarters of the Earth Kingdom Rail Company, on Plum Avenue, was burning when this correspondent passed it at 7:30 p.m., and the offices of the Jade Lantern Insurance Company were gutted. The Middle Ring branch of the Royal Credit Bank was broken open and looted, and a grain syndicate's warehouse was emptied by crowds who carried the sacks away on their backs.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Nearby, in the Exchange district, smoke rose from the new Exchange building, which was struck by two explosions at about 6:15 p.m. Residents said the fire brigades could not get through.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Helicopters and Suits\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Four helicopters of the Garrison's air wing began circling the Lower Ring at about 6 p.m., with loudspeakers that told the crowds to &quot;return to your homes; the Kingdom stands.&quot; Leaflets with the same message were dropped over the Middle Ring.\u003c/p>\u003cp>At about 8 p.m., three of the heavy mechanized suits that were used in the Hundred Year War were brought down to an intersection on Cauldron Street from the Upper Ring armory, according to a Garrison officer, who asked not to be named. Rebels were also reported to have used suits of the same kind in last night's fighting. This correspondent saw no suit fire its weapons.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The effect on the crowd was mixed. Those in the front rows sat down in the street. Further back, bricks were thrown, and a bottle shattered against the leg of one suit. Many in the crowd, which was thinning by 9 p.m., said they had never seen the machines except in pictures.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Count\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Garrison Command put the number killed in the capital at 23. The Lower Ring Free Clinic, which has been treating the wounded in its courtyard, said it had recorded 31. Neither figure could be confirmed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A Garrison spokesman, Major Jiang Tao, said the Square and the Upper Ring had been &quot;secured,&quot; and that order in the other rings would be restored by morning. The succession has not been announced.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily offered the Garrison Command an opportunity to respond to the account of the helicopters and suits; Major Jiang's statement is reproduced above. This dispatch was filed from the Middle Ring.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Garrison says it has retaken the Square, but the Lower and Middle Rings remain contested, and corporate offices burn"],"by":"By Kin Rhee","cr":"","cty":"Ba sing se","y":171,"m":6,"d":8,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Garrison says it has retaken the Square, but the Lower and Middle Rings remain contested, and corporate offices burn","src":"{j} Crowds chanting against the government filled the streets of the Lower and Middle Rings of this capital for most of the day, and the Garrison, which said tonight it had regained the Square and the Upper Ring, put helicopters over the city and old armored suits from the Hundred Year War into the streets in an effort to quiet them.\n\nThe disorder followed the killing this morning of Empress Hou-Ting by a bomb thrown at her motorcade. It has left much of the capital under a state of emergency, with fires burning in at least four districts at the hour of filing.\n\n## Crowds and Chants\n\nBy early afternoon, the first crowds had gathered on Cauldron Street in the Lower Ring, and by 4 p.m. they numbered in the thousands. They did not chant for the Empress. \"Who let them in?\" they shouted at lines of troops, and, again and again, \"Where were the guards?\"\n\nThe question was a pointed one. At a press conference this afternoon, military officials acknowledged that warnings of increased Equalist and anarchist activity in the city had not been acted on in the weeks before the attack. They did not say why.\n\n\"They knew,\" said Bo Zhen, 52, a porter, standing near a barricade of overturned carts. \"They are going to say they didn't, and then they are going to find a poor man to blame.\"\n\n## Offices Burned, Shops Looted\n\nThe crowds turned toward symbols of money by evening. The headquarters of the Earth Kingdom Rail Company, on Plum Avenue, was burning when this correspondent passed it at 7:30 p.m., and the offices of the Jade Lantern Insurance Company were gutted. The Middle Ring branch of the Royal Credit Bank was broken open and looted, and a grain syndicate's warehouse was emptied by crowds who carried the sacks away on their backs.\n\nNearby, in the Exchange district, smoke rose from the new Exchange building, which was struck by two explosions at about 6:15 p.m. Residents said the fire brigades could not get through.\n\n## Helicopters and Suits\n\nFour helicopters of the Garrison's air wing began circling the Lower Ring at about 6 p.m., with loudspeakers that told the crowds to \"return to your homes; the Kingdom stands.\" Leaflets with the same message were dropped over the Middle Ring.\n\nAt about 8 p.m., three of the heavy mechanized suits that were used in the Hundred Year War were brought down to an intersection on Cauldron Street from the Upper Ring armory, according to a Garrison officer, who asked not to be named. Rebels were also reported to have used suits of the same kind in last night's fighting. This correspondent saw no suit fire its weapons.\n\nThe effect on the crowd was mixed. Those in the front rows sat down in the street. Further back, bricks were thrown, and a bottle shattered against the leg of one suit. Many in the crowd, which was thinning by 9 p.m., said they had never seen the machines except in pictures.\n\n## The Count\n\nThe Garrison Command put the number killed in the capital at 23. The Lower Ring Free Clinic, which has been treating the wounded in its courtyard, said it had recorded 31. Neither figure could be confirmed.\n\nA Garrison spokesman, Major Jiang Tao, said the Square and the Upper Ring had been \"secured,\" and that order in the other rings would be restored by morning. The succession has not been announced.\n\n//The Daily offered the Garrison Command an opportunity to respond to the account of the helicopters and suits; Major Jiang's statement is reproduced above. This dispatch was filed from the Middle Ring.//"},"t":1793462132995},"exchange_ends_first_session_at_the_lowest_level_in_its_history_building_damaged_by_blasts":{"title":"Exchange Ends First Session at the Lowest Level in Its History; Building Damaged by Blasts","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>The Ba Sing Se Exchange closed its first session yesterday at the lowest level in its history, which was also its only level, and remained shut this morning after two explosions damaged the building in the night of fighting that followed the death of Empress Hou-Ting.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Exchange's chairman, Hong Bai, and its registrar, Lun Fei, each told the Times that the all-share index, set at 1,000 at the opening bell, stood at 660 when trading was halted at 10:40 a.m. No further trades were made. Both said the Exchange would not reopen until the state of emergency was lifted.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">A Public Exchange, for One Morning\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Exchange opened yesterday as the first in the Earth Kingdom to admit any adult citizen as an investor, replacing the old Bourse, which was limited to members. It had been timed to coincide with the founding-day celebrations. Mr. Hong said about 3,000 people were in the building at the opening.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Empress was killed at about 9:20 a.m., when a bomb was thrown at her motorcade in Ba Sing Se Square. Mr. Lun said the first sale orders arrived within five minutes. &quot;There were no buyers at any price,&quot; he said.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Night's Damage\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Two explosions struck the Exchange building at about 6:15 p.m., according to Mr. Hong, who said he was in a nearby street at the time. Part of the trading floor is open to the sky, and the records office is burned.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Garrison Command, through its spokesman, Major Jiang Tao, put the number of dead near the Exchange at 11. The figure is the Garrison's and has not been checked against hospital records. Major Jiang said the district had been cleared at about 1:30 a.m. by units under Captain Kuvira Beifong of the 85th Infantry Division. He did not say who set off the explosions or whether the building had been targeted.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Banks and the Currency\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Central Bank said last night that all banks would remain closed under the emergency order. A bank officer, who asked not to be named, said depositors had begun queuing at some branches on the Lower Ring before the news of the Empress's death was widely known.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Qianchan Treasury said Earth Kingdom paper was trading at a discount of about 30 percent in Gaoling. The Times could not confirm that figure independently.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Government\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The succession remains undecided, and no member of the royal household or the Royal Council has spoken publicly since the attack. Officials of the Council said an announcement was expected later today. The city is under curfew, and parts of the capital were still burning at the hour of filing.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"bst","t":"report","sec":"Business","kick":"","deck":["Trading halted until further notice under state of emergency; Garrison reports 11 dead near the Exchange"],"by":"By Mei Ran","cr":"","cty":"","y":171,"m":6,"d":9,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Trading halted until further notice under state of emergency; Garrison reports 11 dead near the Exchange","src":"The Ba Sing Se Exchange closed its first session yesterday at the lowest level in its history, which was also its only level, and remained shut this morning after two explosions damaged the building in the night of fighting that followed the death of Empress Hou-Ting.\n\nThe Exchange's chairman, Hong Bai, and its registrar, Lun Fei, each told the Times that the all-share index, set at 1,000 at the opening bell, stood at 660 when trading was halted at 10:40 a.m. No further trades were made. Both said the Exchange would not reopen until the state of emergency was lifted.\n\n## A Public Exchange, for One Morning\n\nThe Exchange opened yesterday as the first in the Earth Kingdom to admit any adult citizen as an investor, replacing the old Bourse, which was limited to members. It had been timed to coincide with the founding-day celebrations. Mr. Hong said about 3,000 people were in the building at the opening.\n\nThe Empress was killed at about 9:20 a.m., when a bomb was thrown at her motorcade in Ba Sing Se Square. Mr. Lun said the first sale orders arrived within five minutes. \"There were no buyers at any price,\" he said.\n\n## The Night's Damage\n\nTwo explosions struck the Exchange building at about 6:15 p.m., according to Mr. Hong, who said he was in a nearby street at the time. Part of the trading floor is open to the sky, and the records office is burned.\n\nThe Garrison Command, through its spokesman, Major Jiang Tao, put the number of dead near the Exchange at 11. The figure is the Garrison's and has not been checked against hospital records. Major Jiang said the district had been cleared at about 1:30 a.m. by units under Captain Kuvira Beifong of the 85th Infantry Division. He did not say who set off the explosions or whether the building had been targeted.\n\n## Banks and the Currency\n\nThe Central Bank said last night that all banks would remain closed under the emergency order. A bank officer, who asked not to be named, said depositors had begun queuing at some branches on the Lower Ring before the news of the Empress's death was widely known.\n\nThe Qianchan Treasury said Earth Kingdom paper was trading at a discount of about 30 percent in Gaoling. The Times could not confirm that figure independently.\n\n## The Government\n\nThe succession remains undecided, and no member of the royal household or the Royal Council has spoken publicly since the attack. Officials of the Council said an announcement was expected later today. The city is under curfew, and parts of the capital were still burning at the hour of filing."},"t":1793463303767},"ba_sing_se_exchange_plunges_on_opening_day_after_empress_is_killed_bombs_hit_trading_floor":{"title":"Ba Sing Se Exchange Plunges on Opening Day After Empress Is Killed; Bombs Hit Trading Floor","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>The Ba Sing Se Exchange, which opened to the public for the first time this morning, collapsed within an hour of the news that Empress Hou-Ting had been killed by a bomb, and was struck by two explosions in the evening as fighting spread through the capital.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Exchange officials said prices fell 34 percent between the opening bell and a halt at 10:40 a.m. Trading did not resume. Fighting was continuing near the palace district at the hour of filing.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">An Opening Day Cut Short\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Exchange was to be the showpiece of the founding-day celebrations. It replaces the old Ba Sing Se Bourse, which was open only to members, and the Empress's government had promised that it would for the first time let any citizen buy shares. About 3,000 people were in the building when the bell rang, an Exchange spokesman said.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Empress was killed at about 9:20 a.m., when a bomb was thrown at her motorcade during the parade in Ba Sing Se Square. Word reached the trading floor within minutes. &quot;Every order was a sell order,&quot; said a clerk reached by telephone, who gave his name as Wen. &quot;There was no one left to buy.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Bombs at the Exchange\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Two explosions hit the Exchange building at about 6:15 p.m., according to residents of the district. A garrison spokesman said at least 11 people were killed and that the figure could not be confirmed. Fire crews were unable to reach the building.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The garrison said armed rebels had been moving through the district. It did not say who they were, and it had not established whether the Exchange was a target or was caught in the fighting.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">A Flight to Safety\u003c/h3>\u003cp>In Gaoling, shares fell 9 percent at the opening of trading, according to the Gaoling Exchange. The Qianchan note rose 4 percent against Earth Kingdom paper, and the price of gold reached a record on the Gaoling market.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Treasury of the Republic of Qianchan said it was &quot;monitoring the situation closely.&quot; Mei-Lin Hao, who has traded on the Gaoling floor for 41 years, said the day recalled the crash of 153 AG. &quot;That one took a week to get going,&quot; she said. &quot;This one took an hour.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Qianchan foreign ministry said it was checking on its nationals in Ba Sing Se. Among those reported to have taken part in the defense of the Square was Captain Kuvira Beifong of the Earth Kingdom's 85th Infantry Division, who also holds Qianchan citizenship.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>Figures are from the Ba Sing Se and Gaoling exchanges and the Qianchan Treasury. The Ba Sing Se casualty count could not be confirmed.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"qr","t":"report","sec":"Business","kick":"","deck":["Gold and Qianchan Won surge as Earth Kingdom Yuan is dumped"],"by":"","cr":"","cty":"","y":171,"m":6,"d":9,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Gold and Qianchan Won surge as Earth Kingdom Yuan is dumped","src":"The Ba Sing Se Exchange, which opened to the public for the first time this morning, collapsed within an hour of the news that Empress Hou-Ting had been killed by a bomb, and was struck by two explosions in the evening as fighting spread through the capital.\n\nExchange officials said prices fell 34 percent between the opening bell and a halt at 10:40 a.m. Trading did not resume. Fighting was continuing near the palace district at the hour of filing.\n\n## An Opening Day Cut Short\n\nThe Exchange was to be the showpiece of the founding-day celebrations. It replaces the old Ba Sing Se Bourse, which was open only to members, and the Empress's government had promised that it would for the first time let any citizen buy shares. About 3,000 people were in the building when the bell rang, an Exchange spokesman said.\n\nThe Empress was killed at about 9:20 a.m., when a bomb was thrown at her motorcade during the parade in Ba Sing Se Square. Word reached the trading floor within minutes. \"Every order was a sell order,\" said a clerk reached by telephone, who gave his name as Wen. \"There was no one left to buy.\"\n\n## Bombs at the Exchange\n\nTwo explosions hit the Exchange building at about 6:15 p.m., according to residents of the district. A garrison spokesman said at least 11 people were killed and that the figure could not be confirmed. Fire crews were unable to reach the building.\n\nThe garrison said armed rebels had been moving through the district. It did not say who they were, and it had not established whether the Exchange was a target or was caught in the fighting.\n\n## A Flight to Safety\n\nIn Gaoling, shares fell 9 percent at the opening of trading, according to the Gaoling Exchange. The Qianchan note rose 4 percent against Earth Kingdom paper, and the price of gold reached a record on the Gaoling market.\n\nThe Treasury of the Republic of Qianchan said it was \"monitoring the situation closely.\" Mei-Lin Hao, who has traded on the Gaoling floor for 41 years, said the day recalled the crash of 153 AG. \"That one took a week to get going,\" she said. \"This one took an hour.\"\n\nThe Qianchan foreign ministry said it was checking on its nationals in Ba Sing Se. Among those reported to have taken part in the defense of the Square was Captain Kuvira Beifong of the Earth Kingdom's 85th Infantry Division, who also holds Qianchan citizenship.\n\n//Figures are from the Ba Sing Se and Gaoling exchanges and the Qianchan Treasury. The Ba Sing Se casualty count could not be confirmed.//"},"t":1793463139062},"stocks_plunge_around_the_world_exchanges_halt_trading":{"title":"Stocks Plunge Around the World; Exchanges Halt Trading","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>Stock prices collapsed on exchanges around the world on Monday and Tuesday, forcing the Republic City Exchange and three others to suspend trading, as the failure of a major Caldera lending house and doubt over who will succeed King Kuei of the Earth Kingdom shook confidence in every market that deals across the oceans.\u003c/p>\u003cp>By the close yesterday, the Caldera Exchange was down 31 percent over two sessions, according to its officials. The Republic City Exchange had lost 22 percent, and the Ba Sing Se Bourse, which closed for two days, was reported down more than a quarter. It was the sharpest fall in stock prices anywhere since the end of the civil war.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Lender That Failed\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The first blow came on Sunday, Sept. 27, when the Hakuryu Trust, a Caldera house that had lent heavily against cargoes of rice and shipping contracts, told its creditors it could not meet its obligations. The Trust is owed, by its own account, the equivalent of several years' revenue of the Caldera harbor.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;Everyone was lending to everyone else against the same few cargoes,&quot; said Henrietta Cole, an economist at Republic City University. &quot;When one of them stopped, the others had no way of knowing who was next.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">A King's Death\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The failure came on top of an uneasiness that had been building since August, when Kuei died in Ba Sing Se. The Earth Kingdom's bonds, held by banks from Qianchan to Republic City, have lost nearly a fifth of their value since then, and traders said the question of who will govern in the capital has made them reluctant to hold anything issued there.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Finance Commissioner Edric Tolliver said in a statement last night that the Republic's banks &quot;remain sound and are in a position to meet every obligation.&quot; He declined to say how much of the Hakuryu Trust's debt is held in the Republic.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Port Feels It\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The effects were being felt at the docks within hours. Two shipping lines at the International Port said they had cancelled bookings, and the Harbor Authority said it had received requests to defer fees from eleven firms. &quot;The ships still come in,&quot; said Harbormaster Aldous Pryce, &quot;but nobody is sure who will pay for the cargo.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Exchange officials in Republic City said trading would resume this morning, but under limits that prevent any share from falling more than 8 percent in a day.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Lorna Voss, president of the Republic City Exchange, said the limits were intended &quot;to give people time to look at what they own.&quot; Asked whether she expected the fall to continue, she said, &quot;I would not like to say.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This article draws on figures supplied by the Republic City, Caldera and Ba Sing Se exchanges. The Hakuryu Trust did not return calls.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"Business","kick":"","deck":["Failure of a Caldera lender and doubt over the Earth Kingdom’s succession set off the steepest two-day fall in memory"],"by":"By Henitta Zhe","cr":"","cty":"Republic City","y":153,"m":9,"d":30,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Failure of a Caldera lender and doubt over the Earth Kingdom’s succession set off the steepest two-day fall in memory","src":"Stock prices collapsed on exchanges around the world on Monday and Tuesday, forcing the Republic City Exchange and three others to suspend trading, as the failure of a major Caldera lending house and doubt over who will succeed King Kuei of the Earth Kingdom shook confidence in every market that deals across the oceans.\n\nBy the close yesterday, the Caldera Exchange was down 31 percent over two sessions, according to its officials. The Republic City Exchange had lost 22 percent, and the Ba Sing Se Bourse, which closed for two days, was reported down more than a quarter. It was the sharpest fall in stock prices anywhere since the end of the civil war.\n\n## The Lender That Failed\n\nThe first blow came on Sunday, Sept. 27, when the Hakuryu Trust, a Caldera house that had lent heavily against cargoes of rice and shipping contracts, told its creditors it could not meet its obligations. The Trust is owed, by its own account, the equivalent of several years' revenue of the Caldera harbor.\n\n\"Everyone was lending to everyone else against the same few cargoes,\" said Henrietta Cole, an economist at Republic City University. \"When one of them stopped, the others had no way of knowing who was next.\"\n\n## A King's Death\n\nThe failure came on top of an uneasiness that had been building since August, when Kuei died in Ba Sing Se. The Earth Kingdom's bonds, held by banks from Qianchan to Republic City, have lost nearly a fifth of their value since then, and traders said the question of who will govern in the capital has made them reluctant to hold anything issued there.\n\nFinance Commissioner Edric Tolliver said in a statement last night that the Republic's banks \"remain sound and are in a position to meet every obligation.\" He declined to say how much of the Hakuryu Trust's debt is held in the Republic.\n\n## The Port Feels It\n\nThe effects were being felt at the docks within hours. Two shipping lines at the International Port said they had cancelled bookings, and the Harbor Authority said it had received requests to defer fees from eleven firms. \"The ships still come in,\" said Harbormaster Aldous Pryce, \"but nobody is sure who will pay for the cargo.\"\n\nExchange officials in Republic City said trading would resume this morning, but under limits that prevent any share from falling more than 8 percent in a day.\n\nLorna Voss, president of the Republic City Exchange, said the limits were intended \"to give people time to look at what they own.\" Asked whether she expected the fall to continue, she said, \"I would not like to say.\"\n\n//This article draws on figures supplied by the Republic City, Caldera and Ba Sing Se exchanges. The Hakuryu Trust did not return calls.//"},"t":1793463261535},"riots_erupt_in_joha_after_dock_workers_march_3_dead":{"title":"Riots Erupt in Joha After Dock Workers’ March; 3 Dead","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>Rioting broke out in the port city of Joha yesterday afternoon after police broke up a march of dock workers, leaving three people dead and dozens injured and prompting the prefectural government to impose a curfew on the harbor district.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The disorder, the worst in Joha since the end of the civil war, came less than two weeks after the sharpest fall in stock prices on the Caldera Exchange in a generation, and less than two months after the death of King Kuei of the Earth Kingdom, whose passing has brought leftist groups back into the streets in several cities.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">A March Turns Violent\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The march was organized by the Joha Dockworkers' Union to mourn the king and to press a claim for back pay that the union says the harbor's employers have owed since the port was rebuilt. Union officials put the crowd at about 6,000. The police said it was nearer 4,000.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The marchers were to have gone from the union hall to the quay memorial. Police stopped them at the Customs Road crossing at about 2 p.m., saying the union had no permit for the route. Witnesses said a line of officers with shields moved forward, bottles and stones were thrown, and the police fired into the crowd.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Three people were killed, according to both the police and the union. Joha Municipal Hospital said it treated 52 injured, among them 19 police officers. The police said 140 persons were arrested.\u003c/p>\u003cp>By evening, crowds had broken shop windows along Customs Road and set fire to a freight office on the eastern quay. Fire crews were kept from the quay until after 9 p.m.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;We marched for a dead king and for our wages,&quot; said Haruki Nomura, the union's secretary. &quot;Nobody told us the police had a different march in mind.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Police Commissioner Rin Katsura said officers &quot;acted when the order of the street was lost.&quot; He said he could not yet say who threw the first stone. &quot;That will be established,&quot; he said.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Exchange and the Docks\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The anger in Joha has an economic root that predates the march. Prices on the Caldera Exchange fell 31 percent over two sessions on Sept. 28 and 29, according to exchange officials, and trading was suspended for a day. Shipping and shipbuilding shares, which had led the rise of recent years, fell hardest.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two Joha companies, a shipping line and a yard, laid off 2,300 workers on Oct. 5, the union said. Neither company confirmed the number. &quot;It was the worst two days since the war,&quot; said Keiji Arai, a professor of economics at Caldera University. &quot;The question for Joha is whether it was a market correction or the start of a long slide.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Ito Matsuda, 38, a rigger, said he lost his job on Monday. &quot;They owed us for the rebuilding and now they say there is no money at all,&quot; he said, standing near the freight office after the fire. &quot;What do you want a man to do?&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">A Name With a History\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Joha carries a weight that other ports do not. The Salvationist movement held its first mass gatherings on the city's quay in the last years of the Hundred Year War, and residents still call it the blood city, after a shooting there in July 100 AG. Many of those on the march had their own memories of that day, or their parents'.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Governor Genji Hoshino did not link yesterday's events to the Salvationists. &quot;Order will be kept in this city,&quot; he said in a statement. &quot;Those who broke it will answer to the law.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Commissioner Katsura said handbills from leftist groups not previously active in Joha had been found among the marchers, but he declined to say what the groups were. Mr. Nomura said the handbills had been handed out by &quot;people the union did not invite.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Curfew\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Governor's office said a curfew would be in force in the harbor district from 8 p.m. to 6 a.m. until further notice. Schools in the district were closed for the day.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Diet is to take up the matter on Monday. The Royal Household Office had no comment.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This article is based on interviews with 14 people in Joha, and on figures supplied by Joha Municipal Hospital and the prefectural police. The Scire could not independently confirm the size of the crowd or who threw the first stone.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["Violence follows a memorial for King Kuei and a plunge in Caldera stock prices; port district placed under curfew"],"by":"","cr":"","cty":"Joha, Tajisaki","y":127,"m":10,"d":10,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Violence follows a memorial for King Kuei and a plunge in Caldera stock prices; port district placed under curfew","src":"Rioting broke out in the port city of Joha yesterday afternoon after police broke up a march of dock workers, leaving three people dead and dozens injured and prompting the prefectural government to impose a curfew on the harbor district.\n\nThe disorder, the worst in Joha since the end of the civil war, came less than two weeks after the sharpest fall in stock prices on the Caldera Exchange in a generation, and less than two months after the death of King Kuei of the Earth Kingdom, whose passing has brought leftist groups back into the streets in several cities.\n\n## A March Turns Violent\n\nThe march was organized by the Joha Dockworkers' Union to mourn the king and to press a claim for back pay that the union says the harbor's employers have owed since the port was rebuilt. Union officials put the crowd at about 6,000. The police said it was nearer 4,000.\n\nThe marchers were to have gone from the union hall to the quay memorial. Police stopped them at the Customs Road crossing at about 2 p.m., saying the union had no permit for the route. Witnesses said a line of officers with shields moved forward, bottles and stones were thrown, and the police fired into the crowd.\n\nThree people were killed, according to both the police and the union. Joha Municipal Hospital said it treated 52 injured, among them 19 police officers. The police said 140 persons were arrested.\n\nBy evening, crowds had broken shop windows along Customs Road and set fire to a freight office on the eastern quay. Fire crews were kept from the quay until after 9 p.m.\n\n\"We marched for a dead king and for our wages,\" said Haruki Nomura, the union's secretary. \"Nobody told us the police had a different march in mind.\"\n\nPolice Commissioner Rin Katsura said officers \"acted when the order of the street was lost.\" He said he could not yet say who threw the first stone. \"That will be established,\" he said.\n\n## The Exchange and the Docks\n\nThe anger in Joha has an economic root that predates the march. Prices on the Caldera Exchange fell 31 percent over two sessions on Sept. 28 and 29, according to exchange officials, and trading was suspended for a day. Shipping and shipbuilding shares, which had led the rise of recent years, fell hardest.\n\nTwo Joha companies, a shipping line and a yard, laid off 2,300 workers on Oct. 5, the union said. Neither company confirmed the number. \"It was the worst two days since the war,\" said Keiji Arai, a professor of economics at Caldera University. \"The question for Joha is whether it was a market correction or the start of a long slide.\"\n\nIto Matsuda, 38, a rigger, said he lost his job on Monday. \"They owed us for the rebuilding and now they say there is no money at all,\" he said, standing near the freight office after the fire. \"What do you want a man to do?\"\n\n## A Name With a History\n\nJoha carries a weight that other ports do not. The Salvationist movement held its first mass gatherings on the city's quay in the last years of the Hundred Year War, and residents still call it the blood city, after a shooting there in July 100 AG. Many of those on the march had their own memories of that day, or their parents'.\n\nGovernor Genji Hoshino did not link yesterday's events to the Salvationists. \"Order will be kept in this city,\" he said in a statement. \"Those who broke it will answer to the law.\"\n\nCommissioner Katsura said handbills from leftist groups not previously active in Joha had been found among the marchers, but he declined to say what the groups were. Mr. Nomura said the handbills had been handed out by \"people the union did not invite.\"\n\n## The Curfew\n\nThe Governor's office said a curfew would be in force in the harbor district from 8 p.m. to 6 a.m. until further notice. Schools in the district were closed for the day.\n\nThe Diet is to take up the matter on Monday. The Royal Household Office had no comment.\n\n//This article is based on interviews with 14 people in Joha, and on figures supplied by Joha Municipal Hospital and the prefectural police. The Scire could not independently confirm the size of the crowd or who threw the first stone.//"},"t":1793462731469},"unrest_grips_joha_cradle_of_salvationists_as_fire_lord_stays_out_of_sight":{"title":"Unrest Grips Joha, Cradle of Salvationists, as Fire Lord Stays Out of Sight","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>For more than a year, strikes and riots have spread from the port city of Joha across the surrounding prefecture, and now the Fire Lord, Zuko, has not been seen in public since he visited here on Sept. 11. Officials will not say where he is.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Royal Household Office said on Sept. 14 that the Fire Lord was resting on the advice of his physicians, and that &quot;certain disturbances&quot; in Joha were the work of Salvationist sympathizers. It gave no date for his return.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Royal Household Office, Sept. 14, 154 AG (abridged): &quot;His Majesty is resting at a residence of the Household on the advice of his physicians. He has not cancelled his obligations to Tajisaki and will resume them when the physicians permit.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The statement, and the silence around it, have left this port of about 400,000 people under curfews that cover four of the prefecture's six districts, with the dead counted in two ways by two authorities and no explanation of the difference.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">A Spreading Unrest\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The trouble began in August 1953 AG, within days of the death of King Kuei of the Earth Kingdom. Leftist groups that had been quiet since the civil war held rallies in several cities, among them Caldera City. In Joha, dock workers marched on Oct. 9 to mourn the king and to demand pay they say they have been owed since the harbor was rebuilt.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Prefectural police broke up the march. Three people were killed, according to both the police and the Joha Dockworkers' Union. Larger marches followed on the next two Saturdays, and by December there were riots in the grain towns inland.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The &quot;Blood City&quot;\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Joha is not a place where officials can claim surprise at a crowd. The Salvationist movement held its first mass gatherings on the city's quay in the last years of the Hundred Year War, and residents still call it the blood city. The name comes from a gathering there in July 100 AG, the week word of Emperor Ozai's death reached the coast, that ended in killings. Residents interviewed by the Scire disagree about who fired.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Governor Genji Hoshino said in an interview that the movement &quot;has been dead for thirty years and is being dug up by people who never saw it.&quot; Police Commissioner Rin Katsura said a cache of weapons found in a warehouse in April, along with handbills carrying old Salvationist slogans, showed &quot;someone is doing the digging on purpose.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The dockworkers' union rejects that account. Its secretary, Haruki Nomura, said the marches were organized by the union. &quot;The Salvationists came later,&quot; he said, &quot;and the Governor needs them to have come first.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Two Counts of the Dead\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The prefectural police say 41 people have been killed since October 153 AG. Dr. Ayame Ōtsuka, director of Joha Municipal Hospital, said its records show 118 deaths from injuries and gunshot wounds over the same period.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Neither side has accounted for the difference. The Governor's office said the hospital's figure included &quot;deaths from other causes during the curfew.&quot; Dr. Ōtsuka rejected that, saying each case is individually documented. The Scire has not been shown the hospital's records.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The question has reached the Diet. &quot;The prefecture is being governed by curfew, and the Diet has not been told by whom,&quot; Daichi Mori, a member of the Reform bloc, said at a sitting on Sept. 2, according to the transcript.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Fire Lord's Visit\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Fire Lord arrived in Joha on Sept. 11 in what officials described as an effort to calm the city. Accounts of what followed differ.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Household Office says the visit was cut short because His Majesty grew tired. Two dock workers who were near the motorcade said it stopped at the quay memorial after a crowd surged forward, and that they heard shots. The police said they could not confirm that shots were fired. The Scire could not independently confirm either account.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Crown Princess Izumi has taken over the ceremonial duties of the throne. A sitting of the Diet that had been set for Sept. 16 was postponed, with no new date announced.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Unanswered Questions\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Household Office has not said where the Fire Lord is, what he is being treated for, or why the visit ended early. The prefectural government has not said who ordered the curfews, or under what authority.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Scire asked the Household Office on Sept. 17 where the Fire Lord is resting and when he will resume his public duties. It had not replied by the time this article went to press.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This article is based on interviews with 23 people in Joha between Sept. 12 and 19, and on records supplied by the prefectural police and Joha Municipal Hospital. The casualty figures could not be reconciled.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"Interior","kick":"","deck":["A year of strikes and riots has spread across the prefecture, the number of dead is in dispute, and Zuko has not appeared in public since visiting the city"],"by":"By Mika Tsuneda","cr":"","cty":"JOHA, Tajisaki","y":154,"m":9,"d":20,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"A year of strikes and riots has spread across the prefecture, the number of dead is in dispute, and Zuko has not appeared in public since visiting the city","src":"For more than a year, strikes and riots have spread from the port city of Joha across the surrounding prefecture, and now the Fire Lord, Zuko, has not been seen in public since he visited here on Sept. 11. Officials will not say where he is.\n\nThe Royal Household Office said on Sept. 14 that the Fire Lord was resting on the advice of his physicians, and that \"certain disturbances\" in Joha were the work of Salvationist sympathizers. It gave no date for his return.\n\n**Official statement.** Royal Household Office, Sept. 14, 154 AG (abridged): \"His Majesty is resting at a residence of the Household on the advice of his physicians. He has not cancelled his obligations to Tajisaki and will resume them when the physicians permit.\"\n\nThe statement, and the silence around it, have left this port of about 400,000 people under curfews that cover four of the prefecture's six districts, with the dead counted in two ways by two authorities and no explanation of the difference.\n\n## A Spreading Unrest\n\nThe trouble began in August 1953 AG, within days of the death of King Kuei of the Earth Kingdom. Leftist groups that had been quiet since the civil war held rallies in several cities, among them Caldera City. In Joha, dock workers marched on Oct. 9 to mourn the king and to demand pay they say they have been owed since the harbor was rebuilt.\n\nPrefectural police broke up the march. Three people were killed, according to both the police and the Joha Dockworkers' Union. Larger marches followed on the next two Saturdays, and by December there were riots in the grain towns inland.\n\n## The \"Blood City\"\n\nJoha is not a place where officials can claim surprise at a crowd. The Salvationist movement held its first mass gatherings on the city's quay in the last years of the Hundred Year War, and residents still call it the blood city. The name comes from a gathering there in July 100 AG, the week word of Emperor Ozai's death reached the coast, that ended in killings. Residents interviewed by the Scire disagree about who fired.\n\nGovernor Genji Hoshino said in an interview that the movement \"has been dead for thirty years and is being dug up by people who never saw it.\" Police Commissioner Rin Katsura said a cache of weapons found in a warehouse in April, along with handbills carrying old Salvationist slogans, showed \"someone is doing the digging on purpose.\"\n\nThe dockworkers' union rejects that account. Its secretary, Haruki Nomura, said the marches were organized by the union. \"The Salvationists came later,\" he said, \"and the Governor needs them to have come first.\"\n\n## Two Counts of the Dead\n\nThe prefectural police say 41 people have been killed since October 153 AG. Dr. Ayame Ōtsuka, director of Joha Municipal Hospital, said its records show 118 deaths from injuries and gunshot wounds over the same period.\n\nNeither side has accounted for the difference. The Governor's office said the hospital's figure included \"deaths from other causes during the curfew.\" Dr. Ōtsuka rejected that, saying each case is individually documented. The Scire has not been shown the hospital's records.\n\nThe question has reached the Diet. \"The prefecture is being governed by curfew, and the Diet has not been told by whom,\" Daichi Mori, a member of the Reform bloc, said at a sitting on Sept. 2, according to the transcript.\n\n## The Fire Lord's Visit\n\nThe Fire Lord arrived in Joha on Sept. 11 in what officials described as an effort to calm the city. Accounts of what followed differ.\n\nThe Household Office says the visit was cut short because His Majesty grew tired. Two dock workers who were near the motorcade said it stopped at the quay memorial after a crowd surged forward, and that they heard shots. The police said they could not confirm that shots were fired. The Scire could not independently confirm either account.\n\nCrown Princess Izumi has taken over the ceremonial duties of the throne. A sitting of the Diet that had been set for Sept. 16 was postponed, with no new date announced.\n\n## Unanswered Questions\n\nThe Household Office has not said where the Fire Lord is, what he is being treated for, or why the visit ended early. The prefectural government has not said who ordered the curfews, or under what authority.\n\nThe Scire asked the Household Office on Sept. 17 where the Fire Lord is resting and when he will resume his public duties. It had not replied by the time this article went to press.\n\n//This article is based on interviews with 23 people in Joha between Sept. 12 and 19, and on records supplied by the prefectural police and Joha Municipal Hospital. The casualty figures could not be reconciled.//"},"t":1793462801685},"can_spirit_vine_energy_replace_nuclear_or_gas":{"title":"Can Spirit-Vine Energy Replace Nuclear or Gas?","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>Spirit-vine energy has emerged as one of the most debated technologies of the century, with researchers, industrial firms and government planners divided over whether it can realistically replace natural gas and nuclear power as the backbone of modern electrical grids.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The debate intensified this week following the publication of several studies by Republic University and the Qianchan Institute of Applied Energetics. While researchers reported significant advances in spirit-vine energy storage and transmission, experts cautioned that laboratory successes do not necessarily translate into reliable nationwide power generation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;The technology has moved beyond the experimental stage,&quot; said Professor Huan Liao, an energy systems specialist at Republic University. &quot;The question facing governments is no longer whether spirit-vine reactors work. The question is whether they can operate safely and consistently enough to replace existing sources of power.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Supporters of spirit-vine energy argue that the technology offers several advantages over conventional fuels. Unlike gas-fired plants, spirit-vine facilities require no imported fuel shipments and produce no industrial emissions. Advocates also note that spirit-vine reactors occupy less space than many traditional generating stations while producing substantial quantities of electricity.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Several industrial firms have already announced pilot projects intended to demonstrate the technology's commercial viability. Construction has begun on two experimental generating facilities outside Republic City, with additional projects proposed in Qianchan and the Earth Confederation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Yet critics warn that fundamental questions remain unanswered.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Energy analyst Sora Wen stated that existing spirit-vine facilities have yet to demonstrate uninterrupted operation over long periods.\u003c/p>\u003cblockquote>&quot;A power grid requires reliability above all else,&quot; Wen said. &quot;Cities can tolerate higher costs. They cannot tolerate uncertainty. At present, we do not know how spirit-vine reactors will perform after ten years, twenty years or thirty years of continuous operation.&quot;\u003c/blockquote>\u003cp>Safety concerns continue to shape the discussion. Municipal regulators have requested additional oversight provisions, citing previous incidents involving unstable spirit-vine reactions and the limited historical record available to engineers.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Several lawmakers have urged caution before approving large-scale deployment.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;No responsible government should abandon proven infrastructure until replacement technologies are thoroughly tested,&quot; Assemblyman Taro Bei said during a committee hearing Tuesday.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Nuclear energy supporters have also challenged claims that spirit-vine power represents a superior alternative. They argue that modern reactor designs already provide reliable, low-emission electricity and benefit from decades of operational experience unavailable to spirit-vine facilities.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Gas producers, meanwhile, contend that existing fuel networks remain essential for industrial stability and emergency generation during periods of peak demand.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Economic considerations may ultimately determine the outcome. According to industry estimates, spirit-vine facilities remain significantly more expensive to construct than conventional gas plants. Backers argue that long-term operating costs could fall once infrastructure expands and new techniques mature.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Merchants and investors have responded cautiously. Trading in several energy corporations rose following publication of the latest research, though market analysts described the reaction as speculative rather than transformative.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For consumers, the immediate effects are likely to be limited. Energy ministries across multiple nations have indicated that gas, nuclear and spirit-vine technologies will continue operating side by side for the foreseeable future.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Even among researchers who support further development, few expect spirit-vine energy to replace existing power sources within the next decade.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;The future may belong to spirit-vines,&quot; Professor Liao said. &quot;But the future has not arrived quite yet.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Government regulators are expected to review several major licensing applications later this year, decisions that could determine whether spirit-vine energy remains a promising experiment or becomes the next pillar of the world's industrial economy.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"qr","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Researchers, utility companies and governments are divided over whether spirit-vine power can move from experimental projects to the foundation of modern energy grids."],"by":"By Mika Kizano","cr":"","cty":"Zaofu City, Qianchan","y":0,"m":2,"d":19,"time":"18:40","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Researchers, utility companies and governments are divided over whether spirit-vine power can move from experimental projects to the foundation of modern energy grids.","src":"Spirit-vine energy has emerged as one of the most debated technologies of the century, with researchers, industrial firms and government planners divided over whether it can realistically replace natural gas and nuclear power as the backbone of modern electrical grids.\n\nThe debate intensified this week following the publication of several studies by Republic University and the Qianchan Institute of Applied Energetics. While researchers reported significant advances in spirit-vine energy storage and transmission, experts cautioned that laboratory successes do not necessarily translate into reliable nationwide power generation.\n\n\"The technology has moved beyond the experimental stage,\" said Professor Huan Liao, an energy systems specialist at Republic University. \"The question facing governments is no longer whether spirit-vine reactors work. The question is whether they can operate safely and consistently enough to replace existing sources of power.\"\n\nSupporters of spirit-vine energy argue that the technology offers several advantages over conventional fuels. Unlike gas-fired plants, spirit-vine facilities require no imported fuel shipments and produce no industrial emissions. Advocates also note that spirit-vine reactors occupy less space than many traditional generating stations while producing substantial quantities of electricity.\n\nSeveral industrial firms have already announced pilot projects intended to demonstrate the technology's commercial viability. Construction has begun on two experimental generating facilities outside Republic City, with additional projects proposed in Qianchan and the Earth Confederation.\n\nYet critics warn that fundamental questions remain unanswered.\n\nEnergy analyst Sora Wen stated that existing spirit-vine facilities have yet to demonstrate uninterrupted operation over long periods.\n\n> \"A power grid requires reliability above all else,\" Wen said. \"Cities can tolerate higher costs. They cannot tolerate uncertainty. At present, we do not know how spirit-vine reactors will perform after ten years, twenty years or thirty years of continuous operation.\"\n\nSafety concerns continue to shape the discussion. Municipal regulators have requested additional oversight provisions, citing previous incidents involving unstable spirit-vine reactions and the limited historical record available to engineers.\n\nSeveral lawmakers have urged caution before approving large-scale deployment.\n\n\"No responsible government should abandon proven infrastructure until replacement technologies are thoroughly tested,\" Assemblyman Taro Bei said during a committee hearing Tuesday.\n\nNuclear energy supporters have also challenged claims that spirit-vine power represents a superior alternative. They argue that modern reactor designs already provide reliable, low-emission electricity and benefit from decades of operational experience unavailable to spirit-vine facilities.\n\nGas producers, meanwhile, contend that existing fuel networks remain essential for industrial stability and emergency generation during periods of peak demand.\n\nEconomic considerations may ultimately determine the outcome. According to industry estimates, spirit-vine facilities remain significantly more expensive to construct than conventional gas plants. Backers argue that long-term operating costs could fall once infrastructure expands and new techniques mature.\n\nMerchants and investors have responded cautiously. Trading in several energy corporations rose following publication of the latest research, though market analysts described the reaction as speculative rather than transformative.\n\nFor consumers, the immediate effects are likely to be limited. Energy ministries across multiple nations have indicated that gas, nuclear and spirit-vine technologies will continue operating side by side for the foreseeable future.\n\nEven among researchers who support further development, few expect spirit-vine energy to replace existing power sources within the next decade.\n\n\"The future may belong to spirit-vines,\" Professor Liao said. \"But the future has not arrived quite yet.\"\n\nGovernment regulators are expected to review several major licensing applications later this year, decisions that could determine whether spirit-vine energy remains a promising experiment or becomes the next pillar of the world's industrial economy."},"t":1793491932630},"37_minutes_of_unexplained_light_and_magnetic_surge_over_agna_qel_a_spirit_oasis_cordoned_off":{"title":"37 minutes of unexplained light and magnetic surge over Agna Qel’a; Spirit Oasis cordoned off","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>A disturbance lasting 37 minutes lit the sky over Agna Qel'a on the night of Dec. 20 and drove the city's geomagnetic readings to about eight times their usual storm peak, according to the Qianchan Meteorological Institute's station here. No injuries from the event itself have been reported. Officials have not said what caused it.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What we know\u003c/h3>\u003cp>\u003cb>The readings.\u003c/b> The station recorded a magnetic swing of 2,400 nanotesla at 11:12 p.m., against a peak of about 300 in the strongest storm of the past decade (station log, Dec. 20; Institute records, 180-189 AG). The Institute logged nine separate pulses at roughly four-minute intervals, then silence.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The sky.\u003c/b> Residents described a band of white and pale green light that covered most of the northern sky and, in at least three accounts, a single column of light rising from the Spirit Oasis. The aurora faded at about 11:49 p.m.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The Oasis.\u003c/b> Wardens of the Spirit Oasis told the Report that the pool's temperature rose from -2 to +33 degrees Celsius in about 20 minutes, according to their own instruments, and that the ice on the surrounding terraces melted. The Report has not seen the wardens' records.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The injured.\u003c/b> Northern Tribe health officials said five people were treated at Agna Qel'a General for temporary blindness and frostbite after watching from the harbor wall. All were released.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">A visitor, and a cordon\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Two people close to the Chief's household told the Report that the Avatar arrived by air on the afternoon of Dec. 20, and was at the Oasis during the disturbance. The Avatar's office in Republic City declined to comment. The Northern Tribe's Chief did not respond to written questions.\u003c/p>\u003cp>By 1 a.m., armed guards with no Tribe insignia had closed the roads to the Oasis. People at the barrier said the guards identified themselves only as a &quot;security detachment.&quot; Residents have taken to calling them the White Guard. The Chief's office said the cordon was &quot;a precaution agreed with our guests.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The city\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Agna Qel'a has about 640,000 residents (Northern Tribe census, 187 AG), rebuilt since the Fire Nation's invasion of 99 AG killed an estimated 800,000. Many in the city say the night recalled stories of the Spirit World that their grandparents told. &quot;My mother said the moon spirits used to speak on nights like this,&quot; said Kaito Mizushima, 61, a fisherman, at the harbor wall. &quot;I thought it was a story.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Qianchan Meteorological Institute said it would publish its full readings by the end of the week. The Northern Tribe's council is to meet on Monday.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"qr","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Instruments recorded nine pulses at the northern pole, and the Northern Tribe’s wardens say the Oasis pool warmed by 35 degrees. Officials have given no cause."],"by":"","cr":"","cty":"AGNA QEL’A","y":189,"m":12,"d":21,"time":"9:05 a.m. Updated 4:30 p.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Instruments recorded nine pulses at the northern pole, and the Northern Tribe’s wardens say the Oasis pool warmed by 35 degrees. Officials have given no cause.","src":"A disturbance lasting 37 minutes lit the sky over Agna Qel'a on the night of Dec. 20 and drove the city's geomagnetic readings to about eight times their usual storm peak, according to the Qianchan Meteorological Institute's station here. No injuries from the event itself have been reported. Officials have not said what caused it.\n\n## What we know\n\n**The readings.** The station recorded a magnetic swing of 2,400 nanotesla at 11:12 p.m., against a peak of about 300 in the strongest storm of the past decade (station log, Dec. 20; Institute records, 180-189 AG). The Institute logged nine separate pulses at roughly four-minute intervals, then silence.\n\n**The sky.** Residents described a band of white and pale green light that covered most of the northern sky and, in at least three accounts, a single column of light rising from the Spirit Oasis. The aurora faded at about 11:49 p.m.\n\n**The Oasis.** Wardens of the Spirit Oasis told the Report that the pool's temperature rose from -2 to +33 degrees Celsius in about 20 minutes, according to their own instruments, and that the ice on the surrounding terraces melted. The Report has not seen the wardens' records.\n\n**The injured.** Northern Tribe health officials said five people were treated at Agna Qel'a General for temporary blindness and frostbite after watching from the harbor wall. All were released.\n\n## A visitor, and a cordon\n\nTwo people close to the Chief's household told the Report that the Avatar arrived by air on the afternoon of Dec. 20, and was at the Oasis during the disturbance. The Avatar's office in Republic City declined to comment. The Northern Tribe's Chief did not respond to written questions.\n\nBy 1 a.m., armed guards with no Tribe insignia had closed the roads to the Oasis. People at the barrier said the guards identified themselves only as a \"security detachment.\" Residents have taken to calling them the White Guard. The Chief's office said the cordon was \"a precaution agreed with our guests.\"\n\n## The city\n\nAgna Qel'a has about 640,000 residents (Northern Tribe census, 187 AG), rebuilt since the Fire Nation's invasion of 99 AG killed an estimated 800,000. Many in the city say the night recalled stories of the Spirit World that their grandparents told. \"My mother said the moon spirits used to speak on nights like this,\" said Kaito Mizushima, 61, a fisherman, at the harbor wall. \"I thought it was a story.\"\n\nThe Qianchan Meteorological Institute said it would publish its full readings by the end of the week. The Northern Tribe's council is to meet on Monday."},"t":1793518615981},"yakone_guilty_of_treason_and_23_counts_of_murder_sentenced_to_death_by_firing_squad":{"title":"Yakone guilty of treason and 23 counts of murder, sentenced to death by firing squad","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">A Circuit Court jury on Wednesday found Yakone guilty of treason, conspiracy and 23 counts of murder over the 14 May bombing at the International Port, and Judge Rafael Imura sentenced him to death by firing squad.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The sentence is to be carried out at Republic Square on a date the Executive will set. Under the Republic's charter, treason is the only offense that carries death, and all capital sentences go automatically to the Supreme Court of the United Republic within 60 days.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The jury of twelve deliberated for three days. The foreman read the verdicts in the courtroom, which was filled with relatives of the dead. Yakone, 43, stood without speaking.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;The defendant did not act alone, and the court has not found that he did,&quot; Imura said before sentencing. &quot;The court has found that he acted, and that 23 people are dead because he did.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">How the case changed\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The treason count was added on 24 February, after the state asked to amend the indictment to allege that Yakone took money from a foreign power to fund a war on the Republic. Imura granted the motion over the defense's objection and reopened the evidence for three weeks. Closing arguments resumed on 25 March.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Defense counsel Marguerite Daw said she would appeal. &quot;A man has been sentenced to death on a count the state did not have when the trial began,&quot; she said outside the courthouse.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Deputy District Attorney Lian Okoro said the amendment &quot;followed the evidence, as it should.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The verdict came 321 days after the blast, which legal scholars have called the fastest terrorism case in the Republic's history.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Reaction\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Qianchan consulate said it &quot;rejects the court's characterization of any foreign government's role&quot; and that the case &quot;concerns a private firm.&quot; The firm, Zhao-Meng and Sons, has been dissolved.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily offered Mr. Yakone's counsel and the Qianchan consulate a chance to reply. Both did.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"Crime & Courts","kick":"","deck":["The jury took three days. The sentence will be carried out at Republic Square."],"by":"By Dalia Okonkwo, Courts Desk","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":127,"m":4,"d":1,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The jury took three days. The sentence will be carried out at Republic Square.","src":"{j} A Circuit Court jury on Wednesday found Yakone guilty of treason, conspiracy and 23 counts of murder over the 14 May bombing at the International Port, and Judge Rafael Imura sentenced him to death by firing squad.\n\nThe sentence is to be carried out at Republic Square on a date the Executive will set. Under the Republic's charter, treason is the only offense that carries death, and all capital sentences go automatically to the Supreme Court of the United Republic within 60 days.\n\nThe jury of twelve deliberated for three days. The foreman read the verdicts in the courtroom, which was filled with relatives of the dead. Yakone, 43, stood without speaking.\n\n\"The defendant did not act alone, and the court has not found that he did,\" Imura said before sentencing. \"The court has found that he acted, and that 23 people are dead because he did.\"\n\n## How the case changed\n\nThe treason count was added on 24 February, after the state asked to amend the indictment to allege that Yakone took money from a foreign power to fund a war on the Republic. Imura granted the motion over the defense's objection and reopened the evidence for three weeks. Closing arguments resumed on 25 March.\n\nDefense counsel Marguerite Daw said she would appeal. \"A man has been sentenced to death on a count the state did not have when the trial began,\" she said outside the courthouse.\n\nDeputy District Attorney Lian Okoro said the amendment \"followed the evidence, as it should.\"\n\nThe verdict came 321 days after the blast, which legal scholars have called the fastest terrorism case in the Republic's history.\n\n## Reaction\n\nThe Qianchan consulate said it \"rejects the court's characterization of any foreign government's role\" and that the case \"concerns a private firm.\" The firm, Zhao-Meng and Sons, has been dissolved.\n\n//The Daily offered Mr. Yakone's counsel and the Qianchan consulate a chance to reply. Both did.//"},"t":1793537603715},"prosecution_the_money_the_timer_the_man_defense_a_story_that_arrived_before_the_evidence":{"title":"Prosecution: \"The money, the timer, the man.\" Defense: \"A story that arrived before the evidence.\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Prosecutors told a Circuit Court jury on Wednesday that three facts convict Yakone of the International Port bombing: the money that reached his brokerage, the timer of Qianchan manufacture, and the man who took delivery.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;Twenty-three people went to work on Pier Nine and did not come home,&quot; Deputy District Attorney Lian Okoro said. &quot;Mr. Yakone is the reason.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Defense counsel Marguerite Daw told the jury the state had &quot;a story that arrived before the evidence.&quot; She said Ren Kaito, the prosecution's cooperating witness, &quot;traded a name for a deal,&quot; and that no one saw her client at Pier Nine on 14 May.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Yakone, who testified for two days in January, told the court he &quot;bought and sold cargo and never bought a bomb.&quot; In cross-examination, Okoro asked him to explain the $140,000. He said the payments were insurance premiums for shipments the state had not examined.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Judge Rafael Imura told the jury it must find the money was intended to fund the bombing, not merely that it moved. The jury of twelve is expected to begin deliberating Friday.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily offered the Qianchan consulate a chance to reply. It said it &quot;continues to reject any suggestion of state involvement.&quot;\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"Crime & Courts","kick":"","deck":["Closing arguments open in the International Port bombing trial."],"by":"By Dalia Okonkwo, Courts Desk","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":127,"m":2,"d":11,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Closing arguments open in the International Port bombing trial.","src":"{j} Prosecutors told a Circuit Court jury on Wednesday that three facts convict Yakone of the International Port bombing: the money that reached his brokerage, the timer of Qianchan manufacture, and the man who took delivery.\n\n\"Twenty-three people went to work on Pier Nine and did not come home,\" Deputy District Attorney Lian Okoro said. \"Mr. Yakone is the reason.\"\n\nDefense counsel Marguerite Daw told the jury the state had \"a story that arrived before the evidence.\" She said Ren Kaito, the prosecution's cooperating witness, \"traded a name for a deal,\" and that no one saw her client at Pier Nine on 14 May.\n\nYakone, who testified for two days in January, told the court he \"bought and sold cargo and never bought a bomb.\" In cross-examination, Okoro asked him to explain the $140,000. He said the payments were insurance premiums for shipments the state had not examined.\n\nJudge Rafael Imura told the jury it must find the money was intended to fund the bombing, not merely that it moved. The jury of twelve is expected to begin deliberating Friday.\n\n//The Daily offered the Qianchan consulate a chance to reply. It said it \"continues to reject any suggestion of state involvement.\"//"},"t":1793537603716},"a_terror_trial_in_nine_months_is_the_speed_justice":{"title":"A terror trial in nine months. Is the speed justice?","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">No terror case in the United Republic has gone from blast to jury as fast as the Yakone trial: 273 days.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;It is the quickest I have seen, and I have studied the files going back to the founding,&quot; said Prof. Imelda Varga of the Republic City University law faculty. She argues speed is not the same as haste: the court kept a ten-week recess and saw 50 witnesses.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Port Authority and the Councilors' Association, which lost one of its own, welcomed the pace. &quot;Every month the case dragged, the Salvationists got another month to say it never happened,&quot; Association chair Orin Tessel said.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The defense bar is less sure. Barrister Eleni Voss said the court &quot;did not give the defense the Qianchan records until after the prosecution had told the jury what was in them.&quot; Defense counsel Marguerite Daw raised the same complaint at trial, and Judge Imura denied her request for a mistrial.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The strongest argument for speed is that the evidence is fresh. The strongest argument against it is that the main foreign-policy question, whether Qianchan paid for this, has not been tested at all. Deputy District Attorney Lian Okoro said the state would show &quot;who paid, not why,&quot; and it did.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If the jury convicts, the speed will be called a triumph. If it acquits, it will be called a rush. The trial itself did not change.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"opinion","sec":"Voices","kick":"","deck":["The Yakone case went from blast to jury in 273 days, faster than any terrorism trial the Republic has held."],"by":"By Teodora Brandt","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":127,"m":2,"d":12,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Yakone case went from blast to jury in 273 days, faster than any terrorism trial the Republic has held.","src":"{j} No terror case in the United Republic has gone from blast to jury as fast as the Yakone trial: 273 days.\n\n\"It is the quickest I have seen, and I have studied the files going back to the founding,\" said Prof. Imelda Varga of the Republic City University law faculty. She argues speed is not the same as haste: the court kept a ten-week recess and saw 50 witnesses.\n\nThe Port Authority and the Councilors' Association, which lost one of its own, welcomed the pace. \"Every month the case dragged, the Salvationists got another month to say it never happened,\" Association chair Orin Tessel said.\n\nThe defense bar is less sure. Barrister Eleni Voss said the court \"did not give the defense the Qianchan records until after the prosecution had told the jury what was in them.\" Defense counsel Marguerite Daw raised the same complaint at trial, and Judge Imura denied her request for a mistrial.\n\nThe strongest argument for speed is that the evidence is fresh. The strongest argument against it is that the main foreign-policy question, whether Qianchan paid for this, has not been tested at all. Deputy District Attorney Lian Okoro said the state would show \"who paid, not why,\" and it did.\n\nIf the jury convicts, the speed will be called a triumph. If it acquits, it will be called a rush. The trial itself did not change."},"t":1793537603717},"who_will_fill_councilor_shen_s_seat":{"title":"Who will fill Councilor Shen's seat?","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Councilor Shen Yiran's seat on the Republic City Council has been empty since the bombing, and the verdict in the Yakone trial will not change that.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Under the city charter, a vacancy is filled by a special election held no later than 180 days after the seat is declared vacant. The Council voted in June to delay the election until the trial ended. Its clerk said on Thursday that it will be set within 30 days of a verdict.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two candidates have said they will run. Union organizer Haruki Doyle, a longtime critic of the Port Authority, said Shen's work on brokerage fees &quot;should be finished by someone who read the file.&quot; Deputy Harbormaster Orin Tessel, who also chairs the Councilors' Association, said he would run &quot;because the ward asked, not because anyone died.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Neither has said how they would vote on the Qianchan trade measures now before the Council.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily asked Mr. Doyle and Mr. Tessel for comment; both replied by Thursday.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["The Harbor Ward has been without a voice on the Council for nine months."],"by":"By Rurik Anand, City Hall Desk","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":127,"m":2,"d":12,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Harbor Ward has been without a voice on the Council for nine months.","src":"{j} Councilor Shen Yiran's seat on the Republic City Council has been empty since the bombing, and the verdict in the Yakone trial will not change that.\n\nUnder the city charter, a vacancy is filled by a special election held no later than 180 days after the seat is declared vacant. The Council voted in June to delay the election until the trial ended. Its clerk said on Thursday that it will be set within 30 days of a verdict.\n\nTwo candidates have said they will run. Union organizer Haruki Doyle, a longtime critic of the Port Authority, said Shen's work on brokerage fees \"should be finished by someone who read the file.\" Deputy Harbormaster Orin Tessel, who also chairs the Councilors' Association, said he would run \"because the ward asked, not because anyone died.\"\n\nNeither has said how they would vote on the Qianchan trade measures now before the Council.\n\n//The Daily asked Mr. Doyle and Mr. Tessel for comment; both replied by Thursday.//"},"t":1793537603718},"who_decides_whether_yakone_dies":{"title":"Who decides whether Yakone dies?","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The sentence handed down on 1 April, death by firing squad at Republic Square, is not final. It is the start of a process with three decision-makers, a 60-day clock, and a city that has already shown it is watching.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Yakone, 43, was convicted of treason, conspiracy and 23 counts of murder over the 14 May bombing at the International Port. Only treason carries death under the Republic's charter. The other counts carry life imprisonment. That is why a case that began as a murder trial turned, in its last weeks, into a treason case.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The charge that makes it capital\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Article VII of the Charter defines treason as &quot;levying war against the Republic or adhering to its enemies, giving them aid.&quot; The clause has been in the Charter since the Republic's first years, written when the Hundred Year War was a recent memory and Salvationist remnants were still numerous.\u003c/p>\u003cp>It has been charged only twice, and never carried out. Both earlier cases, in 108 and 119 AG, ended in acquittals or reduced counts. Legal scholars say prosecutors avoided it because it requires proof of an enemy, and the Republic has never formally declared one.\u003c/p>\u003cp>In February, Deputy District Attorney Lian Okoro asked to amend the indictment to allege that the bombing was funded by a foreign power. The court's finding was that the money, routed through the Qianchan firm Zhao-Meng and Sons, made the Salvationist cell a hostile force acting for a state. Qianchan denies it.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The road to the Supreme Court\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Every capital sentence goes automatically to the Supreme Court of the Republic, with no filing needed from the defense, which then has 60 days to rule. That puts the deadline on 31 May 127 AG.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The five justices can affirm the conviction and the sentence, affirm the conviction and commute the sentence to life, or send the case back for retrial, in whole or in part.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The defense has said it will argue three points: that adding the treason count on 24 February, three months into the trial, denied Yakone notice; that the Qianchan bank records were disclosed to the defense after the jury had heard about them; and that the prosecution's cooperating witness, Ren Kaito, testified under a plea agreement that gave him a reason to lie. The state says all three were raised at trial and ruled on.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The people who hold the pen\u003c/h3>\u003cp>If the Court affirms the sentence, the next step belongs to the Executive. The President sets the date, and the Charter gives the President sole power to commute. The Council cannot overrule either. A Clemency Board advises the President, but its recommendation is not binding.\u003c/p>\u003cp>No sentence has ever reached the clemency stage. A presidential spokesperson said the Executive &quot;will not comment on a case before the court.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Republic Square\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The statute says a capital sentence must be carried out in public, between dawn and 9 a.m., by a squad of the Republic Guard in the presence of a physician and a court officer. It was written for the first treason case in 108 AG and has never been used.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Republic Square was laid out in 109 AG as the city's main public ground, and it has hosted the Founders' Day parade, the Charter reading and, in 118 AG, the thanksgiving after the end of the Salvationist emergency. Using it for this purpose would put the state's most solemn punishment at the center of the city's civic life.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Qianchan question\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Qianchan has said the case &quot;concerns a private firm&quot; and that it &quot;rejects the court's characterization of any foreign government's role.&quot; Zhao-Meng and Sons has been dissolved and its former partners have not commented.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The treason verdict makes the diplomatic question harder. A conviction that finds a foreign state funded an attack, without that state ever being charged or named in court, leaves the Council with trade measures it cannot easily vote on and a government that has said nothing about whether it believes the finding.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Lowtide and the street\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The unrest that began in the Lowtide district on 1 April, with crowds chanting &quot;HE did nothing wrong!&quot;, has been the first test. The police report two dead and 112 arrests. On Friday the Council extended curfews in Lowtide and the Harbor Ward by 14 days.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Residents who have spoken to the Daily describe Yakone, a broker who sold cargo and, they say, paid rents when the Port Authority would not, as a figure who did them favors. The state says he is the man who killed 23 people. Both accounts are being asserted loudly in the same streets.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Commissioner Dessa Whitlock has said Salvationist leaflets were found among the debris. The Lowtide Tenants' Council has said the crowds were no one's creation.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What to watch\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Mid-April: the defense and the state file briefs with the Supreme Court. May: oral argument, date to be set. 31 May: the Court's 60-day deadline. After the ruling: a decision by the Executive, with no deadline in the Charter. Meanwhile: the curfew review, and the Harbor Ward special election, which the clerk has said will be set within 30 days of the verdict.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The case has moved faster than any terrorism case the Republic has tried. What comes next has no precedent to follow.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily draws on court filings, the Charter, and public statements. Mr. Yakone's counsel, the presidency, and the Qianchan consulate were offered a chance to comment.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"explainer","sec":"Explainers","kick":"","deck":["A treason clause the Republic has never used to take a life, a court with 60 days to review it, and a city under curfew."],"by":"By Dalia Okonkwo, Courts Desk","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":127,"m":4,"d":4,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"A treason clause the Republic has never used to take a life, a court with 60 days to review it, and a city under curfew.","src":"{j} The sentence handed down on 1 April, death by firing squad at Republic Square, is not final. It is the start of a process with three decision-makers, a 60-day clock, and a city that has already shown it is watching.\n\nYakone, 43, was convicted of treason, conspiracy and 23 counts of murder over the 14 May bombing at the International Port. Only treason carries death under the Republic's charter. The other counts carry life imprisonment. That is why a case that began as a murder trial turned, in its last weeks, into a treason case.\n\n## The charge that makes it capital\n\nArticle VII of the Charter defines treason as \"levying war against the Republic or adhering to its enemies, giving them aid.\" The clause has been in the Charter since the Republic's first years, written when the Hundred Year War was a recent memory and Salvationist remnants were still numerous.\n\nIt has been charged only twice, and never carried out. Both earlier cases, in 108 and 119 AG, ended in acquittals or reduced counts. Legal scholars say prosecutors avoided it because it requires proof of an enemy, and the Republic has never formally declared one.\n\nIn February, Deputy District Attorney Lian Okoro asked to amend the indictment to allege that the bombing was funded by a foreign power. The court's finding was that the money, routed through the Qianchan firm Zhao-Meng and Sons, made the Salvationist cell a hostile force acting for a state. Qianchan denies it.\n\n## The road to the Supreme Court\n\nEvery capital sentence goes automatically to the Supreme Court of the Republic, with no filing needed from the defense, which then has 60 days to rule. That puts the deadline on 31 May 127 AG.\n\nThe five justices can affirm the conviction and the sentence, affirm the conviction and commute the sentence to life, or send the case back for retrial, in whole or in part.\n\nThe defense has said it will argue three points: that adding the treason count on 24 February, three months into the trial, denied Yakone notice; that the Qianchan bank records were disclosed to the defense after the jury had heard about them; and that the prosecution's cooperating witness, Ren Kaito, testified under a plea agreement that gave him a reason to lie. The state says all three were raised at trial and ruled on.\n\n## The people who hold the pen\n\nIf the Court affirms the sentence, the next step belongs to the Executive. The President sets the date, and the Charter gives the President sole power to commute. The Council cannot overrule either. A Clemency Board advises the President, but its recommendation is not binding.\n\nNo sentence has ever reached the clemency stage. A presidential spokesperson said the Executive \"will not comment on a case before the court.\"\n\n## Republic Square\n\nThe statute says a capital sentence must be carried out in public, between dawn and 9 a.m., by a squad of the Republic Guard in the presence of a physician and a court officer. It was written for the first treason case in 108 AG and has never been used.\n\nRepublic Square was laid out in 109 AG as the city's main public ground, and it has hosted the Founders' Day parade, the Charter reading and, in 118 AG, the thanksgiving after the end of the Salvationist emergency. Using it for this purpose would put the state's most solemn punishment at the center of the city's civic life.\n\n## The Qianchan question\n\nQianchan has said the case \"concerns a private firm\" and that it \"rejects the court's characterization of any foreign government's role.\" Zhao-Meng and Sons has been dissolved and its former partners have not commented.\n\nThe treason verdict makes the diplomatic question harder. A conviction that finds a foreign state funded an attack, without that state ever being charged or named in court, leaves the Council with trade measures it cannot easily vote on and a government that has said nothing about whether it believes the finding.\n\n## Lowtide and the street\n\nThe unrest that began in the Lowtide district on 1 April, with crowds chanting \"HE did nothing wrong!\", has been the first test. The police report two dead and 112 arrests. On Friday the Council extended curfews in Lowtide and the Harbor Ward by 14 days.\n\nResidents who have spoken to the Daily describe Yakone, a broker who sold cargo and, they say, paid rents when the Port Authority would not, as a figure who did them favors. The state says he is the man who killed 23 people. Both accounts are being asserted loudly in the same streets.\n\nCommissioner Dessa Whitlock has said Salvationist leaflets were found among the debris. The Lowtide Tenants' Council has said the crowds were no one's creation.\n\n## What to watch\n\nMid-April: the defense and the state file briefs with the Supreme Court. May: oral argument, date to be set. 31 May: the Court's 60-day deadline. After the ruling: a decision by the Executive, with no deadline in the Charter. Meanwhile: the curfew review, and the Harbor Ward special election, which the clerk has said will be set within 30 days of the verdict.\n\nThe case has moved faster than any terrorism case the Republic has tried. What comes next has no precedent to follow.\n\n//The Daily draws on court filings, the Charter, and public statements. Mr. Yakone's counsel, the presidency, and the Qianchan consulate were offered a chance to comment.//"},"t":1793537603719},"the_fire_lord_is_resting":{"title":"The Fire Lord is resting","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>The Royal Household Office announces that the Fire Lord, His Majesty Zuko, is resting at a residence of the Household following his visit to the city of Joha on the 11th inst.\u003c/p>\u003cp>His Majesty's physicians advise a period of quiet. He has not cancelled his obligations to Tajisaki and will resume them when the physicians permit. No date has been fixed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Office notes that certain disturbances were reported in Joha in the days before and after the visit. These were the work of a few persons acting in the name of the Salvationist cause, whose conduct is condemned. Order has been restored in the city. Reports of loss of life are exaggerated and should not be repeated.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A curfew in Joha remains in force until further notice. Gatherings of more than ten persons are not permitted.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Crown Princess, Her Highness Izumi, will attend to the ceremonial duties of the throne in the interval. The sitting of the Diet due on the 16th is postponed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The people's affection for His Majesty is well known, and the Office thanks them for their patience.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"comm","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["Royal Household Office announces the Fire Lord is resting following his visit to Joha"],"by":"","cr":"Issued by the Royal Household Office","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":154,"m":9,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Royal Household Office announces the Fire Lord is resting following his visit to Joha","src":"The Royal Household Office announces that the Fire Lord, His Majesty Zuko, is resting at a residence of the Household following his visit to the city of Joha on the 11th inst.\n\nHis Majesty's physicians advise a period of quiet. He has not cancelled his obligations to Tajisaki and will resume them when the physicians permit. No date has been fixed.\n\nThe Office notes that certain disturbances were reported in Joha in the days before and after the visit. These were the work of a few persons acting in the name of the Salvationist cause, whose conduct is condemned. Order has been restored in the city. Reports of loss of life are exaggerated and should not be repeated.\n\nA curfew in Joha remains in force until further notice. Gatherings of more than ten persons are not permitted.\n\nThe Crown Princess, Her Highness Izumi, will attend to the ceremonial duties of the throne in the interval. The sitting of the Diet due on the 16th is postponed.\n\nThe people's affection for His Majesty is well known, and the Office thanks them for their patience."},"t":1793537603720},"nuclear_strike_destroys_earth_union_city_at_least_140_000_feared_dead":{"title":"Nuclear strike destroys Earth Union City; at least 140,000 feared dead","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">A nuclear weapon was detonated over [[earth union city|Aomori]] in the [[si wong desert|Si Wong desert]] on Sunday morning, destroying the center of the city and killing an estimated 140,000 people, according to Earth Union authorities. The [[xinan dominion|Xinan Dominion]], which has claimed responsibility, said the strike was a lawful act against an &quot;illegal occupation&quot; of its oil fields.\u003c/p>\u003cp>It is the first nuclear weapon used in war since the [[soen strike|Sōen strike on Ba Sing Se]] nine decades ago, and the first used against a city in the age of radio and satellites.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What we know\u003c/h3>\u003cp>\u003cb>The blast.\u003c/b> The detonation came at 7:42 a.m. local time, according to the Earth Union Ministry of Health. [[concord of nations|Concord of Nations]] technical staff said preliminary readings put the yield at about 36 kilotons. The Daily has not seen the readings.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The toll.\u003c/b> The Earth Union put the dead at 140,000 and the injured at more than 200,000. The Concord's relief agency counted 112,000 dead and 70,000 missing in a first tally, and said the figures would rise. Neither number has been independently verified.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The claim.\u003c/b> The [[xinan dominion|Xinan Dominion]]'s defense ministry announced the strike on state radio about three hours later, saying the Earth Union had &quot;seized Dominion oil under cover of a false union.&quot; It did not say how the weapon was delivered.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The response.\u003c/b> The [[concord of nations|Concord of Nations]] convened an emergency session at noon. The Earth Union government has asked all member states to close their airspace to Xinan aircraft and to suspend fuel sales to the Dominion.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">A city's center, gone\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Aomori, which had about 600,000 residents, was built around the Si Wong oil trade. Reports from the border, relayed by relief workers, describe a district of about two miles across, flattened, with fires burning on its edges. Survivors on the outskirts told the Concord's staff of a flash and a wave of heat before the sound reached them.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;There is no center left,&quot; said Dr. Halima Zhano, a physician at a field hospital on the city's eastern road, in a call to the Daily. &quot;We are treating burns, blindness, and people who walked out and cannot tell us where they walked from.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Fallout\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Earth Kingdom's weather service said winds were carrying a dust cloud east, toward the salt flats and the trade towns beyond them. Authorities in [[gaoling|Gaoling]] and in three border provinces issued advisories telling residents to stay indoors, cover wells, and keep livestock sheltered. Tens of thousands of residents were reported to be leaving the desert, and the Concord said it expected a refugee movement of 300,000 within days.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The world responds\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Republic Council said it would meet tonight and that the Armed forces had been placed on alert for refugee ships. The Kingdom of Tajisaki's General Staff said it had raised its radar posture on the western sea, and the Fire Lord's office said she would address the nation tomorrow.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Avatar's office in [[republic city|Republic City]] had no comment. Pressed by reporters outside the Council chamber, a spokesman said only that &quot;the Avatar has been informed.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Daily offered the [[xinan dominion|Xinan Dominion]]'s consulate an opportunity to reply to the Earth Union's account. It had not responded by publication.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This report draws on statements from the Earth Union Ministry of Health, the [[xinan dominion|Xinan Dominion]] defense ministry and the [[concord of nations|Concord of Nations]], and on telephone interviews with relief workers. Casualty figures are official estimates and could not be verified.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Xinan Dominion says it acted against an \"illegal occupation\" of Si Wong's oil fields; the Concord of Nations is in emergency session"],"by":"By Nikolai Rhee, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":190,"m":10,"d":19,"time":"11:45 a.m.","ph":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","pc":"A mushroom cloud rises over Aomori, Earth Union, in the Si Wong Desert after a nuclear explosion destroyed much of the city's center on Sunday morning, October 19, 190 AG. The blast was photographed from about 500 km (310 miles) away by passenger Li Wei aboard United Airlines flight JY821 en route to Omashu.","pcr":"ZAOFU TUPIAN/Li Wei","pp":"top","ps":"l","pr":"1","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","cd":"","co":"","ch":[],"stt":"pub","sub":"The Xinan Dominion says it acted against an \"illegal occupation\" of Si Wong's oil fields; the Concord of Nations is in emergency session","src":"{j} A nuclear weapon was detonated over [[earth union city|Aomori]] in the [[si wong desert|Si Wong desert]] on Sunday morning, destroying the center of the city and killing an estimated 140,000 people, according to Earth Union authorities. The [[xinan dominion|Xinan Dominion]], which has claimed responsibility, said the strike was a lawful act against an \"illegal occupation\" of its oil fields.\n\nIt is the first nuclear weapon used in war since the [[soen strike|Sōen strike on Ba Sing Se]] nine decades ago, and the first used against a city in the age of radio and satellites.\n\n## What we know\n\n**The blast.** The detonation came at 7:42 a.m. local time, according to the Earth Union Ministry of Health. [[concord of nations|Concord of Nations]] technical staff said preliminary readings put the yield at about 36 kilotons. The Daily has not seen the readings.\n\n**The toll.** The Earth Union put the dead at 140,000 and the injured at more than 200,000. The Concord's relief agency counted 112,000 dead and 70,000 missing in a first tally, and said the figures would rise. Neither number has been independently verified.\n\n**The claim.** The [[xinan dominion|Xinan Dominion]]'s defense ministry announced the strike on state radio about three hours later, saying the Earth Union had \"seized Dominion oil under cover of a false union.\" It did not say how the weapon was delivered.\n\n**The response.** The [[concord of nations|Concord of Nations]] convened an emergency session at noon. The Earth Union government has asked all member states to close their airspace to Xinan aircraft and to suspend fuel sales to the Dominion.\n\n## A city's center, gone\n\nAomori, which had about 600,000 residents, was built around the Si Wong oil trade. Reports from the border, relayed by relief workers, describe a district of about two miles across, flattened, with fires burning on its edges. Survivors on the outskirts told the Concord's staff of a flash and a wave of heat before the sound reached them.\n\n\"There is no center left,\" said Dr. Halima Zhano, a physician at a field hospital on the city's eastern road, in a call to the Daily. \"We are treating burns, blindness, and people who walked out and cannot tell us where they walked from.\"\n\n## Fallout\n\nThe Earth Kingdom's weather service said winds were carrying a dust cloud east, toward the salt flats and the trade towns beyond them. Authorities in [[gaoling|Gaoling]] and in three border provinces issued advisories telling residents to stay indoors, cover wells, and keep livestock sheltered. Tens of thousands of residents were reported to be leaving the desert, and the Concord said it expected a refugee movement of 300,000 within days.\n\n## The world responds\n\nThe Republic Council said it would meet tonight and that the Armed forces had been placed on alert for refugee ships. The Kingdom of Tajisaki's General Staff said it had raised its radar posture on the western sea, and the Fire Lord's office said she would address the nation tomorrow.\n\nThe Avatar's office in [[republic city|Republic City]] had no comment. Pressed by reporters outside the Council chamber, a spokesman said only that \"the Avatar has been informed.\"\n\nThe Daily offered the [[xinan dominion|Xinan Dominion]]'s consulate an opportunity to reply to the Earth Union's account. It had not responded by publication.\n\n//This report draws on statements from the Earth Union Ministry of Health, the [[xinan dominion|Xinan Dominion]] defense ministry and the [[concord of nations|Concord of Nations]], and on telephone interviews with relief workers. Casualty figures are official estimates and could not be verified.//"},"t":1795596347904},"armed_fliers_and_a_floating_temple_strike_republic_city_fires_burn_in_six_districts":{"title":"Armed fliers and a floating temple strike Republic City; fires burn in six districts","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Thousands of armed attackers, many of them flying on gusts of air that no one in the city had seen them use before, struck Republic City this afternoon, and fires were burning in at least six districts at press time.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A temple of stone, which witnesses said came out of a tear in the sky above the harbor, hung over the city for most of the evening. The Avatar was seen fighting above the Unity Tower plaza. The Daily could not reach him.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What we know\u003c/h3>\u003cp>\u003cb>The start.\u003c/b> The attack began at about 3 p.m., during the ceremony for the opening of Unity Tower and the anniversary of the Declaration of Union. Councilor Katara was speaking when the sky opened. Police said the Council and its guests were taken to the shelter under the Government quarter.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The fires.\u003c/b> The Harbor Authority and the fire brigades reported fires in the docks, the Jianyu mills, Longshore, Wada, the rail yards and the Government quarter. The brigades said they had been unable to reach most of them.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The attackers.\u003c/b> Witnesses described people in dark coats who threw wind and called themselves the Denied. A police officer at headquarters told the Daily the working assumption was a Salvationist or Court remnant from the Fire Nation civil war. The Daily could not confirm this, and the Fire Lord's office had not responded.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The dead.\u003c/b> Republic City General Hospital said it had received 1,100 casualties by 8 p.m. and had stopped counting.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The temple\u003c/h3>\u003cp>&quot;It was a whole building, with courtyards, coming down out of nothing,&quot; said Pema Lau, a stallholder in Wada. &quot;Then it just hung there. People stopped running. Then they started again.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Fire Lord Zuko, who was in the city for the anniversary, and Toph Beifong, whose students defend the Government quarter, were reported to be with the Avatar. The Daily has not been able to speak to them.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This is a developing story and will be updated. The Daily offered the Salvationist cause a chance to reply; none was available.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Council has gone underground; the police say the attackers may be Salvationist remnants. Witnesses call them the Denied."],"by":"By Rurik Anand and Dalia Okonkwo","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":9,"d":27,"time":"9:15 p.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"The deck of this report said that police believed the attackers \"may be Salvationist remnants.\" The police withdrew that statement on 28 September and said there is no evidence of a link. The Daily should have attributed the claim to a single officer and not put it in the deck.","lv":"0","sub":"The Council has gone underground; the police say the attackers may be Salvationist remnants. Witnesses call them the Denied.","src":"{j} Thousands of armed attackers, many of them flying on gusts of air that no one in the city had seen them use before, struck Republic City this afternoon, and fires were burning in at least six districts at press time.\n\nA temple of stone, which witnesses said came out of a tear in the sky above the harbor, hung over the city for most of the evening. The Avatar was seen fighting above the Unity Tower plaza. The Daily could not reach him.\n\n## What we know\n\n**The start.** The attack began at about 3 p.m., during the ceremony for the opening of Unity Tower and the anniversary of the Declaration of Union. Councilor Katara was speaking when the sky opened. Police said the Council and its guests were taken to the shelter under the Government quarter.\n\n**The fires.** The Harbor Authority and the fire brigades reported fires in the docks, the Jianyu mills, Longshore, Wada, the rail yards and the Government quarter. The brigades said they had been unable to reach most of them.\n\n**The attackers.** Witnesses described people in dark coats who threw wind and called themselves the Denied. A police officer at headquarters told the Daily the working assumption was a Salvationist or Court remnant from the Fire Nation civil war. The Daily could not confirm this, and the Fire Lord's office had not responded.\n\n**The dead.** Republic City General Hospital said it had received 1,100 casualties by 8 p.m. and had stopped counting.\n\n## The temple\n\n\"It was a whole building, with courtyards, coming down out of nothing,\" said Pema Lau, a stallholder in Wada. \"Then it just hung there. People stopped running. Then they started again.\"\n\nFire Lord Zuko, who was in the city for the anniversary, and Toph Beifong, whose students defend the Government quarter, were reported to be with the Avatar. The Daily has not been able to speak to them.\n\n//This is a developing story and will be updated. The Daily offered the Salvationist cause a chance to reply; none was available.//","co":"The Council has gone underground; the police say the attackers may be Salvationist remnants. Witnesses call them the Denied.","cd":"28 September 118 AG"},"t":1793547879861},"half_of_republic_city_destroyed_in_worst_attack_in_its_history_avatar_says_threat_is_over":{"title":"Half of Republic City destroyed in worst attack in its history; Avatar says threat is over","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Republic City woke this morning to rain, and to ruins. More than half of the built-up area of the capital of the United Republic was burned or leveled yesterday, in the worst attack of any kind in its sixteen-year history.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The police had counted 9,400 dead by dawn. The Council said the total would be &quot;several times&quot; that. Avatar Aang, speaking on the steps of the Council hall at 5 a.m., said the thing that had given the attackers their power had been destroyed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;They can't fly anymore,&quot; he said. &quot;The staff is broken. It's over.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The fight\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The attackers, who called themselves the Denied, were non-benders who had been given airbending, the Avatar said, by an ancient weapon that he named as Sonam's Staff. Their leader, an airbender he called Tagah, was killed in the plaza in front of Unity Tower about 7 p.m. The temple that had hung over the harbor went back into the sky and was gone.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Council said about 800 attackers had died in the fighting and more than 3,000 had been taken. Ministers did not say how many were still at large.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The damage\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Harbor Authority said the docks were gone from Pier One to Pier Eleven. The Jianyu mills, Longshore and the lower Asagawa were burned out. The Government quarter, which Toph Beifong's metalbenders held, was mostly standing. Unity Tower, which opened yesterday, stood in a crater of burned paving, with its glass out.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Republic City General treated more than 11,000 people overnight in its corridors, its car park and a hall lent by the Pro-Bending Arena. Doctors asked citizens to stay away from the harbor.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The first theory\u003c/h3>\u003cp>On Sunday night the police had said the attackers might be Salvationist remnants. Chief of Police Dae-jung Mo told the Daily this morning that the statement &quot;was premature and is withdrawn,&quot; and that there was no evidence of any link.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This report draws on the Council, the police, Republic City General Hospital and the Harbor Authority, and on interviews at the Council hall. All numbers are preliminary.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Avatar says the staff is destroyed and the attackers can no longer fly. The count of the dead stands at 9,400 and is expected to rise several times over."],"by":"By Rurik Anand, with Dalia Okonkwo and Kin Rhee","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":9,"d":28,"time":"7:30 a.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Avatar says the staff is destroyed and the attackers can no longer fly. The count of the dead stands at 9,400 and is expected to rise several times over.","src":"{j} Republic City woke this morning to rain, and to ruins. More than half of the built-up area of the capital of the United Republic was burned or leveled yesterday, in the worst attack of any kind in its sixteen-year history.\n\nThe police had counted 9,400 dead by dawn. The Council said the total would be \"several times\" that. Avatar Aang, speaking on the steps of the Council hall at 5 a.m., said the thing that had given the attackers their power had been destroyed.\n\n\"They can't fly anymore,\" he said. \"The staff is broken. It's over.\"\n\n## The fight\n\nThe attackers, who called themselves the Denied, were non-benders who had been given airbending, the Avatar said, by an ancient weapon that he named as Sonam's Staff. Their leader, an airbender he called Tagah, was killed in the plaza in front of Unity Tower about 7 p.m. The temple that had hung over the harbor went back into the sky and was gone.\n\nThe Council said about 800 attackers had died in the fighting and more than 3,000 had been taken. Ministers did not say how many were still at large.\n\n## The damage\n\nThe Harbor Authority said the docks were gone from Pier One to Pier Eleven. The Jianyu mills, Longshore and the lower Asagawa were burned out. The Government quarter, which Toph Beifong's metalbenders held, was mostly standing. Unity Tower, which opened yesterday, stood in a crater of burned paving, with its glass out.\n\nRepublic City General treated more than 11,000 people overnight in its corridors, its car park and a hall lent by the Pro-Bending Arena. Doctors asked citizens to stay away from the harbor.\n\n## The first theory\n\nOn Sunday night the police had said the attackers might be Salvationist remnants. Chief of Police Dae-jung Mo told the Daily this morning that the statement \"was premature and is withdrawn,\" and that there was no evidence of any link.\n\n//This report draws on the Council, the police, Republic City General Hospital and the Harbor Authority, and on interviews at the Council hall. All numbers are preliminary.//"},"t":1793547879862},"who_are_the_denied":{"title":"Who are the Denied?","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The attackers who burned Republic City on Sunday called themselves the Denied. A week ago the name was known to a few police files and a few harbor insurance clerks. This is what is known.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Who they are\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Denied are non-benders. Most of the 3,912 taken alive were former soldiers, dockhands or mill workers, many from the Earth Kingdom and the Fire Nation, according to police records. The Council put the group's numbers at about 5,000 on Sunday.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The name, they told one captured reporter, comes from a belief that &quot;the world gave everything to the people who could bend it, and left us the work.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What they did\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The police have records of robberies of ancient sites and naval vessels, attributed to the Denied, since last year. In August, a Fire Nation naval vessel was boarded in the western sea. In early September they attacked the Avatar at the Southern Air Temple. They were looking, the Avatar said, for a map.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The staff\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Avatar said that the Denied wanted Sonam's Staff, an object that turned non-benders into airbenders. They had followed his party to the hidden island where it was kept. On Sunday afternoon, Tagah, a man whom the Avatar had freed from a mountain days before, gave them airbending with it.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What is not known\u003c/h3>\u003cp>\u003cb>Who led them before Tagah.\u003c/b> The police named a man called Jie. They do not know who he reports to, or whether he does.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Who paid.\u003c/b> The police said the airships were built by someone with a shipyard. They have not found it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Whether there are more.\u003c/b> The Council said &quot;no one can say.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Whether they were Salvationists.\u003c/b> The police said no on Monday, and the Daily has seen nothing to contradict this.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This explainer draws on police records, Council briefings and interviews with the captured. The Daily has withheld the names of those who were not charged.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"explainer","sec":"Explainers","kick":"","deck":["The group that burned Republic City says the world was built for benders. The Daily explains what is known about it, and what is not."],"by":"By Dalia Okonkwo, Politics Desk","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":9,"d":29,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The group that burned Republic City says the world was built for benders. The Daily explains what is known about it, and what is not.","src":"{j} The attackers who burned Republic City on Sunday called themselves the Denied. A week ago the name was known to a few police files and a few harbor insurance clerks. This is what is known.\n\n## Who they are\n\nThe Denied are non-benders. Most of the 3,912 taken alive were former soldiers, dockhands or mill workers, many from the Earth Kingdom and the Fire Nation, according to police records. The Council put the group's numbers at about 5,000 on Sunday.\n\nThe name, they told one captured reporter, comes from a belief that \"the world gave everything to the people who could bend it, and left us the work.\"\n\n## What they did\n\nThe police have records of robberies of ancient sites and naval vessels, attributed to the Denied, since last year. In August, a Fire Nation naval vessel was boarded in the western sea. In early September they attacked the Avatar at the Southern Air Temple. They were looking, the Avatar said, for a map.\n\n## The staff\n\nThe Avatar said that the Denied wanted Sonam's Staff, an object that turned non-benders into airbenders. They had followed his party to the hidden island where it was kept. On Sunday afternoon, Tagah, a man whom the Avatar had freed from a mountain days before, gave them airbending with it.\n\n## What is not known\n\n**Who led them before Tagah.** The police named a man called Jie. They do not know who he reports to, or whether he does.\n\n**Who paid.** The police said the airships were built by someone with a shipyard. They have not found it.\n\n**Whether there are more.** The Council said \"no one can say.\"\n\n**Whether they were Salvationists.** The police said no on Monday, and the Daily has seen nothing to contradict this.\n\n//This explainer draws on police records, Council briefings and interviews with the captured. The Daily has withheld the names of those who were not charged.//"},"t":1793547879863},"earth_kingdom_says_it_has_no_record_of_airbenders_ancient_war":{"title":"Earth Kingdom says it has no record of airbenders' ancient war","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Earth Kingdom said today that it had no record of a war with the Air Nomads, after the airbender who burned Republic City told its Avatar that &quot;Earth killed my people.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>A statement from the Earth King's office said the Kingdom &quot;has kept its histories for four thousand years, and finds no war with the Air Nomads in them.&quot; It said the Kingdom &quot;grieves with the people of Republic City,&quot; and offered grain and engineers.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The claim\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The airbender, Tagah, told the Avatar on a hidden island that the Earth Kingdom had killed the Air Nomads of his own time, about five thousand years ago, when the earthbender clans united. The Avatar repeated the claim to the Council on Monday, saying he did not know if it was true.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The scholars\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Imperial University of Ba Sing Se said the Kingdom's oldest records are from about 3,200 years ago. Before that, &quot;the clans kept their own accounts, most of which are lost,&quot; said Prof. Liang Wenzhu, of the history faculty. &quot;A war is possible. It is not provable. We do not know of any Air Nomad city in those years, either.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>A second historian, who asked not to be named because the court had not approved comment, said the lack of a record &quot;is not the same as a record of peace.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The staff\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Avatar said the staff he destroyed was made in that war. The Kingdom's Museum of Antiquities told the Times that it holds no object matching his description.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This report draws on a statement of the Earth King's office and interviews at the Imperial University. The Times asked the Avatar's office for comment on the scholars' views. It did not respond by publication.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"bst","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Earth King's office says the Earth Kingdom has no record of the war the dead airbender spoke of. Scholars in Ba Sing Se are less certain."],"by":"By Mei Ran","cr":"","cty":"BA SING SE","y":118,"m":9,"d":30,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Earth King's office says the Earth Kingdom has no record of the war the dead airbender spoke of. Scholars in Ba Sing Se are less certain.","src":"{j} The Earth Kingdom said today that it had no record of a war with the Air Nomads, after the airbender who burned Republic City told its Avatar that \"Earth killed my people.\"\n\nA statement from the Earth King's office said the Kingdom \"has kept its histories for four thousand years, and finds no war with the Air Nomads in them.\" It said the Kingdom \"grieves with the people of Republic City,\" and offered grain and engineers.\n\n## The claim\n\nThe airbender, Tagah, told the Avatar on a hidden island that the Earth Kingdom had killed the Air Nomads of his own time, about five thousand years ago, when the earthbender clans united. The Avatar repeated the claim to the Council on Monday, saying he did not know if it was true.\n\n## The scholars\n\nThe Imperial University of Ba Sing Se said the Kingdom's oldest records are from about 3,200 years ago. Before that, \"the clans kept their own accounts, most of which are lost,\" said Prof. Liang Wenzhu, of the history faculty. \"A war is possible. It is not provable. We do not know of any Air Nomad city in those years, either.\"\n\nA second historian, who asked not to be named because the court had not approved comment, said the lack of a record \"is not the same as a record of peace.\"\n\n## The staff\n\nThe Avatar said the staff he destroyed was made in that war. The Kingdom's Museum of Antiquities told the Times that it holds no object matching his description.\n\n//This report draws on a statement of the Earth King's office and interviews at the Imperial University. The Times asked the Avatar's office for comment on the scholars' views. It did not respond by publication.//"},"t":1793547879864},"fire_lord_back_from_republic_city_diet_asks_what_the_navy_knew_of_the_denied":{"title":"Fire Lord back from Republic City; Diet asks what the navy knew of the Denied","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Fire Lord Zuko returned to Caldera City last night from Republic City, where he fought beside the Avatar, and told the Diet this morning that the Fire Nation would send ships, grain and builders to the republic's harbor &quot;for as long as it takes.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>He did not say how many of the Fire Nation's ships would be sent. The Diet, six weeks after the armistice, has few to spare.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The airship\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Fire Lord confirmed that the airship in which he had flown to the hidden island with the Avatar was lost there. He said the crew had been brought off safely. He did not say what it was doing in the Endless Ocean, and Diet members did not ask.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The August vessel\u003c/h3>\u003cp>A member of the Diet, Ishikawa Reiko of Shu Jing, asked the Grand Chamberlain why a naval vessel boarded by a non-bender group in August, which the Diet had been told was a &quot;customs matter,&quot; was in fact the Denied. The Grand Chamberlain said the Ministry had &quot;treated it as a matter for the navy.&quot; She said she wanted a report by the end of the week.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The old question\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Members also asked if the Denied were linked to the Salvationist remnants, who had been fighting the Royalists since the war. The Fire Lord said he had &quot;seen nothing to suggest it,&quot; and that the Fire Nation &quot;will not be a people who blame what is convenient.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Scire asked the Ministry of the Navy for the August report. It was not available.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["The Fire Lord returned from the burning city last night. The Diet asked why a naval vessel was boarded in August and why nobody said so."],"by":"By Mika Tsuneda","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":118,"m":10,"d":1,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Fire Lord returned from the burning city last night. The Diet asked why a naval vessel was boarded in August and why nobody said so.","src":"{j} Fire Lord Zuko returned to Caldera City last night from Republic City, where he fought beside the Avatar, and told the Diet this morning that the Fire Nation would send ships, grain and builders to the republic's harbor \"for as long as it takes.\"\n\nHe did not say how many of the Fire Nation's ships would be sent. The Diet, six weeks after the armistice, has few to spare.\n\n## The airship\n\nThe Fire Lord confirmed that the airship in which he had flown to the hidden island with the Avatar was lost there. He said the crew had been brought off safely. He did not say what it was doing in the Endless Ocean, and Diet members did not ask.\n\n## The August vessel\n\nA member of the Diet, Ishikawa Reiko of Shu Jing, asked the Grand Chamberlain why a naval vessel boarded by a non-bender group in August, which the Diet had been told was a \"customs matter,\" was in fact the Denied. The Grand Chamberlain said the Ministry had \"treated it as a matter for the navy.\" She said she wanted a report by the end of the week.\n\n## The old question\n\nMembers also asked if the Denied were linked to the Salvationist remnants, who had been fighting the Royalists since the war. The Fire Lord said he had \"seen nothing to suggest it,\" and that the Fire Nation \"will not be a people who blame what is convenient.\"\n\n//The Scire asked the Ministry of the Navy for the August report. It was not available.//"},"t":1793547879865},"what_was_sonam_s_staff_and_why_did_the_avatar_break_it":{"title":"What was Sonam's staff, and why did the Avatar break it?","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The staff that the Avatar destroyed on Sunday had been in the world, in one form or another, for about five thousand years. No one now alive saw it made. This is what is known, and not.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What it was\u003c/h3>\u003cp>A wooden staff, said to be about the height of a tall man, which the Avatar said &quot;channeled the energy of the Spirit World&quot; and could turn a non-bender into an airbender. It belonged to an Avatar named Sonam, whose name is in no list. The Avatar said it was her spirit that told him she had hidden it.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Where it came from\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Tagah said Sonam learned to make it from the lion turtles. Sonam told the Avatar nothing of that. The Earth Kingdom said it had never seen it. The Avatar said he did not know.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The staff is described as an object of a war between the Air Nomads and the Earth Kingdom, a war the Earth Kingdom says it has no record of. On this page, no historian could say if the war happened.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Why it was destroyed\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Avatar told the Council that &quot;something that can make an army of anyone should not exist,&quot; and that the Denied, who had it for hours, had burned a city. He said it was the Air Nomads' own proverb that decided him. He did not elaborate.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What was lost\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The staff and the temple in which it was kept are gone. No fragment was recovered. The Avatar's companions said that the staff, when broken, turned to light.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Open questions\u003c/h3>\u003cp>\u003cb>Whether airbending can be restored to the world.\u003c/b> The Avatar said it could not, and that he did not want it to be. Air Acolytes, the non-bender students he has taken, will not become airbenders.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Whether there were others.\u003c/b> Tagah's account says Sonam made only one.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Where the temple went.\u003c/b> The Avatar said it returned to the Spirit World.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This explainer draws on the Avatar's statements to the Council and on interviews with the survivors of the island. Where they differ, both accounts are given.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"explainer","sec":"Explainers","kick":"","deck":["No one alive knows who made it, or how. The Daily sets out what the Avatar and the survivors have said."],"by":"By Dalia Okonkwo, Politics Desk","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":10,"d":3,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"No one alive knows who made it, or how. The Daily sets out what the Avatar and the survivors have said.","src":"{j} The staff that the Avatar destroyed on Sunday had been in the world, in one form or another, for about five thousand years. No one now alive saw it made. This is what is known, and not.\n\n## What it was\n\nA wooden staff, said to be about the height of a tall man, which the Avatar said \"channeled the energy of the Spirit World\" and could turn a non-bender into an airbender. It belonged to an Avatar named Sonam, whose name is in no list. The Avatar said it was her spirit that told him she had hidden it.\n\n## Where it came from\n\nTagah said Sonam learned to make it from the lion turtles. Sonam told the Avatar nothing of that. The Earth Kingdom said it had never seen it. The Avatar said he did not know.\n\nThe staff is described as an object of a war between the Air Nomads and the Earth Kingdom, a war the Earth Kingdom says it has no record of. On this page, no historian could say if the war happened.\n\n## Why it was destroyed\n\nThe Avatar told the Council that \"something that can make an army of anyone should not exist,\" and that the Denied, who had it for hours, had burned a city. He said it was the Air Nomads' own proverb that decided him. He did not elaborate.\n\n## What was lost\n\nThe staff and the temple in which it was kept are gone. No fragment was recovered. The Avatar's companions said that the staff, when broken, turned to light.\n\n## Open questions\n\n**Whether airbending can be restored to the world.** The Avatar said it could not, and that he did not want it to be. Air Acolytes, the non-bender students he has taken, will not become airbenders.\n\n**Whether there were others.** Tagah's account says Sonam made only one.\n\n**Where the temple went.** The Avatar said it returned to the Spirit World.\n\n//This explainer draws on the Avatar's statements to the Council and on interviews with the survivors of the island. Where they differ, both accounts are given.//"},"t":1793547879866},"council_counts_41_200_dead_in_republic_city_three_fifths_of_the_city_lost":{"title":"Council counts 41,200 dead in Republic City; three-fifths of the city lost","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Republic Council said tonight that 41,200 people were confirmed dead in the attack on Republic City, that 6,100 were missing, and that about 380,000 people had lost their homes. About three-fifths of the city's built-up area was destroyed or condemned.\u003c/p>\u003cp>It is the largest loss of life in a single day in the republic's history, and the first time the capital has been largely destroyed.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The count\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Council said the figures came from the hospital, the Harbor Authority, the police and the morgue at the old customs house. The Daily's own tally from those sources was 38,900 dead. The Council said its figure included bodies found since Wednesday.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The homeless\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Most of the homeless are in the mill districts and the docks, where the fires were worst. About 90,000 are in tents in Republic City Park, and the rest with relatives, on boats or in the hills. The Council opened the Pro-Bending Arena and the rail depots as shelters.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The money\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Treasury said a third of the emergency fund was spent in the first week. The Council asked the Assembly to approve a levy. It said it expected help from the Fire Nation, the Earth Kingdom and Qianchan, all of which have offered grain.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The tower\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Unity Tower, where Councilor Katara was speaking when the attack began, will be rebuilt, the Council said. It will not open again until the dead are buried.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This report draws on the Council, the Harbor Authority, Republic City General Hospital and the city police. Figures are official and differ from the Daily's own count.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Council says 41,200 are dead, 6,100 missing and 380,000 without homes. A third of the city's emergency funds are gone."],"by":"By Henitta Zhe and Rurik Anand","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":10,"d":4,"time":"6:00 p.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Council says 41,200 are dead, 6,100 missing and 380,000 without homes. A third of the city's emergency funds are gone.","src":"{j} The Republic Council said tonight that 41,200 people were confirmed dead in the attack on Republic City, that 6,100 were missing, and that about 380,000 people had lost their homes. About three-fifths of the city's built-up area was destroyed or condemned.\n\nIt is the largest loss of life in a single day in the republic's history, and the first time the capital has been largely destroyed.\n\n## The count\n\nThe Council said the figures came from the hospital, the Harbor Authority, the police and the morgue at the old customs house. The Daily's own tally from those sources was 38,900 dead. The Council said its figure included bodies found since Wednesday.\n\n## The homeless\n\nMost of the homeless are in the mill districts and the docks, where the fires were worst. About 90,000 are in tents in Republic City Park, and the rest with relatives, on boats or in the hills. The Council opened the Pro-Bending Arena and the rail depots as shelters.\n\n## The money\n\nThe Treasury said a third of the emergency fund was spent in the first week. The Council asked the Assembly to approve a levy. It said it expected help from the Fire Nation, the Earth Kingdom and Qianchan, all of which have offered grain.\n\n## The tower\n\nUnity Tower, where Councilor Katara was speaking when the attack began, will be rebuilt, the Council said. It will not open again until the dead are buried.\n\n//This report draws on the Council, the Harbor Authority, Republic City General Hospital and the city police. Figures are official and differ from the Daily's own count.//"},"t":1793547879867},"council_votes_amnesty_for_most_denied_211_to_face_trial":{"title":"Council votes amnesty for most Denied; 211 to face trial","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Republic Council voted 5 to 1 today to give an amnesty to 3,701 of the Denied who surrendered or were taken after the attack on Republic City, and to try 211 on charges of murder or arson.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The vote came after nine days of debate. The Council said the attackers had lost their bending when the staff was destroyed and were &quot;no longer a danger to the republic.&quot; It said many had been told they were fighting for a republic of non-benders, and that the leaders were dead.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The 211\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The 211 to be tried are those whom witnesses saw start fires or kill. They will be tried in the Circuit Court. The Attorney General said he expected the first trials in the spring.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The families\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Outside the Council hall, about 2,000 relatives of the dead stood in the rain. &quot;Forty thousand people died and the ones who did it go home,&quot; said Hao Yi, whose brother worked at the docks. &quot;I thought we were a republic. I thought there were laws.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The dissent\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The sole vote against was from the Earth councilor, who said the amnesty &quot;is for those who surrender, not those who were beaten.&quot; The Council's other members said the choice was between an amnesty and several thousand trials in a city that has no courthouse left.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The question of why\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Council said it had found no foreign hand and no link to the Salvationists. The Avatar, who was at the vote, said only that &quot;the people who followed Tagah were tricked, and so was he.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This report draws on the Council vote and statements, the Attorney General's office and interviews outside the hall.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["Of the 3,912 attackers taken alive, 211 will face trial for murder or arson. Families of the dead say it is not enough."],"by":"By Rurik Anand, City Hall Desk","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":10,"d":12,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Of the 3,912 attackers taken alive, 211 will face trial for murder or arson. Families of the dead say it is not enough.","src":"{j} The Republic Council voted 5 to 1 today to give an amnesty to 3,701 of the Denied who surrendered or were taken after the attack on Republic City, and to try 211 on charges of murder or arson.\n\nThe vote came after nine days of debate. The Council said the attackers had lost their bending when the staff was destroyed and were \"no longer a danger to the republic.\" It said many had been told they were fighting for a republic of non-benders, and that the leaders were dead.\n\n## The 211\n\nThe 211 to be tried are those whom witnesses saw start fires or kill. They will be tried in the Circuit Court. The Attorney General said he expected the first trials in the spring.\n\n## The families\n\nOutside the Council hall, about 2,000 relatives of the dead stood in the rain. \"Forty thousand people died and the ones who did it go home,\" said Hao Yi, whose brother worked at the docks. \"I thought we were a republic. I thought there were laws.\"\n\n## The dissent\n\nThe sole vote against was from the Earth councilor, who said the amnesty \"is for those who surrender, not those who were beaten.\" The Council's other members said the choice was between an amnesty and several thousand trials in a city that has no courthouse left.\n\n## The question of why\n\nThe Council said it had found no foreign hand and no link to the Salvationists. The Avatar, who was at the vote, said only that \"the people who followed Tagah were tricked, and so was he.\"\n\n//This report draws on the Council vote and statements, the Attorney General's office and interviews outside the hall.//"},"t":1793547879868},"fire_nation_war_is_over_royalists_accept_final_salvationist_surrender_at_suiko_armistice_takes_effect":{"title":"Fire Nation war is over: Royalists accept final Salvationist surrender at Suiko; armistice takes effect","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">CALDERA CITY, Aug. 12 — Fire Lord Zuko addressed the Fire Nation by radio broadcast yesterday evening, confirming that Royalist forces had accepted the formal surrender of the last Salvationist holdouts in the Suiko industrial district earlier that afternoon. The civil war that began the day the Hundred Year War ended — and that has in some respects never been separate from it — is over. 'We have come through the fire,' the Fire Lord said in a broadcast that lasted eleven minutes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>'Not the fire of glory — the other kind. The kind that does not make you stronger. The kind that only leaves you with a choice about what to do with what remains.'\u003c/p>\u003cp>The broadcast was heard by an estimated 40 million people across the Fire Nation home islands and diaspora communities. This correspondent listened in the press room of the Caldera City Post, where the senior editor — a woman who has covered Fire Nation politics for thirty years — sat quietly through the broadcast and then left the room without comment. The armistice terms were confirmed simultaneously by the Fire Nation Ministry of Public Affairs and the Qianchan Foreign Ministry, which has been the armistice’s primary international guarantor.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The terms are classified in full but officials from both governments confirmed the broad provisions: the Salvationist movement is formally dissolved as a political organization; its remaining military formations are to disarm within sixty days; its leadership — those who survived — will face a civil accountability process rather than military tribunals.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"x-pq\">“The fire that does not make you stronger. The kind that only leaves you with a choice.” The decision to use civilian accountability rather than military tribunals for the Salvationists is already drawing criticism from the Loyalist families' associations — those who lost relatives to Salvationist violence during the conflict’s middle years.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A statement from the Association of Families of the 78th Brigade, whose regiment was destroyed by Salvationist forces at the Battle of Jinsha Pass in 107 AG, called the decision 'an insult to the dead.' The Fire Lord’s office has not responded to the statement. Officials close to the armistice process told this correspondent that the civilian accountability decision was the most contested provision in the negotiations, and that the alternative — military tribunals that would have required the Royalist government to try soldiers who had been fighting for what they believed was a legitimate revolutionary cause — was assessed as likely to produce martyrs rather than accountability.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Zuko tells 40 million listeners \"we have come through the fire.\" Civilian accountability, not military tribunals, for the Salvationist leadership."],"by":"By Heng Jinrui, Foreign Correspondent","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":118,"m":8,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Zuko tells 40 million listeners \"we have come through the fire.\" Civilian accountability, not military tribunals, for the Salvationist leadership.","src":"{j} CALDERA CITY, Aug. 12 — Fire Lord Zuko addressed the Fire Nation by radio broadcast yesterday evening, confirming that Royalist forces had accepted the formal surrender of the last Salvationist holdouts in the Suiko industrial district earlier that afternoon. The civil war that began the day the Hundred Year War ended — and that has in some respects never been separate from it — is over. 'We have come through the fire,' the Fire Lord said in a broadcast that lasted eleven minutes.\n\n'Not the fire of glory — the other kind. The kind that does not make you stronger. The kind that only leaves you with a choice about what to do with what remains.'\n\nThe broadcast was heard by an estimated 40 million people across the Fire Nation home islands and diaspora communities. This correspondent listened in the press room of the Caldera City Post, where the senior editor — a woman who has covered Fire Nation politics for thirty years — sat quietly through the broadcast and then left the room without comment. The armistice terms were confirmed simultaneously by the Fire Nation Ministry of Public Affairs and the Qianchan Foreign Ministry, which has been the armistice’s primary international guarantor.\n\nThe terms are classified in full but officials from both governments confirmed the broad provisions: the Salvationist movement is formally dissolved as a political organization; its remaining military formations are to disarm within sixty days; its leadership — those who survived — will face a civil accountability process rather than military tribunals. \n\n{pq} “The fire that does not make you stronger. The kind that only leaves you with a choice.” The decision to use civilian accountability rather than military tribunals for the Salvationists is already drawing criticism from the Loyalist families' associations — those who lost relatives to Salvationist violence during the conflict’s middle years.\n\nA statement from the Association of Families of the 78th Brigade, whose regiment was destroyed by Salvationist forces at the Battle of Jinsha Pass in 107 AG, called the decision 'an insult to the dead.' The Fire Lord’s office has not responded to the statement. Officials close to the armistice process told this correspondent that the civilian accountability decision was the most contested provision in the negotiations, and that the alternative — military tribunals that would have required the Royalist government to try soldiers who had been fighting for what they believed was a legitimate revolutionary cause — was assessed as likely to produce martyrs rather than accountability."},"t":1793607117816},"suiko_the_last_battle_three_days_in_an_industrial_district":{"title":"Suiko, the last battle: three days in an industrial district","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">SUIKO DISTRICT, Aug. 13 — The Salvationist movement’s last significant military force was the 'People’s Iron Column' — approximately 2,200 fighters who had retreated into the Suiko industrial district after the collapse of the Salvationist command structure at Jinhou in June. They held Suiko for forty-seven days. The district’s steel foundries and manufacturing plants provided natural fortification and the industrial workers who had been their primary constituency since the movement’s founding provided some continued local support.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Royalist forces' commander at Suiko, Brigadier Sato Junichi, chose containment over direct assault. 'We had enough bodies,' he told this correspondent. 'What we did not have enough of was reasons to add more.'\u003c/p>\u003cp>The containment strategy held the Iron Column in place while supply lines were cut and the political situation outside the district made clear that no relief was coming. The Iron Column’s commander, a woman known in Salvationist circles as 'Red Crane' — her formal name is withheld pending the accountability process — emerged under a white flag on the morning of August 11. She carried a handwritten note addressed to Brigadier Sato.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The note read, in its entirety: 'We have run out of the things that make continuing possible. We request terms.' Sato replied with a written response granting her the civilian accountability process rather than summary judgment.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Iron Column laid down its arms that afternoon. 'Red Crane' was photographed walking out of the Suiko gate. She is approximately forty-five years old.\u003c/p>\u003cp>She had been fighting since she was twenty-seven. The photograph shows a woman who looks considerably older than forty-five. It will be published in tomorrow’s edition.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"War & Peace","kick":"","deck":["Correspondents reconstructed the final engagement of the civil war from officer testimony and survivor accounts."],"by":"By Dao Ruiming, Military Correspondent","cr":"","cty":"SUIKO DISTRICT","y":118,"m":8,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Correspondents reconstructed the final engagement of the civil war from officer testimony and survivor accounts.","src":"{j} SUIKO DISTRICT, Aug. 13 — The Salvationist movement’s last significant military force was the 'People’s Iron Column' — approximately 2,200 fighters who had retreated into the Suiko industrial district after the collapse of the Salvationist command structure at Jinhou in June. They held Suiko for forty-seven days. The district’s steel foundries and manufacturing plants provided natural fortification and the industrial workers who had been their primary constituency since the movement’s founding provided some continued local support.\n\nThe Royalist forces' commander at Suiko, Brigadier Sato Junichi, chose containment over direct assault. 'We had enough bodies,' he told this correspondent. 'What we did not have enough of was reasons to add more.'\n\nThe containment strategy held the Iron Column in place while supply lines were cut and the political situation outside the district made clear that no relief was coming. The Iron Column’s commander, a woman known in Salvationist circles as 'Red Crane' — her formal name is withheld pending the accountability process — emerged under a white flag on the morning of August 11. She carried a handwritten note addressed to Brigadier Sato.\n\nThe note read, in its entirety: 'We have run out of the things that make continuing possible. We request terms.' Sato replied with a written response granting her the civilian accountability process rather than summary judgment.\n\nThe Iron Column laid down its arms that afternoon. 'Red Crane' was photographed walking out of the Suiko gate. She is approximately forty-five years old.\n\nShe had been fighting since she was twenty-seven. The photograph shows a woman who looks considerably older than forty-five. It will be published in tomorrow’s edition."},"t":1793607117817},"what_the_intervention_at_soka_bay_decided":{"title":"What the intervention at Soka Bay decided","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>The Battle of Soka Bay in 109 AG is where the civil war was decided, nine years before it ended. This is not a controversial assessment among military historians — the Loyalist main force was broken at Soka Bay and never reconstituted. The Salvationist faction survived another nine years as a fighting force but was always fighting a war it had already lost, which is a specific kind of war that produces a specific kind of soldier.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Qianchan’s decision to intervene directly — two expeditionary divisions committed to a foreign civil conflict — was the most significant military-political decision this republic has made since the Caldera Accords. The decision is justified, in retrospect, by the outcome. The Royalist government is real.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Fire Nation’s reforms are imperfect and ongoing. The cost was also real. 847 Qianchan personnel died at Soka Bay. Their names are in the records of the 2nd Expeditionary Division.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This newspaper will print them next week, in full, as a matter of record. Decisions that produce outcomes have costs. Both should be in the record.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"feature","sec":"War & Peace","kick":"","deck":["Qianchan committed two divisions to a foreign civil war. The Daily weighs the cost."],"by":"By Ping Wenhua, Analysis","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":8,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Qianchan committed two divisions to a foreign civil war. The Daily weighs the cost.","src":"The Battle of Soka Bay in 109 AG is where the civil war was decided, nine years before it ended. This is not a controversial assessment among military historians — the Loyalist main force was broken at Soka Bay and never reconstituted. The Salvationist faction survived another nine years as a fighting force but was always fighting a war it had already lost, which is a specific kind of war that produces a specific kind of soldier.\n\nQianchan’s decision to intervene directly — two expeditionary divisions committed to a foreign civil conflict — was the most significant military-political decision this republic has made since the Caldera Accords. The decision is justified, in retrospect, by the outcome. The Royalist government is real.\n\nThe Fire Nation’s reforms are imperfect and ongoing. The cost was also real. 847 Qianchan personnel died at Soka Bay. Their names are in the records of the 2nd Expeditionary Division.\n\nThis newspaper will print them next week, in full, as a matter of record. Decisions that produce outcomes have costs. Both should be in the record."},"t":1793607117818},"city_celebrates_council_calls_for_reflection":{"title":"City celebrates; Council calls for reflection","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Republic City marked the end of the Fire Nation civil war with celebrations that ranged from organized street gatherings in the Earthen Quarter to a more subdued civic ceremony outside the Council Hall, where Council President Raimi spoke for six minutes without once using the word 'victory.' 'The war across the water has ended,' Raimi said.\u003c/p>\u003cp>'Our own work has not. The people who fought that war for eighteen years are not done with it. They will carry it home.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The question for this republic is what we do when they arrive.' The Earthen Quarter’s celebration was larger, louder, and more specifically felt. Many of the quarter’s residents have family connections to the colonial territories whose post-war governance is still being shaped.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For them, the end of the Fire Nation civil war means the Royalist government is now the government, which means the reform process that government has promised is now more likely to produce the land restitution and political recognition that the TRC process has so far delivered incompletely. The port district’s response was quieter. Several Fire Nation-heritage families flew the new Fire Nation civil flag — the flower design adopted this year to replace the imperial war flag — outside their homes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Whether this is celebration or signal is a matter this newspaper is watching.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"Interior","kick":"","deck":["\"The war across the water has ended. Our own work has not,\" the Council President says outside the Council Hall."],"by":"By Tan Meizhi, City Correspondent","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":8,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"\"The war across the water has ended. Our own work has not,\" the Council President says outside the Council Hall.","src":"{j} Republic City marked the end of the Fire Nation civil war with celebrations that ranged from organized street gatherings in the Earthen Quarter to a more subdued civic ceremony outside the Council Hall, where Council President Raimi spoke for six minutes without once using the word 'victory.' 'The war across the water has ended,' Raimi said.\n\n'Our own work has not. The people who fought that war for eighteen years are not done with it. They will carry it home.\n\nThe question for this republic is what we do when they arrive.' The Earthen Quarter’s celebration was larger, louder, and more specifically felt. Many of the quarter’s residents have family connections to the colonial territories whose post-war governance is still being shaped.\n\nFor them, the end of the Fire Nation civil war means the Royalist government is now the government, which means the reform process that government has promised is now more likely to produce the land restitution and political recognition that the TRC process has so far delivered incompletely. The port district’s response was quieter. Several Fire Nation-heritage families flew the new Fire Nation civil flag — the flower design adopted this year to replace the imperial war flag — outside their homes.\n\nWhether this is celebration or signal is a matter this newspaper is watching."},"t":1793607117819},"the_war_behind_the_war_eighteen_years_and_what_the_royalist_victory_means":{"title":"The war behind the war: eighteen years, and what the Royalist victory means","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Fire Nation civil war began on the same day the Hundred Year War ended. This is not coincidence. The war that the world fought for a century produced, within the Fire Nation itself, a set of unresolved questions that Ozai’s defeat made simultaneously urgent and unmanageable: what was the empire, what had it done, who was responsible, and what should come after it. Three factions gave three answers. The Royalists, under Zuko, said: a reformed constitutional monarchy, accountability through process, change at the pace the institution can sustain. The Loyalists, under Azula’s leadership until the Loyalist defeat at Soka Bay in 109 AG and her death in the Duel of the Palace in May, said: the empire was correct, its methods were not mistakes but virtues, what failed was the commitment. The Salvationists said: reform is insufficient, the institution itself is the disease, revolutionary transformation is the only honest response to what the empire was. The Royalists won. This is the correct outcome from this newspaper’s perspective. The Fire Nation required a government that could function, that could implement the Caldera Accords, that could begin the accountability process the TRC requires. Zuko’s government has done these things imperfectly and in progress. The alternatives — a Loyalist restoration or a Salvationist revolutionary state — would have produced outcomes worse for every party including the Fire Nation’s own people. This newspaper says the Royalists won correctly while recording what the victory cost.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The cost\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Royalist forces: 84,000 dead, 210,000 wounded. Loyalist forces: an estimated 110,000 dead (records incomplete, because the Kanbukyoku personnel absorbed into the Loyalist coalition were not systematically counted). Salvationist forces: an estimated 67,000 dead. Civilian casualties from the urban phases: 44,000 confirmed, with estimates to 90,000. That is between 305,000 and 461,000 dead in combat and in the urban phases. The Fire Nation’s own estimate, which includes famine, disease and reprisals, is about 3.5 million.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The compromise that made the Royalist victory possible\u003c/h3>\u003cp>In 106 AG, the Royalist government preserved the Ministry of National Enlightenment under a new name rather than dissolving it. The Ministry’s broadcast infrastructure and curriculum delivery network were necessary for governance. The reform government did not have the capacity to replace them. The Ministry survived. This is a factual matter, not an accusation. The Fire Lord made a pragmatic decision in a war for survival. The Republican Daily supported the decision at the time and supports it now. We also note it clearly, because the Ministry of Public Information and Cultural Affairs — which is what the Ministry of National Enlightenment is now called — employs former personnel of the Kanbukyoku’s propaganda division, and those people are now in the business of shaping what the Fire Nation believes about itself. This is worth knowing.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"x-pq\">“The right side does not mean without compromise. It means the compromises were worth the outcome.”\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What the Salvationist movement got right\u003c/h3>\u003cp>This may surprise readers of this newspaper, which has consistently opposed the Salvationist faction’s methods and goals. We are stating it plainly because the historical record requires it. The Salvationists correctly identified that the Hundred Year War was produced by a structural arrangement — bender supremacy institutionalized as political order — that the Royalist reform program does not fully address. Zuko’s Fire Nation is better than Ozai’s Fire Nation. It remains a state in which bending ability shapes access to power, social standing, and economic opportunity. The Royalist victory has not changed this. It has made it more acceptable. The Salvationists' analysis was correct. Their methods were catastrophic. These two things are compatible. An accurate diagnosis of a problem does not license any method of treatment. The methods they chose — armed revolution, the specific violence of the Jinsha Pass battle, the Suiko district’s forty-seven-day holdout that cost lives on both sides — produced their own injuries and did not produce the transformation they promised. The ideas survive the faction’s defeat. They will resurface. This newspaper expects to be covering them for the rest of its operational life.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"explainer","sec":"Explainers","kick":"","deck":["The civil war cost between 305,000 and 461,000 lives in combat and the cities. The Daily says the Royalists won correctly, and records what the victory cost."],"by":"By Heng Jinrui, Senior Foreign Correspondent","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":118,"m":8,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The civil war cost between 305,000 and 461,000 lives in combat and the cities. The Daily says the Royalists won correctly, and records what the victory cost.","src":"{j} The Fire Nation civil war began on the same day the Hundred Year War ended. This is not coincidence. The war that the world fought for a century produced, within the Fire Nation itself, a set of unresolved questions that Ozai’s defeat made simultaneously urgent and unmanageable: what was the empire, what had it done, who was responsible, and what should come after it. Three factions gave three answers. The Royalists, under Zuko, said: a reformed constitutional monarchy, accountability through process, change at the pace the institution can sustain. The Loyalists, under Azula’s leadership until the Loyalist defeat at Soka Bay in 109 AG and her death in the Duel of the Palace in May, said: the empire was correct, its methods were not mistakes but virtues, what failed was the commitment. The Salvationists said: reform is insufficient, the institution itself is the disease, revolutionary transformation is the only honest response to what the empire was. The Royalists won. This is the correct outcome from this newspaper’s perspective. The Fire Nation required a government that could function, that could implement the Caldera Accords, that could begin the accountability process the TRC requires. Zuko’s government has done these things imperfectly and in progress. The alternatives — a Loyalist restoration or a Salvationist revolutionary state — would have produced outcomes worse for every party including the Fire Nation’s own people. This newspaper says the Royalists won correctly while recording what the victory cost.\n\n## The cost\n\nRoyalist forces: 84,000 dead, 210,000 wounded. Loyalist forces: an estimated 110,000 dead (records incomplete, because the Kanbukyoku personnel absorbed into the Loyalist coalition were not systematically counted). Salvationist forces: an estimated 67,000 dead. Civilian casualties from the urban phases: 44,000 confirmed, with estimates to 90,000. That is between 305,000 and 461,000 dead in combat and in the urban phases. The Fire Nation’s own estimate, which includes famine, disease and reprisals, is about 3.5 million.\n\n## The compromise that made the Royalist victory possible\n\nIn 106 AG, the Royalist government preserved the Ministry of National Enlightenment under a new name rather than dissolving it. The Ministry’s broadcast infrastructure and curriculum delivery network were necessary for governance. The reform government did not have the capacity to replace them. The Ministry survived. This is a factual matter, not an accusation. The Fire Lord made a pragmatic decision in a war for survival. The Republican Daily supported the decision at the time and supports it now. We also note it clearly, because the Ministry of Public Information and Cultural Affairs — which is what the Ministry of National Enlightenment is now called — employs former personnel of the Kanbukyoku’s propaganda division, and those people are now in the business of shaping what the Fire Nation believes about itself. This is worth knowing. \n\n{pq} “The right side does not mean without compromise. It means the compromises were worth the outcome.”\n\n## What the Salvationist movement got right\n\nThis may surprise readers of this newspaper, which has consistently opposed the Salvationist faction’s methods and goals. We are stating it plainly because the historical record requires it. The Salvationists correctly identified that the Hundred Year War was produced by a structural arrangement — bender supremacy institutionalized as political order — that the Royalist reform program does not fully address. Zuko’s Fire Nation is better than Ozai’s Fire Nation. It remains a state in which bending ability shapes access to power, social standing, and economic opportunity. The Royalist victory has not changed this. It has made it more acceptable. The Salvationists' analysis was correct. Their methods were catastrophic. These two things are compatible. An accurate diagnosis of a problem does not license any method of treatment. The methods they chose — armed revolution, the specific violence of the Jinsha Pass battle, the Suiko district’s forty-seven-day holdout that cost lives on both sides — produced their own injuries and did not produce the transformation they promised. The ideas survive the faction’s defeat. They will resurface. This newspaper expects to be covering them for the rest of its operational life."},"t":1793607117820},"i_was_at_soka_bay_i_think_about_it":{"title":"I was at Soka Bay. I think about it.","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Liang Jiao, 34, works as a construction contractor in Republic City’s port district. He came here after his discharge from the Qianchan military in 112 AG. He agreed to speak on record.\u003c/p>\u003cp>His account has been lightly edited for length.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">On joining the 2nd Expeditionary Division\u003c/h3>\u003cp>'I was twenty-five when we crossed into Fire Nation territory. I had done mandatory service, served three years, was released, went back to my family’s construction business in Gaoling. Then the Congress voted 287 to 194 and we got call-up notices.\u003c/p>\u003cp>I thought about not going. I had a life. My father needed me.\u003c/p>\u003cp>I went. I think most of us went because the mandatory service culture means you go when called, even when you’re past the initial service window. That’s what the compact is.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">On Soka Bay\u003c/h3>\u003cp>'Eleven days. The Loyalist force we engaged was well-organized. Better than I expected from a faction that had been losing for nine years.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Kanbukyoku training culture produces a specific kind of discipline — they fight well because they were taught to function toward a goal under pressure and they don’t stop easily. We broke them on the eighth day. The last three days were the cleanup, which is the worst part.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Cleanup is when you see who specifically is dead. 'I lost four people from my unit. I know their names.\u003c/p>\u003cp>I think about Soka Bay regularly. Not constantly, but regularly. I don’t think that will stop.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The veterans' support network here in the port district helps. There are more of us than you’d expect.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">On what the intervention was for\u003c/h3>\u003cp>'I think we did the right thing. I believed that then and I believe it now. The Royalist government is a real reform government and it would not exist without Soka Bay.\u003c/p>\u003cp>But I want to be clear about something that sometimes gets lost in the 'right thing' framing: 847 people from my division died for that outcome. They are not abstractions. They had families.\u003c/p>\u003cp>They had mandatory service obligations that put them in that field. The outcome was worth the cost. The cost was also real.\u003c/p>\u003cp>I want both of those sentences to be heard.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">On living in the republic\u003c/h3>\u003cp>'Republic City is good. The port district is mostly Fire Nation-heritage and EK-heritage people who have figured out how to be neighbors. We argue about the TRC.\u003c/p>\u003cp>We argue about who owns what. We have the same arguments every week in the tavern. Nobody gets hit.\u003c/p>\u003cp>That’s a good neighborhood. 'I have been watching the civil war end from here. I am glad it’s over.\u003c/p>\u003cp>I am glad the right side won. I am going to have a drink tonight with my neighbors, including the ones whose families were on the other side of Soka Bay, and we are going to not talk about that and talk about something else, because that is also how you live with what happened.'\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"interview","sec":"Voices","kick":"","deck":["A veteran of the Qianchan 2nd Expeditionary Division speaks on the record for the first time about the engagement that decided the civil war."],"by":"By Dao Ruiming, Military Correspondent","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":8,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"A veteran of the Qianchan 2nd Expeditionary Division speaks on the record for the first time about the engagement that decided the civil war.","src":"{j} Liang Jiao, 34, works as a construction contractor in Republic City’s port district. He came here after his discharge from the Qianchan military in 112 AG. He agreed to speak on record.\n\nHis account has been lightly edited for length.\n\n## On joining the 2nd Expeditionary Division\n\n'I was twenty-five when we crossed into Fire Nation territory. I had done mandatory service, served three years, was released, went back to my family’s construction business in Gaoling. Then the Congress voted 287 to 194 and we got call-up notices.\n\nI thought about not going. I had a life. My father needed me.\n\nI went. I think most of us went because the mandatory service culture means you go when called, even when you’re past the initial service window. That’s what the compact is.\n\n## On Soka Bay\n\n'Eleven days. The Loyalist force we engaged was well-organized. Better than I expected from a faction that had been losing for nine years.\n\nThe Kanbukyoku training culture produces a specific kind of discipline — they fight well because they were taught to function toward a goal under pressure and they don’t stop easily. We broke them on the eighth day. The last three days were the cleanup, which is the worst part.\n\nCleanup is when you see who specifically is dead. 'I lost four people from my unit. I know their names.\n\nI think about Soka Bay regularly. Not constantly, but regularly. I don’t think that will stop.\n\nThe veterans' support network here in the port district helps. There are more of us than you’d expect.\n\n## On what the intervention was for\n\n'I think we did the right thing. I believed that then and I believe it now. The Royalist government is a real reform government and it would not exist without Soka Bay.\n\nBut I want to be clear about something that sometimes gets lost in the 'right thing' framing: 847 people from my division died for that outcome. They are not abstractions. They had families.\n\nThey had mandatory service obligations that put them in that field. The outcome was worth the cost. The cost was also real.\n\nI want both of those sentences to be heard.\n\n## On living in the republic\n\n'Republic City is good. The port district is mostly Fire Nation-heritage and EK-heritage people who have figured out how to be neighbors. We argue about the TRC.\n\nWe argue about who owns what. We have the same arguments every week in the tavern. Nobody gets hit.\n\nThat’s a good neighborhood. 'I have been watching the civil war end from here. I am glad it’s over.\n\nI am glad the right side won. I am going to have a drink tonight with my neighbors, including the ones whose families were on the other side of Soka Bay, and we are going to not talk about that and talk about something else, because that is also how you live with what happened.'"},"t":1793607117821},"what_comes_after_the_salvationist_ideas_outlive_the_salvationist_faction":{"title":"What comes after: the Salvationist ideas outlive the Salvationist faction","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>The Salvationist movement is formally dissolved. Its military formations are disarming. Its leadership faces the accountability process.\u003c/p>\u003cp>By every formal measure it has lost. Its ideas have not lost, because ideas do not dissolve with the organizations that hold them. The Salvationist argument — that the Hundred Year War was produced by bender supremacy as a structural political arrangement, and that structural problems require structural solutions, not reformed monarchies — will persist in Fire Nation political culture as a critique from the left of whatever the Royalist government produces.\u003c/p>\u003cp>It will also persist internationally. This newspaper has been told by sources within the Qianchan academic community that the Gaoling Political Science faculty has received three manuscript submissions in the past year that draw explicitly on Salvationist analytical frameworks while explicitly rejecting Salvationist methods. The manuscripts are not from Salvationist survivors.\u003c/p>\u003cp>They are from Qianchan academics who found the analysis useful. The ideas are moving. They will arrive somewhere.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This is worth watching.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"feature","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["By every formal measure the movement has lost. Its ideas are already moving."],"by":"By Ping Wenhua, Analysis","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":8,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"By every formal measure the movement has lost. Its ideas are already moving.","src":"The Salvationist movement is formally dissolved. Its military formations are disarming. Its leadership faces the accountability process.\n\nBy every formal measure it has lost. Its ideas have not lost, because ideas do not dissolve with the organizations that hold them. The Salvationist argument — that the Hundred Year War was produced by bender supremacy as a structural political arrangement, and that structural problems require structural solutions, not reformed monarchies — will persist in Fire Nation political culture as a critique from the left of whatever the Royalist government produces.\n\nIt will also persist internationally. This newspaper has been told by sources within the Qianchan academic community that the Gaoling Political Science faculty has received three manuscript submissions in the past year that draw explicitly on Salvationist analytical frameworks while explicitly rejecting Salvationist methods. The manuscripts are not from Salvationist survivors.\n\nThey are from Qianchan academics who found the analysis useful. The ideas are moving. They will arrive somewhere.\n\nThis is worth watching."},"t":1793607117822},"senate_to_debate_nuclear_framework_in_september":{"title":"Assembly to debate nuclear framework in September","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The URN National Assembly’s Foreign Affairs Committee has scheduled a September session to address the Arms Control Treaty framework established at the First Treaty of Gaoling in 100 AG and revised at the Caldera Accords. The session will consider two questions: whether the current Tieshan provisions adequately reflect the post-civil-war strategic situation, and whether the URN should seek formal observer status in the treaty framework currently managed by Qianchan and the Fire Nation. The URN is not a signatory to the Arms Control Treaty.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The republic’s position since 104 AG has been that as a non-nuclear state it has no treaty obligations and therefore no treaty standing. Assembly member Kira Baolin of the Earthen Quarter delegation has been the primary advocate for observer status, arguing that a republic whose founding territory sits between two nuclear-capable states has a direct interest in the treaty’s terms regardless of its signatory status. The Qianchan Foreign Ministry declined to comment on whether it would support URN observer status.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Fire Nation’s new government has not issued a formal position. The treaty framework debate is expected to be contentious.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["The republic is not a signatory to the Arms Control Treaty. Senator Kira Baolin wants observer status."],"by":"By Weng Lishu, Congressional Correspondent","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":8,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The republic is not a signatory to the Arms Control Treaty. Senator Kira Baolin wants observer status.","src":"{j} The URN National Assembly’s Foreign Affairs Committee has scheduled a September session to address the Arms Control Treaty framework established at the First Treaty of Gaoling in 100 AG and revised at the Caldera Accords. The session will consider two questions: whether the current Tieshan provisions adequately reflect the post-civil-war strategic situation, and whether the URN should seek formal observer status in the treaty framework currently managed by Qianchan and the Fire Nation. The URN is not a signatory to the Arms Control Treaty.\n\nThe republic’s position since 104 AG has been that as a non-nuclear state it has no treaty obligations and therefore no treaty standing. Assembly member Kira Baolin of the Earthen Quarter delegation has been the primary advocate for observer status, arguing that a republic whose founding territory sits between two nuclear-capable states has a direct interest in the treaty’s terms regardless of its signatory status. The Qianchan Foreign Ministry declined to comment on whether it would support URN observer status.\n\nThe Fire Nation’s new government has not issued a formal position. The treaty framework debate is expected to be contentious."},"t":1793607117823},"land_commission_s_year_two_report_due_next_week_early_numbers_are_concerning":{"title":"Land Commission's Year Two report due next week; early numbers are concerning","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Land Restitution Commission will deliver its second-year progress report to Congress next Wednesday. Sources familiar with the report’s contents told this newspaper the numbers are below the target set in the Commission’s founding charter. Year One target: 25% of contested claims resolved.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Year One actual: 18%. Year Two target: additional 20%. Year Two preliminary: approximately 12-14% additional resolution, pending final certification.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The primary source of below-target performance is the same as in Year One: contested claims involving land currently held by Fire Nation settler-heritage families with pre-Accord legal documentation. The Commission’s authority to override property law documentation is limited by the constitutional framework under which it was established. The claims that remain unresolved are disproportionately the ones where the legal documentation is strongest on the current holder’s side and the historical injustice is clearest on the claimant’s side.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Commissioner Dao Linfang is expected to request an expansion of the Commission’s legal authority in the Year Two report. The request will require Congressional action. The Civic party bloc has previously opposed authority expansion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Coalition bloc has the votes to pass it with one or two Civic defections.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["Year One: 18% of claims resolved against a 25% target. Year Two is tracking 12 to 14% against 20%."],"by":"By Chen Dawei, Domestic Correspondent","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":8,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Year One: 18% of claims resolved against a 25% target. Year Two is tracking 12 to 14% against 20%.","src":"{j} The Land Restitution Commission will deliver its second-year progress report to Congress next Wednesday. Sources familiar with the report’s contents told this newspaper the numbers are below the target set in the Commission’s founding charter. Year One target: 25% of contested claims resolved.\n\nYear One actual: 18%. Year Two target: additional 20%. Year Two preliminary: approximately 12-14% additional resolution, pending final certification.\n\nThe primary source of below-target performance is the same as in Year One: contested claims involving land currently held by Fire Nation settler-heritage families with pre-Accord legal documentation. The Commission’s authority to override property law documentation is limited by the constitutional framework under which it was established. The claims that remain unresolved are disproportionately the ones where the legal documentation is strongest on the current holder’s side and the historical injustice is clearest on the claimant’s side.\n\nCommissioner Dao Linfang is expected to request an expansion of the Commission’s legal authority in the Year Two report. The request will require Congressional action. The Civic party bloc has previously opposed authority expansion.\n\nThe Coalition bloc has the votes to pass it with one or two Civic defections."},"t":1793607117824},"port_authority_approves_third_phase_of_harbor_expansion":{"title":"Port Authority approves third phase of harbor expansion","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Republic City Port Authority voted 7-2 yesterday to approve the third phase of the harbor expansion project. The approved phase adds twelve deep-water berths capable of accommodating the new generation of heavy transport vessels and includes a dedicated Fire Nation shipping lane — a provision that was the most contested element of the vote. The dedicated Fire Nation lane was opposed by the two Coalition representatives on the Authority board on the grounds that it creates preferential infrastructure for what remains, in practice, a primarily Fire Nation-heritage commercial network.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Civic majority argued that Fire Nation-heritage shippers represent 34% of the port’s traffic and that infrastructure that serves 34% of traffic is reasonable allocation rather than preference. Construction is expected to begin in spring 119 AG and complete within three years. The expansion is projected to increase port throughput by 40% and generate approximately 800 permanent jobs in the port district, with an additional 2,000 construction positions during the build phase.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"Port & Trade","kick":"","deck":["Twelve new deep-water berths and a dedicated Fire Nation shipping lane, the most contested part of the vote."],"by":"By Tan Meizhi, City Correspondent","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":8,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Twelve new deep-water berths and a dedicated Fire Nation shipping lane, the most contested part of the vote.","src":"{j} The Republic City Port Authority voted 7-2 yesterday to approve the third phase of the harbor expansion project. The approved phase adds twelve deep-water berths capable of accommodating the new generation of heavy transport vessels and includes a dedicated Fire Nation shipping lane — a provision that was the most contested element of the vote. The dedicated Fire Nation lane was opposed by the two Coalition representatives on the Authority board on the grounds that it creates preferential infrastructure for what remains, in practice, a primarily Fire Nation-heritage commercial network.\n\nThe Civic majority argued that Fire Nation-heritage shippers represent 34% of the port’s traffic and that infrastructure that serves 34% of traffic is reasonable allocation rather than preference. Construction is expected to begin in spring 119 AG and complete within three years. The expansion is projected to increase port throughput by 40% and generate approximately 800 permanent jobs in the port district, with an additional 2,000 construction positions during the build phase."},"t":1793607117825},"the_wrong_victory":{"title":"The wrong victory","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>The Republican Daily has declared the Royalist victory correct. I want to offer a different framing, not because I preferred the Loyalists — I did not — but because I think 'correct' is doing too much work in that sentence. The Royalist government is better than the alternatives.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This is true. It is also the minimum standard for correctness: being better than two factions, one of which wanted to restore an empire that killed a hundred thousand people in this republic’s founding decade and one of which wanted to burn down the institutional structure before building something to replace it. Being better than those options is not a high bar.\u003c/p>\u003cp>What the Royalist victory preserves, alongside the genuine reforms, is a Fire Nation in which the apparatus that ran the board — the documented, named, recorded Kanbukyoku files — has been disbanded as an organization and its personnel dispersed into other roles. Twelve people have been convicted. Fourteen thousand names are in the Commission’s record.\u003c/p>\u003cp>These numbers are in relationship with each other and the relationship is a problem. I am not saying Zuko should have done otherwise. I am saying 'correct' should be able to contain the full weight of what it means, including the gap between twelve and fourteen thousand.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If it cannot, we should use a different word.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>Yun Dashan is a contributing columnist. His views are his own.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"opinion","sec":"Voices","kick":"","deck":["\"Correct\" is doing too much work in that sentence, writes Yun Dashan. Twelve people have been convicted. Fourteen thousand names are in the Commission’s record."],"by":"By Yun Dashan, Contributing Columnist","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":8,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"\"Correct\" is doing too much work in that sentence, writes Yun Dashan. Twelve people have been convicted. Fourteen thousand names are in the Commission’s record.","src":"The Republican Daily has declared the Royalist victory correct. I want to offer a different framing, not because I preferred the Loyalists — I did not — but because I think 'correct' is doing too much work in that sentence. The Royalist government is better than the alternatives.\n\nThis is true. It is also the minimum standard for correctness: being better than two factions, one of which wanted to restore an empire that killed a hundred thousand people in this republic’s founding decade and one of which wanted to burn down the institutional structure before building something to replace it. Being better than those options is not a high bar.\n\nWhat the Royalist victory preserves, alongside the genuine reforms, is a Fire Nation in which the apparatus that ran the board — the documented, named, recorded Kanbukyoku files — has been disbanded as an organization and its personnel dispersed into other roles. Twelve people have been convicted. Fourteen thousand names are in the Commission’s record.\n\nThese numbers are in relationship with each other and the relationship is a problem. I am not saying Zuko should have done otherwise. I am saying 'correct' should be able to contain the full weight of what it means, including the gap between twelve and fourteen thousand.\n\nIf it cannot, we should use a different word.\n\n//Yun Dashan is a contributing columnist. His views are his own.//"},"t":1793607117826},"letters_on_the_civil_war_s_end":{"title":"Letters: on the civil war's end","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>Eighteen years. My brother was at Soka Bay with the 2nd Expedition. He came home. Four men from his unit did not. The Republican Daily has covered this war honestly, which is more than I can say for most of the press on either side of the water. Thank you for that.\u003c/p>\u003cp>— K.L., Port District\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Republican Daily calls the Royalist victory 'correct.' It may be correct. It is also convenient for a newspaper published in a republic that backed the Royalists with two divisions. Convenience and correctness can coincide. They are not the same thing.\u003c/p>\u003cp>— Name withheld\u003c/p>\u003cp>My family came from the colonial territories. My grandmother was in the fifth colony revolt. Qianchan watched that fail too, as it watched the first four fail. Now the civil war ends and we are supposed to celebrate that the right government won in the Fire Nation. I am glad the right government won. I would also like someone to explain why the right government winning across the water is more important than the right governance happening here, where the Land Commission is running behind target and the Civic bloc is blocking the authority expansion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>— Mao Jinge, Earthen Quarter\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Salvationist ideas did not die at Suiko. Your own analysis says this on page two. That is the most important sentence in today’s paper and I am not certain the editorial board understood what it was printing.\u003c/p>\u003cp>— T.W., New Quarter\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"letter","sec":"Letters","kick":"","deck":["Readers write about Soka Bay, the word \"correct\", the land commission, and Suiko."],"by":"","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":8,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Readers write about Soka Bay, the word \"correct\", the land commission, and Suiko.","src":"Eighteen years. My brother was at Soka Bay with the 2nd Expedition. He came home. Four men from his unit did not. The Republican Daily has covered this war honestly, which is more than I can say for most of the press on either side of the water. Thank you for that.\n\n— K.L., Port District\n\nThe Republican Daily calls the Royalist victory 'correct.' It may be correct. It is also convenient for a newspaper published in a republic that backed the Royalists with two divisions. Convenience and correctness can coincide. They are not the same thing.\n\n— Name withheld\n\nMy family came from the colonial territories. My grandmother was in the fifth colony revolt. Qianchan watched that fail too, as it watched the first four fail. Now the civil war ends and we are supposed to celebrate that the right government won in the Fire Nation. I am glad the right government won. I would also like someone to explain why the right government winning across the water is more important than the right governance happening here, where the Land Commission is running behind target and the Civic bloc is blocking the authority expansion.\n\n— Mao Jinge, Earthen Quarter\n\nThe Salvationist ideas did not die at Suiko. Your own analysis says this on page two. That is the most important sentence in today’s paper and I am not certain the editorial board understood what it was printing.\n\n— T.W., New Quarter"},"t":1793607117827},"deaths_the_end_of_the_fire_nation_civil_war":{"title":"Deaths: the end of the Fire Nation civil war","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>CHEN WEI — age 29, Qianchan 2nd Expeditionary Division, reserve activation Died August 11 of wounds sustained at Suiko. Born in Gaoling. He was a carpenter by trade. He is survived by his parents and a sister. His company commander described him as reliable and steady. He was one of four Qianchan reserve personnel killed in the final week of operations.\u003c/p>\u003cp>MARISOL OF THE THIRD WAVE — age 41, Salvationist People’s Iron Column Died August 11, Suiko district. Her given family name was not provided by those who knew her. She had been fighting for fourteen years. She is survived by a son, age 10, whose location is not provided by her surviving comrades at their request. No further information available.\u003c/p>\u003cp>HARU KESHIN — age 67, Royalist Home Guard, retired Died August 9 of natural causes, Caldera City. He served in the Home Guard from 100-108 AG. He was a schoolteacher before the civil conflict and returned to teaching after his service. He is survived by his wife, three children, and seven grandchildren. His students described him as patient and demanding in equal measure.\u003c/p>\u003cp>UNKNOWN SALVATIONIST FIGHTER — estimated age 20-25, Suiko district, final engagement Killed August 10 during the final days of the Suiko engagement. Identity not established at time of publication. Royalist forces are working to identify remains. If you have information about a missing family member who may have been with the Iron Column, contact the Royalist Army Records Office.\u003c/p>\u003cp>YUKI SANA — age 44, Civilian — former Kanbukyoku Truth Commission archivist Died August 7 of illness, Republic City. She compiled the Truth Commission’s nine-volume record and resigned when it closed its hearings. She is survived by a husband and two daughters. Her resignation letter is in the public record. She is also survived by nine volumes that will outlast everyone who reads this column.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"obit","sec":"Obituaries","kick":"","deck":["Those killed in the final engagements are noted."],"by":"","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":118,"m":8,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Those killed in the final engagements are noted.","src":"CHEN WEI — age 29, Qianchan 2nd Expeditionary Division, reserve activation Died August 11 of wounds sustained at Suiko. Born in Gaoling. He was a carpenter by trade. He is survived by his parents and a sister. His company commander described him as reliable and steady. He was one of four Qianchan reserve personnel killed in the final week of operations.\n\nMARISOL OF THE THIRD WAVE — age 41, Salvationist People’s Iron Column Died August 11, Suiko district. Her given family name was not provided by those who knew her. She had been fighting for fourteen years. She is survived by a son, age 10, whose location is not provided by her surviving comrades at their request. No further information available.\n\nHARU KESHIN — age 67, Royalist Home Guard, retired Died August 9 of natural causes, Caldera City. He served in the Home Guard from 100-108 AG. He was a schoolteacher before the civil conflict and returned to teaching after his service. He is survived by his wife, three children, and seven grandchildren. His students described him as patient and demanding in equal measure.\n\nUNKNOWN SALVATIONIST FIGHTER — estimated age 20-25, Suiko district, final engagement Killed August 10 during the final days of the Suiko engagement. Identity not established at time of publication. Royalist forces are working to identify remains. If you have information about a missing family member who may have been with the Iron Column, contact the Royalist Army Records Office.\n\nYUKI SANA — age 44, Civilian — former Kanbukyoku Truth Commission archivist Died August 7 of illness, Republic City. She compiled the Truth Commission’s nine-volume record and resigned when it closed its hearings. She is survived by a husband and two daughters. Her resignation letter is in the public record. She is also survived by nine volumes that will outlast everyone who reads this column."},"t":1793607117828},"first_concord_forum_ends_31_hours_late_with_a_charter_one_earth_delegation_that_would_not_sign_and_no_agreement_on_what_a_leftist_is":{"title":"First Concord forum ends 31 hours late with a charter, one Earth delegation that would not sign and no agreement on what a leftist is","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The first full forum of the Concord of Nations, a body that has existed on paper since the Caldera Accord of 121 AG, closed at 2:10 a.m. on Sunday, 31 hours after it was due to end, with a standing charter signed by 19 of the 23 delegations that came to Caldera City, an Earth State delegation that would not sign, a warning from an exiled king and no agreement on what the word &quot;leftist&quot; means.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Palace Protocol Office, Sept. 17, 176 AG (printed in full): &quot;The Protocol Office regrets the delay to the closing session. The Office confirms that the charter was signed in accordance with the rules of the forum. Questions of seating were settled by the delegations themselves.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Why the forum met\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The forum was called by Tajisaki and Qianchan after the last forces of the People's Republic of Earth were defeated this year, to give the Concord a standing charter and a standing body of states that would work against the Equalist and leftist movements. It opened on Sept. 14 at the Palace, with Fire Lord Izumi in the chair. Foreign Minister Hayato Mori said it would be &quot;a place where states argue before they arm.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">One chair, two claims\u003c/h3>\u003cp>One delegation arrived from the Earth lands, and it was led by the Generalissimo herself. Kuvira, who has ruled the Earth State from Ba Sing Se since the coup of 174 AG, came to Caldera City with her spokesman, Chen Yulan, and demanded the chair marked &quot;Earth Kingdom.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The claim was contested before the first session ended. On the second day the United Republic's delegation handed the Protocol Office a letter from King Wu, who has lived in exile in Republic City since 171 AG. It was read into the record. &quot;A general who broke her oath to me asks you for my chair,&quot; the letter said. &quot;Seat her, and you will have taught her that a coup is a credential. I ask you only to remember what she did with it.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Protocol Office placed one chair at the end of the hall with no placard. The Earth seat was left vacant in the charter, and the Earth State's delegation did not sign. Asked by reporters whether her state meant to take a grander name, Kuvira said: &quot;The Earth State will be called what its people call it.&quot; Qianchan, which is working with the Royalists to restore King Wu or the old confederation, did not comment on the letter.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What is a leftist?\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The disagreement that cost the forum most of its extra day was over Article 4, which commits members to exchange information on groups seeking to overthrow a member's order by force. The United Republic wanted the article to name the Equalists, whom it declared terrorists in 170 AG. Tajisaki wanted &quot;leftist movements&quot; added. The Northern Water Tribe's envoy, Sedna, objected that the term would cover the labor guilds of the north.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The first vote failed, 12 to 8 with 3 abstentions, short of the 14 needed. The article passed on Saturday evening, 19 to 0 with 4 abstentions, after the word &quot;leftist&quot; was struck. Qianchan's Deputy Foreign Minister Shen Lihua abstained. &quot;We came to build a table,&quot; she said, &quot;not to agree on who sits at the other side of it.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Interpreters and names\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The session was suspended for two hours and forty minutes on Friday when the three court interpreters for old Earth Kingdom speech refused to translate for the Earth State's delegation. The Protocol Office said they had been &quot;released from their duty.&quot; The Earth State's text was later read in the common tongue.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The plenary record, kept by clerks, shows &quot;Tajisaki&quot; used 214 times and &quot;Fire Nation&quot; 38 times, 29 of them by the envoy of the Southern Water Tribe, Naalak.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The gate\u003c/h3>\u003cp>A delegation from the Sand Union, the Si Wong coalition, was denied a seat and withdrew on Saturday, with a statement that the forum &quot;meets in a Palace and speaks for no desert.&quot; Outside the Palace gate, demonstrators carrying banners for the Equalists and the Collective gathered on all three days. Caldera police said 31 people were detained. The organizers said 54.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Concord's first assembly is to meet in the spring.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This report draws on the plenary record supplied by the Protocol Office and interviews with eleven delegates. The Scire could not confirm the organizers' count of detentions, and could not confirm that the letter attributed to King Wu was written by him.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Nineteen of 23 delegations signed in Caldera City after a seating dispute, a warning from the exiled King Wu, an interpreter strike and a walkout"],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":176,"m":9,"d":17,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Nineteen of 23 delegations signed in Caldera City after a seating dispute, a warning from the exiled King Wu, an interpreter strike and a walkout","src":"{j} The first full forum of the Concord of Nations, a body that has existed on paper since the Caldera Accord of 121 AG, closed at 2:10 a.m. on Sunday, 31 hours after it was due to end, with a standing charter signed by 19 of the 23 delegations that came to Caldera City, an Earth State delegation that would not sign, a warning from an exiled king and no agreement on what the word \"leftist\" means.\n\n**Official statement.** Palace Protocol Office, Sept. 17, 176 AG (printed in full): \"The Protocol Office regrets the delay to the closing session. The Office confirms that the charter was signed in accordance with the rules of the forum. Questions of seating were settled by the delegations themselves.\"\n\n## Why the forum met\n\nThe forum was called by Tajisaki and Qianchan after the last forces of the People's Republic of Earth were defeated this year, to give the Concord a standing charter and a standing body of states that would work against the Equalist and leftist movements. It opened on Sept. 14 at the Palace, with Fire Lord Izumi in the chair. Foreign Minister Hayato Mori said it would be \"a place where states argue before they arm.\"\n\n## One chair, two claims\n\nOne delegation arrived from the Earth lands, and it was led by the Generalissimo herself. Kuvira, who has ruled the Earth State from Ba Sing Se since the coup of 174 AG, came to Caldera City with her spokesman, Chen Yulan, and demanded the chair marked \"Earth Kingdom.\"\n\nThe claim was contested before the first session ended. On the second day the United Republic's delegation handed the Protocol Office a letter from King Wu, who has lived in exile in Republic City since 171 AG. It was read into the record. \"A general who broke her oath to me asks you for my chair,\" the letter said. \"Seat her, and you will have taught her that a coup is a credential. I ask you only to remember what she did with it.\"\n\nThe Protocol Office placed one chair at the end of the hall with no placard. The Earth seat was left vacant in the charter, and the Earth State's delegation did not sign. Asked by reporters whether her state meant to take a grander name, Kuvira said: \"The Earth State will be called what its people call it.\" Qianchan, which is working with the Royalists to restore King Wu or the old confederation, did not comment on the letter.\n\n## What is a leftist?\n\nThe disagreement that cost the forum most of its extra day was over Article 4, which commits members to exchange information on groups seeking to overthrow a member's order by force. The United Republic wanted the article to name the Equalists, whom it declared terrorists in 170 AG. Tajisaki wanted \"leftist movements\" added. The Northern Water Tribe's envoy, Sedna, objected that the term would cover the labor guilds of the north.\n\nThe first vote failed, 12 to 8 with 3 abstentions, short of the 14 needed. The article passed on Saturday evening, 19 to 0 with 4 abstentions, after the word \"leftist\" was struck. Qianchan's Deputy Foreign Minister Shen Lihua abstained. \"We came to build a table,\" she said, \"not to agree on who sits at the other side of it.\"\n\n## Interpreters and names\n\nThe session was suspended for two hours and forty minutes on Friday when the three court interpreters for old Earth Kingdom speech refused to translate for the Earth State's delegation. The Protocol Office said they had been \"released from their duty.\" The Earth State's text was later read in the common tongue.\n\nThe plenary record, kept by clerks, shows \"Tajisaki\" used 214 times and \"Fire Nation\" 38 times, 29 of them by the envoy of the Southern Water Tribe, Naalak.\n\n## The gate\n\nA delegation from the Sand Union, the Si Wong coalition, was denied a seat and withdrew on Saturday, with a statement that the forum \"meets in a Palace and speaks for no desert.\" Outside the Palace gate, demonstrators carrying banners for the Equalists and the Collective gathered on all three days. Caldera police said 31 people were detained. The organizers said 54.\n\nThe Concord's first assembly is to meet in the spring.\n\n//This report draws on the plenary record supplied by the Protocol Office and interviews with eleven delegates. The Scire could not confirm the organizers' count of detentions, and could not confirm that the letter attributed to King Wu was written by him.//"},"t":1793608031205},"navy_gives_the_diet_no_account_of_how_kuvira_died_as_the_earth_lands_count_their_dead":{"title":"Navy gives the Diet no account of how Kuvira died as the Earth lands count their dead","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Three months after the reports of Kuvira’s death at the hands of the Avatar, no government has confirmed them, and the Diet was told on Tuesday that the Navy will not supply an account.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The former Empress of the Earth Empire was reported dead in December 182 AG, in the last weeks of the war with the United Republic, and the Avatar’s office has neither confirmed nor denied it. The Earth lands are still counting the cost of that war, and the Diet was asked on Tuesday to vote 60 million crowns of grain credit for the western provinces.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Foreign Affairs, March 12, 183 AG (abridged): &quot;The Ministry has no information on the circumstances of the death of the person formerly styled Empress of the Earth Empire that it is able to make public. The Ministry's concern is the people of the Earth lands.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The question\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Daichi Mori, a member of the Reform bloc, asked the Minister whether Fire Navy ships or pilots in the western theater had witnessed the death. &quot;The Diet is being asked to feed a country whose last ruler died in circumstances nobody will describe,&quot; he said, according to the transcript. &quot;I do not ask for her sake. I ask because a state that cannot say how its ruler died cannot say who rules.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Minister Mori said the Navy had ships and pilots &quot;in the region&quot; and that their reports were &quot;not matters for this chamber.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The credit\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The grain credit passed, 211 to 94. Members of the Old Guard bloc voted against it, saying Tajisaki had no duty to the Earth lands. Refugees from the western provinces are said by the Concord's relief agency to number about 400,000. The Scire could not confirm that figure.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This article is based on the Diet transcript of March 12 and a statement from the Ministry. The Scire asked the Ministry of the Navy to describe what its crews saw. It had not replied by the time this article went to press.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["Six months after the second civil war and the war with the Republic ended, no government has published how the former Empress died"],"by":"By Mika Tsuneda","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":183,"m":3,"d":12,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Six months after the second civil war and the war with the Republic ended, no government has published how the former Empress died","src":"{j} Three months after the reports of Kuvira’s death at the hands of the Avatar, no government has confirmed them, and the Diet was told on Tuesday that the Navy will not supply an account.\n\nThe former Empress of the Earth Empire was reported dead in December 182 AG, in the last weeks of the war with the United Republic, and the Avatar’s office has neither confirmed nor denied it. The Earth lands are still counting the cost of that war, and the Diet was asked on Tuesday to vote 60 million crowns of grain credit for the western provinces.\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Foreign Affairs, March 12, 183 AG (abridged): \"The Ministry has no information on the circumstances of the death of the person formerly styled Empress of the Earth Empire that it is able to make public. The Ministry's concern is the people of the Earth lands.\"\n\n## The question\n\nDaichi Mori, a member of the Reform bloc, asked the Minister whether Fire Navy ships or pilots in the western theater had witnessed the death. \"The Diet is being asked to feed a country whose last ruler died in circumstances nobody will describe,\" he said, according to the transcript. \"I do not ask for her sake. I ask because a state that cannot say how its ruler died cannot say who rules.\"\n\nMinister Mori said the Navy had ships and pilots \"in the region\" and that their reports were \"not matters for this chamber.\"\n\n## The credit\n\nThe grain credit passed, 211 to 94. Members of the Old Guard bloc voted against it, saying Tajisaki had no duty to the Earth lands. Refugees from the western provinces are said by the Concord's relief agency to number about 400,000. The Scire could not confirm that figure.\n\n//This article is based on the Diet transcript of March 12 and a statement from the Ministry. The Scire asked the Ministry of the Navy to describe what its crews saw. It had not replied by the time this article went to press.//"},"t":1793608032205},"agna_qel_a_settlers_dig_in_on_the_northern_earth_plains_as_tajisaki_asks_for_a_line_on_the_map":{"title":"Agna Qel'a settlers dig in on the northern Earth plains as Tajisaki asks for a line on the map","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Twelve settlements built by the Kingdom of Agna Qel'a now stand on the northern plains of the Earth lands, two years after the expedition of 181 AG landed, and the Kingdom of Tajisaki has formally asked for a line on the map.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The settlers are mostly from the North, in prefab houses of ice-board and timber, and they have been given grain by the Northern Chief's office. The plains are claimed by the Grand Council of Ba Sing Se, which has not controlled them since 174 AG, and by two village leagues, which say they never left.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Foreign Affairs, June 2, 183 AG (abridged): &quot;Tajisaki has no claim on the plains. It asks Agna Qel'a to state in writing the boundaries of the lands it holds, so that no ship or caravan of Tajisaki is taken for a trespasser.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Settlers and villagers\u003c/h3>\u003cp>In the largest of the settlements, which its residents call New Tupiq, 900 people have been given plots. &quot;We were told the land was empty,&quot; said Aput Kalluk, 41, a net-maker. &quot;We have met the people it was empty of. They bring us salt.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Rapa Sun, a village-league elder at Hanjiang, said the settlers' wells had drained a spring that fed three villages. &quot;They did not ask. Nobody asks anymore.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Chief's office\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Agna Qel'a's office said the settlers were &quot;guests of the Earth people&quot; and that no state had been consulted because &quot;no state exists on those plains to consult.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This report is based on a visit to four settlements and interviews with 31 people. The Scire could not confirm the settlers' number, which the Northern Chief's office put at 6,000 and the village leagues at 15,000.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Two years after the Kingdom of Agna Qel'a sent its expedition south, 12 new settlements stand beyond the old frontier and no one has said who owns the land"],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"NORTHERN EARTH KINGDOM","y":183,"m":6,"d":2,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Two years after the Kingdom of Agna Qel'a sent its expedition south, 12 new settlements stand beyond the old frontier and no one has said who owns the land","src":"{j} Twelve settlements built by the Kingdom of Agna Qel'a now stand on the northern plains of the Earth lands, two years after the expedition of 181 AG landed, and the Kingdom of Tajisaki has formally asked for a line on the map.\n\nThe settlers are mostly from the North, in prefab houses of ice-board and timber, and they have been given grain by the Northern Chief's office. The plains are claimed by the Grand Council of Ba Sing Se, which has not controlled them since 174 AG, and by two village leagues, which say they never left.\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Foreign Affairs, June 2, 183 AG (abridged): \"Tajisaki has no claim on the plains. It asks Agna Qel'a to state in writing the boundaries of the lands it holds, so that no ship or caravan of Tajisaki is taken for a trespasser.\"\n\n## Settlers and villagers\n\nIn the largest of the settlements, which its residents call New Tupiq, 900 people have been given plots. \"We were told the land was empty,\" said Aput Kalluk, 41, a net-maker. \"We have met the people it was empty of. They bring us salt.\"\n\nRapa Sun, a village-league elder at Hanjiang, said the settlers' wells had drained a spring that fed three villages. \"They did not ask. Nobody asks anymore.\"\n\n## The Chief's office\n\nAgna Qel'a's office said the settlers were \"guests of the Earth people\" and that no state had been consulted because \"no state exists on those plains to consult.\"\n\n//This report is based on a visit to four settlements and interviews with 31 people. The Scire could not confirm the settlers' number, which the Northern Chief's office put at 6,000 and the village leagues at 15,000.//"},"t":1793608033205},"si_wong_states_sign_earth_union_charter_ba_sing_se_s_grand_council_stays_home":{"title":"Si Wong states sign Earth Union charter; Ba Sing Se's Grand Council stays home","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Nine states of the Si Wong Desert and the western rivers signed the charter of the Earth Union at Earth Union City on Saturday, agreeing to common tariffs on oil, a common court and a joint force of 8,000, while the Grand Council of Ba Sing Se declined its invitation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Union's seat is the desert trading city on the Si Wong oil road. Its first Speaker, Haddad Roukan, said the Union was &quot;not a throne and not a general, but a table.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Foreign Affairs, April 20, 184 AG: &quot;Tajisaki has sent an observer to the signing. The observer holds no instructions to recognize any government.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Who signed\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The signers include the former Sand Union and the river cities of the west. Omashu sent a delegate but did not sign. The Grand Council of Ba Sing Se, which governs the old kingdom's center, said the Union was &quot;a customs league dressed as a country&quot; and that it would not &quot;sit at a table set by oil merchants.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The oil\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Under the charter, oil leaving the Si Wong pays a single Union tariff, which the Union says will fund the court and the force. Caldera's shipping houses said they would study it. &quot;A common tariff is a better price than nine,&quot; said Keiji Arai, a professor of economics at Caldera University. &quot;It is also one more thing for somebody to fight over.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This article is based on the signing ceremony and interviews with 17 delegates. The Scire could not confirm the size of the joint force.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Nine desert and river states agree to common tariffs and a common court; Tajisaki sends an observer, not an ambassador"],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"EARTH UNION CITY","y":184,"m":4,"d":20,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Nine desert and river states agree to common tariffs and a common court; Tajisaki sends an observer, not an ambassador","src":"{j} Nine states of the Si Wong Desert and the western rivers signed the charter of the Earth Union at Earth Union City on Saturday, agreeing to common tariffs on oil, a common court and a joint force of 8,000, while the Grand Council of Ba Sing Se declined its invitation.\n\nThe Union's seat is the desert trading city on the Si Wong oil road. Its first Speaker, Haddad Roukan, said the Union was \"not a throne and not a general, but a table.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Foreign Affairs, April 20, 184 AG: \"Tajisaki has sent an observer to the signing. The observer holds no instructions to recognize any government.\"\n\n## Who signed\n\nThe signers include the former Sand Union and the river cities of the west. Omashu sent a delegate but did not sign. The Grand Council of Ba Sing Se, which governs the old kingdom's center, said the Union was \"a customs league dressed as a country\" and that it would not \"sit at a table set by oil merchants.\"\n\n## The oil\n\nUnder the charter, oil leaving the Si Wong pays a single Union tariff, which the Union says will fund the court and the force. Caldera's shipping houses said they would study it. \"A common tariff is a better price than nine,\" said Keiji Arai, a professor of economics at Caldera University. \"It is also one more thing for somebody to fight over.\"\n\n//This article is based on the signing ceremony and interviews with 17 delegates. The Scire could not confirm the size of the joint force.//"},"t":1793608034205},"union_tariff_lifts_price_of_si_wong_oil_in_caldera_as_diet_argues_over_a_fuel_levy":{"title":"Union tariff lifts price of Si Wong oil in Caldera as Diet argues over a fuel levy","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The price of diesel at the Caldera harbor is up 14 percent since the spring, shipping houses said on Monday, and the Diet has taken up a proposal to put a levy on fuel to protect the fishing fleets of the south coast.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The increase follows the Earth Union's single tariff on Si Wong oil, which took effect in January. Port authorities said Caldera imported 40 percent of its fuel from the Union last year.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Trade, Oct. 6, 185 AG (abridged): &quot;The Ministry notes the rise in the price of diesel. It has no proposal to set prices. The Diet will decide whether a levy is warranted.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">In the Diet\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Daichi Mori, speaking for the Reform bloc, said the levy &quot;would tax the fishermen who can least afford it in order to protect the shipowners who can most.&quot; A member of the Old Guard bloc, Ishikawa Reiko of Shu Jing, said the Union's tariff was &quot;a tax laid on us by a country that does not exist.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The levy was sent to committee, 190 to 130.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">At the harbor\u003c/h3>\u003cp>&quot;We do not ask for a favor,&quot; said Takeo Sato, 52, who owns two boats at the south pier. &quot;We ask who sets the price of the thing we burn.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Scire could not confirm the Ministry's 40 percent figure, which was supplied by the harbor authority.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"Economy","kick":"","deck":["Diesel up 14 percent at the harbor, shipping houses blame the desert and the desert blames the shipping houses"],"by":"By Mika Tsuneda","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":185,"m":10,"d":6,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Diesel up 14 percent at the harbor, shipping houses blame the desert and the desert blames the shipping houses","src":"{j} The price of diesel at the Caldera harbor is up 14 percent since the spring, shipping houses said on Monday, and the Diet has taken up a proposal to put a levy on fuel to protect the fishing fleets of the south coast.\n\nThe increase follows the Earth Union's single tariff on Si Wong oil, which took effect in January. Port authorities said Caldera imported 40 percent of its fuel from the Union last year.\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Trade, Oct. 6, 185 AG (abridged): \"The Ministry notes the rise in the price of diesel. It has no proposal to set prices. The Diet will decide whether a levy is warranted.\"\n\n## In the Diet\n\nDaichi Mori, speaking for the Reform bloc, said the levy \"would tax the fishermen who can least afford it in order to protect the shipowners who can most.\" A member of the Old Guard bloc, Ishikawa Reiko of Shu Jing, said the Union's tariff was \"a tax laid on us by a country that does not exist.\"\n\nThe levy was sent to committee, 190 to 130.\n\n## At the harbor\n\n\"We do not ask for a favor,\" said Takeo Sato, 52, who owns two boats at the south pier. \"We ask who sets the price of the thing we burn.\"\n\n//The Scire could not confirm the Ministry's 40 percent figure, which was supplied by the harbor authority.//"},"t":1793608035205},"xinan_dominion_says_desert_oil_fields_are_under_illegal_occupation_and_warns_the_earth_union":{"title":"Xinan Dominion says desert oil fields are under 'illegal occupation' and warns the Earth Union","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Xinan Dominion said on Tuesday that three oil fields in the Si Wong Desert are under &quot;illegal occupation&quot; by the Earth Union and that it &quot;reserves every lawful remedy.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The statement was read on the Dominion's state radio and reached Caldera City through the Concord's technical staff. It gave no date and no field names.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Foreign Affairs, May 11, 186 AG: &quot;The Ministry has noted the statement of the Dominion. It has also noted that the Union has not responded.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The fields\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Union says the three fields were developed by its own member states and have never belonged to anyone else. The Dominion's statement said the fields lay &quot;within the historic lands of the Xinan people&quot; and that their wells had been &quot;drilled under the occupier's flag.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Speaker Haddad Roukan said the Union would answer in the Concord. &quot;We will not answer a radio,&quot; he said.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What is Xinan?\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Little is known in Caldera about the Dominion, whose capital the Concord's staff list as &quot;not confirmed.&quot; Its envoy did not attend the Concord's spring assembly. The Navy's western observers declined to comment.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This article is based on the Dominion's broadcast, as transcribed by the Concord, and statements from the Ministry and the Union. The Scire could not confirm the Dominion's claim to the fields.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["A statement on state radio names no date for action; the Ministry of Foreign Affairs says it has \"noted\" it"],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":186,"m":5,"d":11,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"A statement on state radio names no date for action; the Ministry of Foreign Affairs says it has \"noted\" it","src":"{j} The Xinan Dominion said on Tuesday that three oil fields in the Si Wong Desert are under \"illegal occupation\" by the Earth Union and that it \"reserves every lawful remedy.\"\n\nThe statement was read on the Dominion's state radio and reached Caldera City through the Concord's technical staff. It gave no date and no field names.\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Foreign Affairs, May 11, 186 AG: \"The Ministry has noted the statement of the Dominion. It has also noted that the Union has not responded.\"\n\n## The fields\n\nThe Union says the three fields were developed by its own member states and have never belonged to anyone else. The Dominion's statement said the fields lay \"within the historic lands of the Xinan people\" and that their wells had been \"drilled under the occupier's flag.\"\n\nSpeaker Haddad Roukan said the Union would answer in the Concord. \"We will not answer a radio,\" he said.\n\n## What is Xinan?\n\nLittle is known in Caldera about the Dominion, whose capital the Concord's staff list as \"not confirmed.\" Its envoy did not attend the Concord's spring assembly. The Navy's western observers declined to comment.\n\n//This article is based on the Dominion's broadcast, as transcribed by the Concord, and statements from the Ministry and the Union. The Scire could not confirm the Dominion's claim to the fields.//"},"t":1793608036205},"earth_seat_at_the_concord_vacant_for_a_second_time_as_ba_sing_se_and_the_union_both_claim_it":{"title":"Earth seat at the Concord vacant for a second time as Ba Sing Se and the Union both claim it","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The chair marked &quot;Earth Kingdom&quot; stayed empty at the Concord of Nations on Saturday, as it did in 176 AG, after the Grand Council of Ba Sing Se and the Earth Union each sent a delegation to claim it and each refused to sit while the other did.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Protocol Office, which had hoped for a compromise, placed no one in it. It was the second time the seat has been left vacant since the charter of 176 AG.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Palace Protocol Office, March 28, 187 AG: &quot;The Earth seat remains vacant pending the agreement of the claimants. The Concord's business will proceed.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Two claims\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Grand Council's delegation, led by Councilor Yun Baoshan, said the Council governs the capital and the old kingdom's center and is &quot;the Earth Kingdom's only continuing state.&quot; The Union's, led by Speaker Haddad Roukan, said the Council &quot;governs a city and a rumor&quot; and that nine member states, an oil trade and a common court were more than that.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Neither could be seated without the other's walkout. &quot;In 176 AG, there was a chair and two claims, and a letter from the king,&quot; said Shen Lihua, Qianchan's Deputy Foreign Minister, who now chairs the Concord's seating committee. &quot;Now there is a chair and two states. We have not progressed, but we have more to argue about.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Fire Nation's vote\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Tajisaki abstained on a motion by the Southern Water Tribe to seat both on half-votes. The motion failed, 11 to 9, with 3 abstentions.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This report draws on the plenary record supplied by the Protocol Office and interviews with nine delegates.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Delegations from the Grand Council and the Earth Union each demanded the chair marked \"Earth Kingdom\"; the Protocol Office placed no one in it"],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":187,"m":3,"d":28,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Delegations from the Grand Council and the Earth Union each demanded the chair marked \"Earth Kingdom\"; the Protocol Office placed no one in it","src":"{j} The chair marked \"Earth Kingdom\" stayed empty at the Concord of Nations on Saturday, as it did in 176 AG, after the Grand Council of Ba Sing Se and the Earth Union each sent a delegation to claim it and each refused to sit while the other did.\n\nThe Protocol Office, which had hoped for a compromise, placed no one in it. It was the second time the seat has been left vacant since the charter of 176 AG.\n\n**Official statement.** Palace Protocol Office, March 28, 187 AG: \"The Earth seat remains vacant pending the agreement of the claimants. The Concord's business will proceed.\"\n\n## Two claims\n\nThe Grand Council's delegation, led by Councilor Yun Baoshan, said the Council governs the capital and the old kingdom's center and is \"the Earth Kingdom's only continuing state.\" The Union's, led by Speaker Haddad Roukan, said the Council \"governs a city and a rumor\" and that nine member states, an oil trade and a common court were more than that.\n\nNeither could be seated without the other's walkout. \"In 176 AG, there was a chair and two claims, and a letter from the king,\" said Shen Lihua, Qianchan's Deputy Foreign Minister, who now chairs the Concord's seating committee. \"Now there is a chair and two states. We have not progressed, but we have more to argue about.\"\n\n## The Fire Nation's vote\n\nTajisaki abstained on a motion by the Southern Water Tribe to seat both on half-votes. The motion failed, 11 to 9, with 3 abstentions.\n\n//This report draws on the plenary record supplied by the Protocol Office and interviews with nine delegates.//"},"t":1793608037205},"xinan_dominion_refuses_concord_inspectors_and_fire_lord_izumi_orders_the_western_radar_kept_on_watch":{"title":"Xinan Dominion refuses Concord inspectors and Fire Lord Izumi orders the western radar kept on watch","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Xinan Dominion has refused a request by the Concord's technical staff to inspect its reactors, and Fire Lord Izumi has ordered the radar stations on the western sea kept on continuous watch.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The request was made on Aug. 4 after the Concord's staff observed what its report called &quot;an unexplained draw on the Dominion's power grid.&quot; The Dominion replied on Aug. 15, saying it had &quot;nothing to show&quot; and that the Concord had &quot;no standing in a sovereign house.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Palace, Aug. 19, 188 AG (abridged): &quot;The Fire Lord has directed the Ministry of the Navy to keep the western radar stations on continuous watch. This is ordinary prudence and is not directed at any named state.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">In the Diet\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Daichi Mori asked whether the posture was &quot;ordinary prudence or a rehearsal.&quot; Minister Mori said the question would be answered &quot;in the proper session.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What the staff saw\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Concord's staff report, which the Scire has read, says the unexplained draw is equal to &quot;a medium city&quot; and began in the spring. The report did not say what the Dominion was using it for.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Scire could not confirm the report's findings independently. The Ministry of the Navy declined to comment on the radar posture.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Dominion says it has \"nothing to show\"; the Palace calls the posture \"ordinary prudence\""],"by":"By Mika Tsuneda","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":188,"m":8,"d":19,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Dominion says it has \"nothing to show\"; the Palace calls the posture \"ordinary prudence\"","src":"{j} The Xinan Dominion has refused a request by the Concord's technical staff to inspect its reactors, and Fire Lord Izumi has ordered the radar stations on the western sea kept on continuous watch.\n\nThe request was made on Aug. 4 after the Concord's staff observed what its report called \"an unexplained draw on the Dominion's power grid.\" The Dominion replied on Aug. 15, saying it had \"nothing to show\" and that the Concord had \"no standing in a sovereign house.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Palace, Aug. 19, 188 AG (abridged): \"The Fire Lord has directed the Ministry of the Navy to keep the western radar stations on continuous watch. This is ordinary prudence and is not directed at any named state.\"\n\n## In the Diet\n\nDaichi Mori asked whether the posture was \"ordinary prudence or a rehearsal.\" Minister Mori said the question would be answered \"in the proper session.\"\n\n## What the staff saw\n\nThe Concord's staff report, which the Scire has read, says the unexplained draw is equal to \"a medium city\" and began in the spring. The report did not say what the Dominion was using it for.\n\n//The Scire could not confirm the report's findings independently. The Ministry of the Navy declined to comment on the radar posture.//"},"t":1793608038205},"fire_navy_logs_record_light_and_surge_over_the_northern_pole_agna_qel_a_calls_it_atmospheric":{"title":"Fire Navy logs record 'light and surge' over the northern pole; Agna Qel'a calls it atmospheric","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Fire Navy observatories on the northern sea logged 37 minutes of unexplained light and magnetic disturbance over the northern pole on the night of Dec. 20, and the Northern Chief's office has called it &quot;an atmospheric event.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The disturbance began at 11:12 p.m., according to the logs, which were supplied by the Ministry of the Navy. Compass needles on two patrol ships turned for the length of it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Office of the Northern Chief, Dec. 21, 189 AG (abridged): &quot;A known and harmless atmospheric event was observed over the pole. There is no cause for concern. The Spirit Oasis was closed for a precaution agreed with our guests.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Spirit Oasis\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Residents of Agna Qel'a said armed men without insignia had cordoned the Spirit Oasis that night, and that the cordon was still in place on Tuesday. The Chief's office said the men were &quot;guests.&quot; The Scire could not establish who they were. Several residents called them the &quot;white guard.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Avatar\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Asked whether the Avatar, Korra, had been at the Oasis, the Chief's office said it &quot;did not discuss the Avatar's movements.&quot; The Avatar's office in Republic City did not reply.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This report is based on logs from the Ministry of the Navy, interviews with 12 residents of Agna Qel'a, and statements from the Chief's office. The Scire could not confirm the cause of the disturbance.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Thirty-seven minutes of light and a magnetic disturbance were logged on Dec. 20; men without insignia were seen at the Spirit Oasis"],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":189,"m":12,"d":22,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Thirty-seven minutes of light and a magnetic disturbance were logged on Dec. 20; men without insignia were seen at the Spirit Oasis","src":"{j} Fire Navy observatories on the northern sea logged 37 minutes of unexplained light and magnetic disturbance over the northern pole on the night of Dec. 20, and the Northern Chief's office has called it \"an atmospheric event.\"\n\nThe disturbance began at 11:12 p.m., according to the logs, which were supplied by the Ministry of the Navy. Compass needles on two patrol ships turned for the length of it.\n\n**Official statement.** Office of the Northern Chief, Dec. 21, 189 AG (abridged): \"A known and harmless atmospheric event was observed over the pole. There is no cause for concern. The Spirit Oasis was closed for a precaution agreed with our guests.\"\n\n## The Spirit Oasis\n\nResidents of Agna Qel'a said armed men without insignia had cordoned the Spirit Oasis that night, and that the cordon was still in place on Tuesday. The Chief's office said the men were \"guests.\" The Scire could not establish who they were. Several residents called them the \"white guard.\"\n\n## The Avatar\n\nAsked whether the Avatar, Korra, had been at the Oasis, the Chief's office said it \"did not discuss the Avatar's movements.\" The Avatar's office in Republic City did not reply.\n\n//This report is based on logs from the Ministry of the Navy, interviews with 12 residents of Agna Qel'a, and statements from the Chief's office. The Scire could not confirm the cause of the disturbance.//"},"t":1793608039205},"gz_grain_exchange":{"title":"Autumn Grain Exchange Opens With Prices Held Level","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>The autumn session of the Grain Exchange opened at the sixth hour with opening prices held level to those of the spring close, the Exchange Board announced.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Board’s registrar said the granaries of the river provinces were reported full by the district offices, and that no member had asked to withdraw lots from the floor.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Millers’ Association asked that payment terms be extended from thirty to forty-five days. The Board said it would hear the request on Monday.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"orn\">* * *\u003c/p>\u003cp>The session continues until the evening bell.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"gaozu","t":"report","sec":"Port & Trade","kick":"","deck":["Exchange Board Cites Full Granaries; Millers Ask for Longer Terms"],"by":"By Lin Shaoyu","cr":"","cty":"Gaoling","y":190,"m":10,"d":24,"time":"6:05 a.m.","sub":"Exchange Board Cites Full Granaries; Millers Ask for Longer Terms","src":"The autumn session of the Grain Exchange opened at the sixth hour with opening prices held level to those of the spring close, the Exchange Board announced.\n\nThe Board’s registrar said the granaries of the river provinces were reported full by the district offices, and that no member had asked to withdraw lots from the floor.\n\nThe Millers’ Association asked that payment terms be extended from thirty to forty-five days. The Board said it would hear the request on Monday.\n\n* * *\n\nThe session continues until the evening bell."},"t":1793608040205},"war_ends_with_kuvira_s_death_and_river_towns_pass_to_committees":{"title":"Empire’s river provinces pass to committees as its radio retracts word of Kuvira’s death","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The war in which Tajisaki has fought beside Qianchan and the Republic of Nations against the Earth Empire since Dec. 25, 180 AG has not ended, but the Empire’s grip on the river provinces has gone, and the river towns it left behind are being run by committees of men and women who give out grain.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Empire’s radio announced Kuvira’s death on Sept. 4 and withdrew the statement the next day. The Empire’s garrisons in the river provinces have since withdrawn or deserted, and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs said on Monday that it &quot;does not recognize any authority in the Earth lands as the successor of the Empire.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Oct. 1, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Kingdom of Tajisaki has taken note of the loss of authority in the river provinces of the Earth Empire. It has no instructions to recognize any committee, council or person as the government of the Earth lands.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The committees\u003c/h3>\u003cp>In at least nine river towns, committees have opened grain stores that the Empire had closed. In Qiaotou, where a ferry was bombed in December 180 and 47 people were killed, a committee held a public trial on Sept. 19 of three Imperial officials accused of informing, and two were executed the same day. The Scire could not confirm the number of trials.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;They fed us when the Empire took the grain,&quot; said Lan Meirong, 44, a boatman in Qiaotou. &quot;I do not ask them for more than that. I do not ask what happens to the next man who is tried.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">In the Diet\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Daichi Mori, speaking for the Reform bloc, asked whether the committees were &quot;the same people who ran the First Civil War, in new coats.&quot; The Minister said Tajisaki would be &quot;watching, not choosing.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This article is based on interviews with 19 people in four river towns, and a statement from the Ministry. The Scire could not confirm the committees' size or leadership.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Empire is gone, the Republic is quiet, and the river towns are fed by committees whose leaders nobody can name"],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":182,"m":10,"d":1,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Empire is gone, the Republic is quiet, and the river towns are fed by committees whose leaders nobody can name","src":"{j} The war in which Tajisaki has fought beside Qianchan and the Republic of Nations against the Earth Empire since Dec. 25, 180 AG has not ended, but the Empire’s grip on the river provinces has gone, and the river towns it left behind are being run by committees of men and women who give out grain.\n\nThe Empire’s radio announced Kuvira’s death on Sept. 4 and withdrew the statement the next day. The Empire’s garrisons in the river provinces have since withdrawn or deserted, and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs said on Monday that it \"does not recognize any authority in the Earth lands as the successor of the Empire.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Oct. 1, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Kingdom of Tajisaki has taken note of the loss of authority in the river provinces of the Earth Empire. It has no instructions to recognize any committee, council or person as the government of the Earth lands.\"\n\n## The committees\n\nIn at least nine river towns, committees have opened grain stores that the Empire had closed. In Qiaotou, where a ferry was bombed in December 180 and 47 people were killed, a committee held a public trial on Sept. 19 of three Imperial officials accused of informing, and two were executed the same day. The Scire could not confirm the number of trials.\n\n\"They fed us when the Empire took the grain,\" said Lan Meirong, 44, a boatman in Qiaotou. \"I do not ask them for more than that. I do not ask what happens to the next man who is tried.\"\n\n## In the Diet\n\nDaichi Mori, speaking for the Reform bloc, asked whether the committees were \"the same people who ran the First Civil War, in new coats.\" The Minister said Tajisaki would be \"watching, not choosing.\"\n\n//This article is based on interviews with 19 people in four river towns, and a statement from the Ministry. The Scire could not confirm the committees' size or leadership.//"},"t":1793685480215},"imperial_ministry_says_republic_attacked_xilan_and_cape_hengshan_the_empire_is_at_war":{"title":"Imperial Ministry says Republic attacked Xilan and Cape Hengshan; the Empire is at war","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Imperial Ministry of Defense announced at 9 p.m. yesterday that the Republic of Nations had attacked the naval base at Xilan on Nov. 30 and the air base at Cape Hengshan on Dec. 1, and that the Empire &quot;considers itself at war.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Times has not been permitted to send a correspondent to Xilan or to Cape Hengshan. It prints the Ministry's account below as the Ministry's, and has no second source for any of it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Imperial Ministry of Defense, Dec. 2, 180 AG (abridged): &quot;The Empire's western coast has been attacked without warning by the Republic of Nations. The Empire will answer. The people are asked to remain calm and to expect no other news than that issued by this Ministry.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What the Ministry says\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Ministry said the Republic's forces had destroyed &quot;military installations on the western coast&quot; and killed &quot;a number of loyal servants of the Empress.&quot; It gave no number. The Times asked the Ministry for a casualty figure at 10:15 p.m. and was told that none would be published.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What the Times has not been able to establish\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Two foreign newspapers reported that the explosions at Xilan were heard across the bay, and one gave a figure of 800 dead. The Times cannot confirm it. It has no named source for the cause of the explosions at either base. The Ministry did not say whether any aircraft of the Republic were seen over either place.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Under the new Press Order of Dec. 1, newspapers of the Empire may print only communiqués of the Ministry of Information on matters of war. The Order does not say what happens to a paper that prints more.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Times was filed at 11:40 p.m. It will print the next Ministry communiqué as received.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"bst","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Times has not been permitted to send correspondents to either base; the figures below are the Ministry’s"],"by":"By the Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"BA SING SE","y":180,"m":12,"d":2,"time":"11:40 p.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Times has not been permitted to send correspondents to either base; the figures below are the Ministry’s","src":"{j} The Imperial Ministry of Defense announced at 9 p.m. yesterday that the Republic of Nations had attacked the naval base at Xilan on Nov. 30 and the air base at Cape Hengshan on Dec. 1, and that the Empire \"considers itself at war.\"\n\nThe Times has not been permitted to send a correspondent to Xilan or to Cape Hengshan. It prints the Ministry's account below as the Ministry's, and has no second source for any of it.\n\n**Official statement.** Imperial Ministry of Defense, Dec. 2, 180 AG (abridged): \"The Empire's western coast has been attacked without warning by the Republic of Nations. The Empire will answer. The people are asked to remain calm and to expect no other news than that issued by this Ministry.\"\n\n## What the Ministry says\n\nThe Ministry said the Republic's forces had destroyed \"military installations on the western coast\" and killed \"a number of loyal servants of the Empress.\" It gave no number. The Times asked the Ministry for a casualty figure at 10:15 p.m. and was told that none would be published.\n\n## What the Times has not been able to establish\n\nTwo foreign newspapers reported that the explosions at Xilan were heard across the bay, and one gave a figure of 800 dead. The Times cannot confirm it. It has no named source for the cause of the explosions at either base. The Ministry did not say whether any aircraft of the Republic were seen over either place.\n\nUnder the new Press Order of Dec. 1, newspapers of the Empire may print only communiqués of the Ministry of Information on matters of war. The Order does not say what happens to a paper that prints more.\n\n//The Times was filed at 11:40 p.m. It will print the next Ministry communiqué as received.//"},"t":1793702866882},"ministry_says_qianchan_ambushed_an_imperial_column_at_the_zaofu_omashu_border_strikes_follow":{"title":"Ministry says Qianchan ambushed an Imperial column at the Zaofu–Omashu border; strikes follow","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Imperial Ministry of Defense said last night that Qianchan's Zaofu Iron Guard had ambushed an Imperial supply column at the Zaofu–Omashu border on Dec. 23, killing 19, and that Imperial aircraft and artillery had &quot;answered&quot; on the eastern coast of Qianchan on Dec. 24 and at the border on Dec. 25.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Times has no named source for the ambush, for the strikes, or for the Ministry's description of them.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Imperial Ministry of Defense, Dec. 25, 180 AG (abridged): &quot;The Republic of Qianchan has joined the aggression of the Republic of Nations. Its Iron Guard has killed soldiers of the Empire on the Empire's own road. The Empire's reply is lawful and proportionate.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What is known\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Times has confirmed from a named customs official in Huamu that the Imperial garrisons at the border were reinforced on Dec. 22, a day before the ambush. The official declined to say why.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Times has not been able to confirm that the Iron Guard, which fields Tiejia-III armor, was at the border on Dec. 23. The Government of Qianchan has not responded to a request for comment.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Press\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Press Order of Dec. 1 remains in force. This report was filed from Ba Sing Se and was submitted to the Ministry of Information before printing, as the Order requires.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Times could not send a correspondent to the border or to the eastern coast of Qianchan.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"bst","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Empire says it answered on Dec. 24 and 25; the Times has no source on the ground"],"by":"By the Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"BA SING SE","y":180,"m":12,"d":25,"time":"10:55 p.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Empire says it answered on Dec. 24 and 25; the Times has no source on the ground","src":"{j} The Imperial Ministry of Defense said last night that Qianchan's Zaofu Iron Guard had ambushed an Imperial supply column at the Zaofu–Omashu border on Dec. 23, killing 19, and that Imperial aircraft and artillery had \"answered\" on the eastern coast of Qianchan on Dec. 24 and at the border on Dec. 25.\n\nThe Times has no named source for the ambush, for the strikes, or for the Ministry's description of them.\n\n**Official statement.** Imperial Ministry of Defense, Dec. 25, 180 AG (abridged): \"The Republic of Qianchan has joined the aggression of the Republic of Nations. Its Iron Guard has killed soldiers of the Empire on the Empire's own road. The Empire's reply is lawful and proportionate.\"\n\n## What is known\n\nThe Times has confirmed from a named customs official in Huamu that the Imperial garrisons at the border were reinforced on Dec. 22, a day before the ambush. The official declined to say why.\n\nThe Times has not been able to confirm that the Iron Guard, which fields Tiejia-III armor, was at the border on Dec. 23. The Government of Qianchan has not responded to a request for comment.\n\n## Press\n\nThe Press Order of Dec. 1 remains in force. This report was filed from Ba Sing Se and was submitted to the Ministry of Information before printing, as the Order requires.\n\n//The Times could not send a correspondent to the border or to the eastern coast of Qianchan.//"},"t":1793702867882},"bombs_fall_on_the_western_ports_for_a_second_day_the_empire_says_it_will_answer_the_republic":{"title":"Bombs fall on the western ports for a second day; the Empire says it will \"answer\" the Republic","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Imperial aircraft bombed the western ports of Hanyuan and Dongpu for a second day yesterday, and guns across the gulf shelled the quays, in the heaviest attack on the Republic since the founding.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Ministry of Defense said at least 600 people had been killed by last evening. Hospitals in Hanyuan said they had counted 410 dead in their wards and the streets around them. The Daily could not reconcile the two figures.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Defense, Dec. 3, 180 AG (abridged): &quot;The Republic has been attacked without a declaration. Its air defense on the western coast is thin. It is being strengthened tonight.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The sky\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Republic has few interceptors on the western coast. Pilots at Hanyuan said the Imperial aircraft flew in three waves at about 8,000 meters and could not be reached. &quot;We saw them. We could not touch them,&quot; said Lan Qiu, 29, who flies a fighter based at Hanyuan.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Empire's account\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Empire said on Dec. 2 that the Republic had attacked its bases at Xilan and Cape Hengshan on Nov. 30 and Dec. 1. The Ministry of Defense of the Republic said it had no forces within 200 kilometers of either place and had given no such order. &quot;We did not do this,&quot; said the Minister. &quot;Someone did, and wants us to have done it.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Congress\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Congress is to meet at noon tomorrow. The President has asked both chambers to sit in joint session.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This article is based on figures supplied by the Ministry of Defense and by Hanyuan hospitals, and on interviews with 11 people in the two ports. The Daily could not confirm the Empire's account of the attacks on its bases.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"News","kick":"","deck":["Hanyuan and Dongpu are on fire; the Ministry of Defense says at least 600 are dead and the number will rise"],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"HANYUAN","y":180,"m":12,"d":3,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Hanyuan and Dongpu are on fire; the Ministry of Defense says at least 600 are dead and the number will rise","src":"{j} Imperial aircraft bombed the western ports of Hanyuan and Dongpu for a second day yesterday, and guns across the gulf shelled the quays, in the heaviest attack on the Republic since the founding.\n\nThe Ministry of Defense said at least 600 people had been killed by last evening. Hospitals in Hanyuan said they had counted 410 dead in their wards and the streets around them. The Daily could not reconcile the two figures.\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Defense, Dec. 3, 180 AG (abridged): \"The Republic has been attacked without a declaration. Its air defense on the western coast is thin. It is being strengthened tonight.\"\n\n## The sky\n\nThe Republic has few interceptors on the western coast. Pilots at Hanyuan said the Imperial aircraft flew in three waves at about 8,000 meters and could not be reached. \"We saw them. We could not touch them,\" said Lan Qiu, 29, who flies a fighter based at Hanyuan.\n\n## The Empire's account\n\nThe Empire said on Dec. 2 that the Republic had attacked its bases at Xilan and Cape Hengshan on Nov. 30 and Dec. 1. The Ministry of Defense of the Republic said it had no forces within 200 kilometers of either place and had given no such order. \"We did not do this,\" said the Minister. \"Someone did, and wants us to have done it.\"\n\n## Congress\n\nThe Congress is to meet at noon tomorrow. The President has asked both chambers to sit in joint session.\n\n//This article is based on figures supplied by the Ministry of Defense and by Hanyuan hospitals, and on interviews with 11 people in the two ports. The Daily could not confirm the Empire's account of the attacks on its bases.//"},"t":1793702868882},"congress_declares_war_on_the_earth_empire":{"title":"Congress declares war on the Earth Empire","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Congress of the United Republic of Nations declared war on the Earth Empire at 4:10 p.m. yesterday, after a third day of bombing and shelling of the western coast.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The House of Representatives voted 244 to 41. The High Council voted by more than the two-thirds the Charter requires. The vote followed a joint session called by the President.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Office of the President, Dec. 4, 180 AG (abridged): &quot;The Republic did not choose this war. It will fight it. Every citizen who has completed national service is a member of the militia of the Republic, and the militia is called.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The debate\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Members from the western regions asked for aircraft and air defense, not speeches. &quot;I do not want a declaration. I want fighters over Hanyuan,&quot; said Pei Anshan, 51, a Representative from the western coast.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Four members of the High Council voted for the declaration and spoke in favor of it at length. A House member said afterward that the debate had been &quot;unusually short for a vote of such weight.&quot; The Daily could not confirm how many members had asked to speak.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The militia\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Ministry of National Service said 1.2 million citizens in active reserve status had been called, under Article 52. The Daily could not confirm that figure.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This article is based on the Congress's record of the vote and interviews with nine members.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["The House voted 244 to 41 and the High Council by more than two-thirds, after the third day of bombing"],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":180,"m":12,"d":4,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The House voted 244 to 41 and the High Council by more than two-thirds, after the third day of bombing","src":"{j} The Congress of the United Republic of Nations declared war on the Earth Empire at 4:10 p.m. yesterday, after a third day of bombing and shelling of the western coast.\n\nThe House of Representatives voted 244 to 41. The High Council voted by more than the two-thirds the Charter requires. The vote followed a joint session called by the President.\n\n**Official statement.** Office of the President, Dec. 4, 180 AG (abridged): \"The Republic did not choose this war. It will fight it. Every citizen who has completed national service is a member of the militia of the Republic, and the militia is called.\"\n\n## The debate\n\nMembers from the western regions asked for aircraft and air defense, not speeches. \"I do not want a declaration. I want fighters over Hanyuan,\" said Pei Anshan, 51, a Representative from the western coast.\n\nFour members of the High Council voted for the declaration and spoke in favor of it at length. A House member said afterward that the debate had been \"unusually short for a vote of such weight.\" The Daily could not confirm how many members had asked to speak.\n\n## The militia\n\nThe Ministry of National Service said 1.2 million citizens in active reserve status had been called, under Article 52. The Daily could not confirm that figure.\n\n//This article is based on the Congress's record of the vote and interviews with nine members.//"},"t":1793702869882},"defector_names_republic_officers_who_handled_the_false_flags_joint_inquiry_opens":{"title":"Defector names Republic officers who handled the false flags; Joint Inquiry opens","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">An Imperial intelligence officer who crossed the lines last month has given the Republic's Ministry of Internal Affairs records naming Republic officers who handled the incidents of the war's first months, and the Congress voted Tuesday to open a Joint Inquiry.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Ministry said the records name &quot;a number of persons in the armed forces and in the civil service.&quot; It did not say how many. It said it would not name them &quot;until the Inquiry has heard them.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Internal Affairs, Oct. 8, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;The Ministry has received material from a source in the Empire's intelligence service. The material is being authenticated. It indicates that some of the incidents of Dec. 180 to March 181 were not what they appeared to be.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Inquiry\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Joint Inquiry into the Penetration of the Republic's Institutions will be made up of members of both chambers. Its chair will be chosen by lot. &quot;It must not be a body that can be led,&quot; said a member of the House.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Which incidents\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Ministry said the material concerns the bombing of the Qiaotou ferry, the shooting at Mohe, and the shelling of the hospital ship Gongping at Port Hanyuan. The Daily asked whether it concerned the attack on the Tianhe road convoy. The Ministry said it &quot;could not say.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily could not see the records, or confirm the identity of the officer.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["An Imperial intelligence officer crossed the lines with records; the Congress forms a Joint Inquiry of both chambers"],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":181,"m":10,"d":8,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"An Imperial intelligence officer crossed the lines with records; the Congress forms a Joint Inquiry of both chambers","src":"{j} An Imperial intelligence officer who crossed the lines last month has given the Republic's Ministry of Internal Affairs records naming Republic officers who handled the incidents of the war's first months, and the Congress voted Tuesday to open a Joint Inquiry.\n\nThe Ministry said the records name \"a number of persons in the armed forces and in the civil service.\" It did not say how many. It said it would not name them \"until the Inquiry has heard them.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Internal Affairs, Oct. 8, 181 AG (abridged): \"The Ministry has received material from a source in the Empire's intelligence service. The material is being authenticated. It indicates that some of the incidents of Dec. 180 to March 181 were not what they appeared to be.\"\n\n## The Inquiry\n\nThe Joint Inquiry into the Penetration of the Republic's Institutions will be made up of members of both chambers. Its chair will be chosen by lot. \"It must not be a body that can be led,\" said a member of the House.\n\n## Which incidents\n\nThe Ministry said the material concerns the bombing of the Qiaotou ferry, the shooting at Mohe, and the shelling of the hospital ship Gongping at Port Hanyuan. The Daily asked whether it concerned the attack on the Tianhe road convoy. The Ministry said it \"could not say.\"\n\n//The Daily could not see the records, or confirm the identity of the officer.//"},"t":1793702870882},"inquiry_names_four_high_councillors_and_a_quarter_of_the_house_as_agents_of_the_empire":{"title":"Inquiry names four High Councillors and a quarter of the House as agents of the Empire","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Joint Inquiry said Friday that four members of the High Council and about a quarter of the House of Representatives, roughly 75 of 300 members, were working for the Earth Empire, and that the Empire's records show agents in the army staff, in supply and in the shipyards.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The records are those of the Empire's intelligence service, seized in the autumn, when committees overran Imperial offices in the river provinces. They add to the material the defector brought last year.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Joint Inquiry, Nov. 20, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Inquiry finds that the incidents of Dec. 180 to March 181 were in part arranged by officers and members of the Republic acting for the Empire, and in part by agents of the Empire. The Inquiry will publish the names after each person has been heard.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What the Congress cannot do\u003c/h3>\u003cp>With four seats of the High Council tainted, the Council cannot raise the two-thirds it needs to ratify a treaty, to confirm a minister, or to try an impeachment. A quarter of the House is under inquiry. &quot;The Charter we wrote needs majorities that we no longer have,&quot; said the Speaker of the House.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Mobilization Act\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Inquiry said the members who pressed hardest for the Mobilization Act of March 181 were among those named. It said the Act itself had been &quot;steered, not forced.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily has not seen the names. The numbers are the Inquiry's.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["The Empire’s archives, seized after the death of Kuvira, list paid informants in both chambers, the army staff and the shipyards"],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":182,"m":11,"d":20,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Empire’s archives, seized after the death of Kuvira, list paid informants in both chambers, the army staff and the shipyards","src":"{j} The Joint Inquiry said Friday that four members of the High Council and about a quarter of the House of Representatives, roughly 75 of 300 members, were working for the Earth Empire, and that the Empire's records show agents in the army staff, in supply and in the shipyards.\n\nThe records are those of the Empire's intelligence service, seized in the autumn, when committees overran Imperial offices in the river provinces. They add to the material the defector brought last year.\n\n**Official statement.** Joint Inquiry, Nov. 20, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Inquiry finds that the incidents of Dec. 180 to March 181 were in part arranged by officers and members of the Republic acting for the Empire, and in part by agents of the Empire. The Inquiry will publish the names after each person has been heard.\"\n\n## What the Congress cannot do\n\nWith four seats of the High Council tainted, the Council cannot raise the two-thirds it needs to ratify a treaty, to confirm a minister, or to try an impeachment. A quarter of the House is under inquiry. \"The Charter we wrote needs majorities that we no longer have,\" said the Speaker of the House.\n\n## The Mobilization Act\n\nThe Inquiry said the members who pressed hardest for the Mobilization Act of March 181 were among those named. It said the Act itself had been \"steered, not forced.\"\n\n//The Daily has not seen the names. The numbers are the Inquiry's.//"},"t":1793702871882},"congress_passes_the_second_constitutional_reform_act_high_council_and_house_to_be_dissolved":{"title":"Congress passes the Second Constitutional Reform Act; High Council and House to be dissolved","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Congress voted last night to dissolve itself under the Second Constitutional Reform Act, called &quot;the Restoration&quot; in the corridors, which returns the Republic to one chamber, the National Assembly, and adds a Constitutional Court and a vetting commission.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Act passed the House 190 to 22, with 70 members not voting, and the High Council by the two-thirds required, with the four named Councillors absent. It must be confirmed by referendum.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Office of the President, Dec. 15, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Republic has learned what two chambers can be made to do. It will have one, and it will have it checked.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What changes\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The six Founding Seats of the High Council become six designated blocs inside the Assembly: Fire, Earth, Water, Air and two Non-Bender. The Speaker will rotate yearly among the blocs. The Assembly impeaches, and the new Constitutional Court tries. A vetting commission will check members of the Assembly, ministers and senior officers.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What stays\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Presidency stays, with a Federal Cabinet whose ministers the Assembly confirms. Rights, the right to bear arms and national service are unchanged and cannot be amended.\u003c/p>\u003cp>&quot;We took the Council's six seats and the Reform's president and left the rest,&quot; said a member of the House who voted for the Act. &quot;I would not call it elegant.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This article is based on the Congress's record of the vote and interviews with 14 members.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["A single National Assembly will return, with six founding blocs, a Constitutional Court and a vetting commission; the Presidency stays"],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"YU DAO","y":182,"m":12,"d":15,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"A single National Assembly will return, with six founding blocs, a Constitutional Court and a vetting commission; the Presidency stays","src":"{j} The Congress voted last night to dissolve itself under the Second Constitutional Reform Act, called \"the Restoration\" in the corridors, which returns the Republic to one chamber, the National Assembly, and adds a Constitutional Court and a vetting commission.\n\nThe Act passed the House 190 to 22, with 70 members not voting, and the High Council by the two-thirds required, with the four named Councillors absent. It must be confirmed by referendum.\n\n**Official statement.** Office of the President, Dec. 15, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Republic has learned what two chambers can be made to do. It will have one, and it will have it checked.\"\n\n## What changes\n\nThe six Founding Seats of the High Council become six designated blocs inside the Assembly: Fire, Earth, Water, Air and two Non-Bender. The Speaker will rotate yearly among the blocs. The Assembly impeaches, and the new Constitutional Court tries. A vetting commission will check members of the Assembly, ministers and senior officers.\n\n## What stays\n\nThe Presidency stays, with a Federal Cabinet whose ministers the Assembly confirms. Rights, the right to bear arms and national service are unchanged and cannot be amended.\n\n\"We took the Council's six seats and the Reform's president and left the rest,\" said a member of the House who voted for the Act. \"I would not call it elegant.\"\n\n//This article is based on the Congress's record of the vote and interviews with 14 members.//"},"t":1793702872882},"tajisaki_declares_war_on_the_earth_empire_after_strikes_on_qianchan":{"title":"Tajisaki declares war on the Earth Empire after strikes on Qianchan","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Tajisaki declared war on the Earth Empire at 6 p.m. yesterday, hours after Qianchan did, and the Diet voted its support, 391 to 38.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Fire Lord Izumi addressed a joint sitting of both Houses. &quot;The Empire has struck our ally's coast and our ally's border, and it has lied about why,&quot; she said, according to the transcript. &quot;We do not enter this war to be a province's conqueror. We enter it so that the Empire cannot be.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Palace, Dec. 25, 180 AG (printed in full): &quot;The Kingdom of Tajisaki is at war with the Earth Empire. The Fire Lord has directed the Ministry of the Navy and the Ministry of War to act with the forces of Qianchan and the Republic of Nations.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Diet\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Foreign Minister Hayato Mori said Tajisaki had been asked by Qianchan to join, and had been in contact with the Republic of Nations since Dec. 3. Daichi Mori, speaking for the Reform bloc, voted yes and asked for &quot;a plain account of what the Fire Navy can do against aircraft that fly above its guns.&quot; The Minister said the question would be answered in closed session.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Ishikawa Reiko of Shu Jing, for the Old Guard bloc, said Tajisaki's quarrel was with the Empire's government, not with the Earth lands. &quot;We should say so loudly,&quot; she said.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Concord\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Concord of Nations did not meet. A staff officer said no member had asked for an emergency session, &quot;because one side's members would be sitting on both.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This report is based on the Diet transcript and a statement from the Palace. The Scire could not confirm the size of the forces to be sent.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Fire Lord Izumi told the Diet the Empire \"has chosen\"; Foreign Minister Mori says Tajisaki acts with Qianchan and the Republic"],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":180,"m":12,"d":25,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Fire Lord Izumi told the Diet the Empire \"has chosen\"; Foreign Minister Mori says Tajisaki acts with Qianchan and the Republic","src":"{j} Tajisaki declared war on the Earth Empire at 6 p.m. yesterday, hours after Qianchan did, and the Diet voted its support, 391 to 38.\n\nFire Lord Izumi addressed a joint sitting of both Houses. \"The Empire has struck our ally's coast and our ally's border, and it has lied about why,\" she said, according to the transcript. \"We do not enter this war to be a province's conqueror. We enter it so that the Empire cannot be.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Palace, Dec. 25, 180 AG (printed in full): \"The Kingdom of Tajisaki is at war with the Earth Empire. The Fire Lord has directed the Ministry of the Navy and the Ministry of War to act with the forces of Qianchan and the Republic of Nations.\"\n\n## The Diet\n\nForeign Minister Hayato Mori said Tajisaki had been asked by Qianchan to join, and had been in contact with the Republic of Nations since Dec. 3. Daichi Mori, speaking for the Reform bloc, voted yes and asked for \"a plain account of what the Fire Navy can do against aircraft that fly above its guns.\" The Minister said the question would be answered in closed session.\n\nIshikawa Reiko of Shu Jing, for the Old Guard bloc, said Tajisaki's quarrel was with the Empire's government, not with the Earth lands. \"We should say so loudly,\" she said.\n\n## The Concord\n\nThe Concord of Nations did not meet. A staff officer said no member had asked for an emergency session, \"because one side's members would be sitting on both.\"\n\n//This report is based on the Diet transcript and a statement from the Palace. The Scire could not confirm the size of the forces to be sent.//"},"t":1793702873882},"empire_still_holds_the_sky_over_the_gulf_navy_says_it_lost_31_aircraft_in_the_first_3_weeks":{"title":"Empire still holds the sky over the gulf; Navy says it lost 31 aircraft in the first 3 weeks","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Empire still controls the sky over the gulf and the western coast, the Ministry of the Navy told the Diet on Tuesday, and the Fire Navy has lost 31 aircraft and two ships in three weeks of fighting.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Ministry said the Imperial air arm had a bomber that flies higher and faster than anything Tajisaki can send against it. The Concord's technical staff call it the B70. The Ministry confirmed the name and nothing else.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of the Navy, Jan. 14, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;The Navy's carrier groups have contested the gulf. The Navy has lost aircraft and two ships. The Navy has not lost the sea.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The second weapon\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Daichi Mori asked about &quot;a weapon of the Empire's that the newspapers of Qianchan call the CL1201.&quot; The Minister said it was &quot;a matter for the closed session.&quot; Mr. Mori said the Diet had a right to know &quot;what we are fighting.&quot; The Speaker ruled the question out of order.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What the Navy wants\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Ministry has asked the Diet for funds for 200 interceptors and for a radar line along the western sea. The Diet is to vote Friday.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Scire asked the Ministry for the range of the B70. It had not replied by the time this article went to press.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Ministry names a new Imperial bomber the Concord’s staff calls the B70; it will not describe a second weapon the Empire is said to be using"],"by":"By Mika Tsuneda","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":181,"m":1,"d":14,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Ministry names a new Imperial bomber the Concord’s staff calls the B70; it will not describe a second weapon the Empire is said to be using","src":"{j} The Empire still controls the sky over the gulf and the western coast, the Ministry of the Navy told the Diet on Tuesday, and the Fire Navy has lost 31 aircraft and two ships in three weeks of fighting.\n\nThe Ministry said the Imperial air arm had a bomber that flies higher and faster than anything Tajisaki can send against it. The Concord's technical staff call it the B70. The Ministry confirmed the name and nothing else.\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of the Navy, Jan. 14, 181 AG (abridged): \"The Navy's carrier groups have contested the gulf. The Navy has lost aircraft and two ships. The Navy has not lost the sea.\"\n\n## The second weapon\n\nDaichi Mori asked about \"a weapon of the Empire's that the newspapers of Qianchan call the CL1201.\" The Minister said it was \"a matter for the closed session.\" Mr. Mori said the Diet had a right to know \"what we are fighting.\" The Speaker ruled the question out of order.\n\n## What the Navy wants\n\nThe Ministry has asked the Diet for funds for 200 interceptors and for a radar line along the western sea. The Diet is to vote Friday.\n\n//The Scire asked the Ministry for the range of the B70. It had not replied by the time this article went to press.//"},"t":1793702874882},"republic_s_congress_dissolves_itself_palace_says_it_will_deal_with_whoever_the_republic_names":{"title":"Republic’s Congress dissolves itself; Palace says it \"will deal with whoever the Republic names\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Palace said on Tuesday that Tajisaki &quot;will deal with whoever the Republic names&quot; after its Congress voted to dissolve itself, and that the Restoration &quot;is the Republic's own business.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Foreign Minister Hayato Mori said the Ministry had been told in 181 AG that the false flags of the war's first months were not all the Empire's work. &quot;We did not know how many hands had been in our ally's house,&quot; he said. &quot;Now we know.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Dec. 16, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Kingdom of Tajisaki notes the Second Constitutional Reform Act of the Republic of Nations. It has no comment on the form of another state's constitution.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Diet\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Ishikawa Reiko asked whether Tajisaki's own officers were safe. &quot;A state that cannot say who in its government is loyal has no business lecturing a republic that has just counted,&quot; she said, according to the transcript. The Minister said the Ministry of Internal Affairs &quot;has no reason to open such an inquiry.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This report draws on the Diet transcript and a statement from the Ministry.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Tajisaki’s Foreign Ministry says the Restoration is an internal matter and notes that the Republic’s traitors were in its Congress"],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":182,"m":12,"d":16,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Tajisaki’s Foreign Ministry says the Restoration is an internal matter and notes that the Republic’s traitors were in its Congress","src":"{j} The Palace said on Tuesday that Tajisaki \"will deal with whoever the Republic names\" after its Congress voted to dissolve itself, and that the Restoration \"is the Republic's own business.\"\n\nForeign Minister Hayato Mori said the Ministry had been told in 181 AG that the false flags of the war's first months were not all the Empire's work. \"We did not know how many hands had been in our ally's house,\" he said. \"Now we know.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Dec. 16, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Kingdom of Tajisaki notes the Second Constitutional Reform Act of the Republic of Nations. It has no comment on the form of another state's constitution.\"\n\n## The Diet\n\nIshikawa Reiko asked whether Tajisaki's own officers were safe. \"A state that cannot say who in its government is loyal has no business lecturing a republic that has just counted,\" she said, according to the transcript. The Minister said the Ministry of Internal Affairs \"has no reason to open such an inquiry.\"\n\n//This report draws on the Diet transcript and a statement from the Ministry.//"},"t":1793702875882},"qianchan_at_war_143_dead_on_the_east_coast_31_at_the_zaofu_border_8_ports_shut":{"title":"Qianchan at war: 143 dead on the east coast, 31 at the Zaofu border, 8 ports shut","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Federal Defense Office counted 143 dead on the east coast of Qianchan and 31 at the Zaofu–Omashu border after the Empire's strikes on Dec. 24 and 25, and said 8 eastern ports had been closed to civilian shipping.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Qianchan declared war on the Earth Empire at noon on Dec. 25, by 198 votes to 14 in the House of Representatives and 21 to 3 in the Senate, the Federal Defense Office said.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Figures.\u003c/b> Federal Defense Office, Dec. 26, 180 AG: east coast dead, 143; border dead, 31; injured, 612; ports closed, 8; aircraft lost, 17. The Office noted that these figures are from the first day and &quot;will rise.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The east coast\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Imperial aircraft struck port installations and radar stations on Dec. 24. The Office said it had no warning of the raid. &quot;The first the east coast knew of the war was the sirens,&quot; said Mei Fang, 38, a harbor clerk at Tongyuan.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Zaofu border\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Zaofu Iron Guard held the pass at the Zaofu–Omashu border on Dec. 25. Suyin Beifong's office said it had &quot;never been at the place where the Empire says its column was ambushed.&quot; The Qianchan Report could not confirm either account.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The numbers that are not known\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Defense Office did not say how many aircraft the Empire had committed. It did not say how many of the 17 aircraft lost were shot down.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>This article is based on figures supplied by the Federal Defense Office and interviews with 12 people. The Qianchan Report could not confirm the figures independently.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"qr","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["The Federal Defense Office’s first count is below the Empire’s claims and above the President’s office’s estimate"],"by":"By the Politics Desk","cr":"","cty":"QIANCHAN","y":180,"m":12,"d":26,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Federal Defense Office’s first count is below the Empire’s claims and above the President’s office’s estimate","src":"{j} The Federal Defense Office counted 143 dead on the east coast of Qianchan and 31 at the Zaofu–Omashu border after the Empire's strikes on Dec. 24 and 25, and said 8 eastern ports had been closed to civilian shipping.\n\nQianchan declared war on the Earth Empire at noon on Dec. 25, by 198 votes to 14 in the House of Representatives and 21 to 3 in the Senate, the Federal Defense Office said.\n\n**Figures.** Federal Defense Office, Dec. 26, 180 AG: east coast dead, 143; border dead, 31; injured, 612; ports closed, 8; aircraft lost, 17. The Office noted that these figures are from the first day and \"will rise.\"\n\n## The east coast\n\nImperial aircraft struck port installations and radar stations on Dec. 24. The Office said it had no warning of the raid. \"The first the east coast knew of the war was the sirens,\" said Mei Fang, 38, a harbor clerk at Tongyuan.\n\n## The Zaofu border\n\nThe Zaofu Iron Guard held the pass at the Zaofu–Omashu border on Dec. 25. Suyin Beifong's office said it had \"never been at the place where the Empire says its column was ambushed.\" The Qianchan Report could not confirm either account.\n\n## The numbers that are not known\n\nThe Defense Office did not say how many aircraft the Empire had committed. It did not say how many of the 17 aircraft lost were shot down.\n\n//This article is based on figures supplied by the Federal Defense Office and interviews with 12 people. The Qianchan Report could not confirm the figures independently.//"},"t":1793702876882},"the_air_war_turns_loss_ratio_falls_from_5_to_1_to_1_4_to_1_in_four_weeks":{"title":"The air war turns: loss ratio falls from 5 to 1 to 1.4 to 1 in four weeks","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Empire's advantage in the air has fallen from five aircraft lost to the allies for each one of its own on Dec. 26 to 1.4 to one on Jan. 29, the Federal Defense Office said, as Qianchan's and Tajisaki's interceptors have come into service.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Figures.\u003c/b> Federal Defense Office, Jan. 30, 181 AG: Allied aircraft lost since Dec. 24, 212. Imperial aircraft lost, 148. Loss ratio in the last week, 1.4 to 1. Interceptors delivered to the western gulf, 96.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What changed\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Qianchan's interceptors, sent from the east coast, were joined on Jan. 11 by 60 from Tajisaki. The Defense Office said the allies had gained &quot;a working radar line&quot; on the 18th, which it said had cut the Empire's surprise in half.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The bomber and the weapon\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Empire's B70 bomber still flies. The Office said it had shot down two. It did not describe the weapon the newspapers call the CL1201, and said it would not. &quot;We will not name what we cannot yet counter,&quot; said the Office's spokesman.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What has not moved\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The ground lines have not moved. The Empire has made three probing attacks, at the Zaofu pass, at the south gate of the desert and on the north-east lowlands, and each has been thrown back. The Qianchan Report asked whether the Federal Council or the House had been told of the CL1201. It had not been given an answer.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Qianchan Report could not confirm the loss figures.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"qr","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["Qianchan and Tajisaki air arms have cut the Empire’s margin; the Empire’s heavy bomber and its “weapon” still fly"],"by":"By the Politics Desk","cr":"","cty":"QIANCHAN","y":181,"m":1,"d":30,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Qianchan and Tajisaki air arms have cut the Empire’s margin; the Empire’s heavy bomber and its “weapon” still fly","src":"{j} The Empire's advantage in the air has fallen from five aircraft lost to the allies for each one of its own on Dec. 26 to 1.4 to one on Jan. 29, the Federal Defense Office said, as Qianchan's and Tajisaki's interceptors have come into service.\n\n**Figures.** Federal Defense Office, Jan. 30, 181 AG: Allied aircraft lost since Dec. 24, 212. Imperial aircraft lost, 148. Loss ratio in the last week, 1.4 to 1. Interceptors delivered to the western gulf, 96.\n\n## What changed\n\nQianchan's interceptors, sent from the east coast, were joined on Jan. 11 by 60 from Tajisaki. The Defense Office said the allies had gained \"a working radar line\" on the 18th, which it said had cut the Empire's surprise in half.\n\n## The bomber and the weapon\n\nThe Empire's B70 bomber still flies. The Office said it had shot down two. It did not describe the weapon the newspapers call the CL1201, and said it would not. \"We will not name what we cannot yet counter,\" said the Office's spokesman.\n\n## What has not moved\n\nThe ground lines have not moved. The Empire has made three probing attacks, at the Zaofu pass, at the south gate of the desert and on the north-east lowlands, and each has been thrown back. The Qianchan Report asked whether the Federal Council or the House had been told of the CL1201. It had not been given an answer.\n\n//The Qianchan Report could not confirm the loss figures.//"},"t":1793702877882},"federal_defense_office_reports_engagements_at_the_zaofu_omashu_pass_iron_guard_holds":{"title":"Federal Defense Office reports engagements at the Zaofu–Omashu pass; Iron Guard holds","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>The Federal Defense Office reported on Dec. 26 that the Zaofu Iron Guard had held the Zaofu–Omashu pass on Dec. 25 against an Imperial attack, and that 31 persons had been killed at the border.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Office stated that no Qianchan force had been present at the place where the Empire's Ministry says an Imperial supply column was ambushed on Dec. 23. The Office stated that the Empire's account was &quot;untrue.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Federal Defense Office supplied the following figures: dead at the border, 31; dead on the east coast, 143; ports closed, 8.\u003c/p>\u003cp>* * *\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Office of the President stated that Qianchan was at war with the Earth Empire. The President's office stated that the Federal Council had been informed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Beifong Foundation stated that Zaofu's industrial plants were in full operation.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"gaozu","t":"report","sec":"Defense","kick":"","deck":["Thirty-one Killed; Pass Held; Office Says Imperial Column Was Not Ambushed by Qianchan"],"by":"By Lin Shaoyu","cr":"","cty":"Gaoling","y":180,"m":12,"d":26,"time":"9:20 a.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Thirty-one Killed; Pass Held; Office Says Imperial Column Was Not Ambushed by Qianchan","src":"The Federal Defense Office reported on Dec. 26 that the Zaofu Iron Guard had held the Zaofu–Omashu pass on Dec. 25 against an Imperial attack, and that 31 persons had been killed at the border.\n\nThe Office stated that no Qianchan force had been present at the place where the Empire's Ministry says an Imperial supply column was ambushed on Dec. 23. The Office stated that the Empire's account was \"untrue.\"\n\nThe Federal Defense Office supplied the following figures: dead at the border, 31; dead on the east coast, 143; ports closed, 8.\n\n* * *\n\nThe Office of the President stated that Qianchan was at war with the Earth Empire. The President's office stated that the Federal Council had been informed.\n\nThe Beifong Foundation stated that Zaofu's industrial plants were in full operation."},"t":1793702878882},"parliament_records_votes_for_war_federal_council_votes_21_to_3":{"title":"Parliament Records Votes for War; Federal Council Votes 21 to 3","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>The Office of the Speaker of Parliament published on Dec. 28 the record of the votes of Dec. 25 declaring war on the Earth Empire.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The House of Representatives voted 198 to 14. The Federal Council voted 21 to 3. The Office stated that the three dissenting Senators had asked that their reasons be entered in the record. The reasons were entered.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Office of the Speaker stated that the three Senators had objected that &quot;the Empire's account of the ambush has not been disproved, and the Parliament should say so before declaring war.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>* * *\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Federal Defense Office stated that the number of citizens called to service under Article V was 410,000. It stated that no figure for those already in service could be given.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"gaozu","t":"report","sec":"Parliament","kick":"","deck":["House Voted 198 to 14; Three Senators Dissent; Office of the Speaker Gives Figures"],"by":"By Lin Shaoyu","cr":"","cty":"Gaoling","y":180,"m":12,"d":28,"time":"8:00 a.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"House Voted 198 to 14; Three Senators Dissent; Office of the Speaker Gives Figures","src":"The Office of the Speaker of Parliament published on Dec. 28 the record of the votes of Dec. 25 declaring war on the Earth Empire.\n\nThe House of Representatives voted 198 to 14. The Federal Council voted 21 to 3. The Office stated that the three dissenting Senators had asked that their reasons be entered in the record. The reasons were entered.\n\nThe Office of the Speaker stated that the three Senators had objected that \"the Empire's account of the ambush has not been disproved, and the Parliament should say so before declaring war.\"\n\n* * *\n\nThe Federal Defense Office stated that the number of citizens called to service under Article V was 410,000. It stated that no figure for those already in service could be given."},"t":1793702879882},"night_of_no_sirens_empire_s_aircraft_strike_caldera_city_navy_says_gulf_squadron_sunk":{"title":"Night of No Sirens: Empire’s aircraft strike Caldera City; Navy says gulf squadron sunk","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Aircraft of the Earth Empire bombed Caldera City in the dark early yesterday and sank the Fire Navy's gulf squadron at dawn, and no siren sounded until the first bombs had fallen, the Ministry of the Navy said.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Ministry said four cruisers and nine destroyers were lost, with about 3,100 sailors. The city's Office of Public Safety counted about 640 civilians dead at the naval yards, the arsenal and the rail junction. The Palace district was not struck.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of the Navy, Feb. 3, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;The Empire's aircraft were not detected by the western radar line. They did not come from the west or the sea.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What the city saw\u003c/h3>\u003cp>&quot;There was a sound like tearing cloth very high up, and then the yards were burning,&quot; said Haruka Endo, 44, a ferry pilot. Observers on the hills saw no lights on the aircraft.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The fleet\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The squadron had sailed to cover the yards. It was struck at anchor off Cape Ryujin and again under way. &quot;We fired at the sound,&quot; said a gunnery officer who was picked up from the water. &quot;There was nothing to fire at.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Where from\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Ministry said it believed the aircraft had flown from the Si Wong Desert, and that a caravan guide had reported &quot;a shape on the sand the size of a harbor&quot; on Jan. 30. It would not say more.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Scire could not confirm the Ministry's casualty figures. It was not able to reach the yards.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Four cruisers and nine destroyers lost; no alarm sounded before the first bombs; Palace district untouched"],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":181,"m":2,"d":3,"time":"6:30 a.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Four cruisers and nine destroyers lost; no alarm sounded before the first bombs; Palace district untouched","src":"{j} Aircraft of the Earth Empire bombed Caldera City in the dark early yesterday and sank the Fire Navy's gulf squadron at dawn, and no siren sounded until the first bombs had fallen, the Ministry of the Navy said.\n\nThe Ministry said four cruisers and nine destroyers were lost, with about 3,100 sailors. The city's Office of Public Safety counted about 640 civilians dead at the naval yards, the arsenal and the rail junction. The Palace district was not struck.\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of the Navy, Feb. 3, 181 AG (abridged): \"The Empire's aircraft were not detected by the western radar line. They did not come from the west or the sea.\"\n\n## What the city saw\n\n\"There was a sound like tearing cloth very high up, and then the yards were burning,\" said Haruka Endo, 44, a ferry pilot. Observers on the hills saw no lights on the aircraft.\n\n## The fleet\n\nThe squadron had sailed to cover the yards. It was struck at anchor off Cape Ryujin and again under way. \"We fired at the sound,\" said a gunnery officer who was picked up from the water. \"There was nothing to fire at.\"\n\n## Where from\n\nThe Ministry said it believed the aircraft had flown from the Si Wong Desert, and that a caravan guide had reported \"a shape on the sand the size of a harbor\" on Jan. 30. It would not say more.\n\n//The Scire could not confirm the Ministry's casualty figures. It was not able to reach the yards.//"},"t":1793720824711},"diet_votes_emergency_funds_after_the_raid_mori_asks_about_the_flying_harbor":{"title":"Diet votes emergency funds after the raid; Mori asks about \"the flying harbor\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Diet voted 402 to 21 on Thursday for emergency funds to rebuild the gulf squadron and to buy 300 interceptors and a low radar net, two days after the raid the press has named the Night of No Sirens.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Minister of the Navy said the Concord's staff now believed the Empire had built the aircraft the Qianchan press calls the CL1201: a nuclear-powered flying wing about 340 meters across that carries fighters and bombers and can stay aloft for weeks. &quot;It has not been seen on any coast. It has been in the desert for weeks,&quot; he said, according to the transcript.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Palace, Feb. 5, 181 AG (printed in full): &quot;The Fire Lord has heard the Diet. The Navy will be rebuilt. The Palace will not be moved.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Diet\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Daichi Mori asked who in the government had known such a machine could be built. The Minister said &quot;no one with the authority to act.&quot; Ishikawa Reiko said the Palace district had been spared &quot;because the Empire wants us to know it could have been otherwise.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Speaker closed the session to the press after the vote.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Scire could not confirm the description of the CL1201 beyond the Minister's account.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Funds for 300 interceptors and a low radar net pass 402 to 21; Daichi Mori demands a closed-session answer on the CL1201"],"by":"By Mika Tsuneda","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":181,"m":2,"d":5,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Funds for 300 interceptors and a low radar net pass 402 to 21; Daichi Mori demands a closed-session answer on the CL1201","src":"{j} The Diet voted 402 to 21 on Thursday for emergency funds to rebuild the gulf squadron and to buy 300 interceptors and a low radar net, two days after the raid the press has named the Night of No Sirens.\n\nThe Minister of the Navy said the Concord's staff now believed the Empire had built the aircraft the Qianchan press calls the CL1201: a nuclear-powered flying wing about 340 meters across that carries fighters and bombers and can stay aloft for weeks. \"It has not been seen on any coast. It has been in the desert for weeks,\" he said, according to the transcript.\n\n**Official statement.** Palace, Feb. 5, 181 AG (printed in full): \"The Fire Lord has heard the Diet. The Navy will be rebuilt. The Palace will not be moved.\"\n\n## The Diet\n\nDaichi Mori asked who in the government had known such a machine could be built. The Minister said \"no one with the authority to act.\" Ishikawa Reiko said the Palace district had been spared \"because the Empire wants us to know it could have been otherwise.\"\n\nThe Speaker closed the session to the press after the vote.\n\n//The Scire could not confirm the description of the CL1201 beyond the Minister's account.//"},"t":1793720825711},"ministry_imperial_aircraft_struck_the_fire_nation_s_capital_and_fleet_with_no_loss":{"title":"Ministry: Imperial aircraft \"struck the Fire Nation’s capital and fleet with no loss\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Imperial Ministry of Defense said on Feb. 4 that aircraft of the Empire had struck &quot;the military installations of Caldera City and the Fire Navy's gulf squadron&quot; on the night of Feb. 2–3, and that every aircraft had returned.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Imperial Ministry of Defense, Feb. 4, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;The Empire's air arm, from the Tiancheng, has shown that the sky belongs to the Empire. The Fire Nation's fleet has been sunk. Its capital has been struck. The Empire's aircraft have returned without loss.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Times has been told by the Ministry of Information that the Tiancheng is &quot;the great work of the Empress's air service.&quot; It has not been told what it is, and has not seen it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Ministry of Information has asked that readers be reminded of the Press Order. The Times does so.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"bst","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Times prints the Ministry’s communiqué in full; no independent account is available"],"by":"By the Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"BA SING SE","y":181,"m":2,"d":4,"time":"10:45 p.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Times prints the Ministry’s communiqué in full; no independent account is available","src":"{j} The Imperial Ministry of Defense said on Feb. 4 that aircraft of the Empire had struck \"the military installations of Caldera City and the Fire Navy's gulf squadron\" on the night of Feb. 2–3, and that every aircraft had returned.\n\n**Official statement.** Imperial Ministry of Defense, Feb. 4, 181 AG (abridged): \"The Empire's air arm, from the Tiancheng, has shown that the sky belongs to the Empire. The Fire Nation's fleet has been sunk. Its capital has been struck. The Empire's aircraft have returned without loss.\"\n\nThe Times has been told by the Ministry of Information that the Tiancheng is \"the great work of the Empress's air service.\" It has not been told what it is, and has not seen it.\n\nThe Ministry of Information has asked that readers be reminded of the Press Order. The Times does so."},"t":1793720826711},"republic_s_air_arm_we_cannot_see_what_hit_caldera_ministry_of_defense_asks_congress_for_emergency_radar_funds":{"title":"Republic’s air arm: \"we cannot see what hit Caldera\"; Ministry of Defense asks Congress for emergency radar funds","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Republic's Ministry of Defense told the Congress on Wednesday that it could not detect the aircraft that struck Caldera City, and asked for emergency funds for sea-search radar and for long-range interceptors.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Minister said the Empire's two aircraft types, the high-flying B70 and the CL1201, &quot;defeat the Republic's air defense by different means.&quot; He said the CL1201 was &quot;a machine we had thought was a drawing,&quot; and that it carried its bombers to the target &quot;from a place we did not know to watch.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Defense, Feb. 4, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;The Republic's western coast is as open as Caldera's. The Ministry asks the Congress to act before it is tested.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Congress\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Pei Anshan said the Congress had &quot;voted for a war with no air defense.&quot; A High Council member who spoke in favor of the radar funds said the Republic &quot;must not be made to feel it can only wait.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily could not confirm the Ministry's account of the Empire's aircraft.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"News","kick":"","deck":["The Ministry says it cannot defend against an aircraft carrier that flies; an over-the-horizon sea radar is proposed"],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":181,"m":2,"d":4,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Ministry says it cannot defend against an aircraft carrier that flies; an over-the-horizon sea radar is proposed","src":"{j} The Republic's Ministry of Defense told the Congress on Wednesday that it could not detect the aircraft that struck Caldera City, and asked for emergency funds for sea-search radar and for long-range interceptors.\n\nThe Minister said the Empire's two aircraft types, the high-flying B70 and the CL1201, \"defeat the Republic's air defense by different means.\" He said the CL1201 was \"a machine we had thought was a drawing,\" and that it carried its bombers to the target \"from a place we did not know to watch.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Defense, Feb. 4, 181 AG (abridged): \"The Republic's western coast is as open as Caldera's. The Ministry asks the Congress to act before it is tested.\"\n\n## The Congress\n\nPei Anshan said the Congress had \"voted for a war with no air defense.\" A High Council member who spoke in favor of the radar funds said the Republic \"must not be made to feel it can only wait.\"\n\n//The Daily could not confirm the Ministry's account of the Empire's aircraft.//"},"t":1793720827711},"after_caldera_qianchan_asks_where_the_empire_s_aircraft_came_from_east_coast_patrols_doubled":{"title":"After Caldera: Qianchan asks where the Empire’s aircraft came from; east-coast patrols doubled","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Federal Defense Office said Thursday it had doubled sea patrols and desert reconnaissance off Qianchan's east coast and had no explanation for how the Empire's aircraft crossed Tajisaki's line unseen, as the Federal Council asked for a briefing on what Zaofu's laboratories had learned of the CL1201.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Office said the strike on Caldera City on Feb. 2–3 would be &quot;treated as a warning, not a surprise.&quot; It said the Empire had built &quot;an aircraft of a size and a kind that has not flown before.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Federal Defense Office, Feb. 5, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;The Office has not seen the CL1201 on radar. It has seen what it carries. It will not say more.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Zaofu\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Suyin Beifong's office said the Beifong Foundation had &quot;long known the design was on paper&quot; and that it would brief the Council in closed session.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Qianchan Report could not confirm that any Imperial aircraft had been recovered.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"qr","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["The Federal Defense Office says the sea routes are now patrolled; the Council asks what the Beifong labs know of the CL1201"],"by":"By the Politics Desk","cr":"","cty":"QIANCHAN","y":181,"m":2,"d":5,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Federal Defense Office says the sea routes are now patrolled; the Council asks what the Beifong labs know of the CL1201","src":"{j} The Federal Defense Office said Thursday it had doubled sea patrols and desert reconnaissance off Qianchan's east coast and had no explanation for how the Empire's aircraft crossed Tajisaki's line unseen, as the Federal Council asked for a briefing on what Zaofu's laboratories had learned of the CL1201.\n\nThe Office said the strike on Caldera City on Feb. 2–3 would be \"treated as a warning, not a surprise.\" It said the Empire had built \"an aircraft of a size and a kind that has not flown before.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Federal Defense Office, Feb. 5, 181 AG (abridged): \"The Office has not seen the CL1201 on radar. It has seen what it carries. It will not say more.\"\n\n## Zaofu\n\nSuyin Beifong's office said the Beifong Foundation had \"long known the design was on paper\" and that it would brief the Council in closed session.\n\n//The Qianchan Report could not confirm that any Imperial aircraft had been recovered.//"},"t":1793720828711},"federal_defense_office_caldera_strike_launched_from_sea_east_coast_patrols_doubled":{"title":"Federal Defense Office: Caldera Strike Launched from Desert; East-Coast Patrols Doubled","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>The Federal Defense Office stated on Feb. 5 that aircraft of the Earth Empire had struck Caldera City and the Fire Navy's gulf squadron on Feb. 2-3 and had not been detected by Tajisaki's radar.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Office stated that the aircraft had been launched from the Si Wong Desert, from a carrier aircraft it designates the CL1201, and that sea patrols off the east coast of Qianchan had been doubled.\u003c/p>\u003cp>* * *\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Federal Council requested a briefing on the CL1201 and the B70. The Office stated that the briefing would be held in closed session on Feb. 8.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"gaozu","t":"report","sec":"Defense","kick":"","deck":["Office States Imperial Aircraft Struck Tajisaki on Feb. 2-3; Federal Council Asks for Briefing"],"by":"By Lin Shaoyu","cr":"","cty":"Gaoling","y":181,"m":2,"d":5,"time":"9:00 a.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Office States Imperial Aircraft Struck Tajisaki on Feb. 2-3; Federal Council Asks for Briefing","src":"The Federal Defense Office stated on Feb. 5 that aircraft of the Earth Empire had struck Caldera City and the Fire Navy's gulf squadron on Feb. 2-3 and had not been detected by Tajisaki's radar.\n\nThe Office stated that the aircraft had been launched from the Si Wong Desert, from a carrier aircraft it designates the CL1201, and that sea patrols off the east coast of Qianchan had been doubled.\n\n* * *\n\nThe Federal Council requested a briefing on the CL1201 and the B70. The Office stated that the briefing would be held in closed session on Feb. 8."},"t":1793720829711},"republic_city_bombed_ministry_of_defense_mint_and_western_rail_yards_hit_the_congress_hall_spared":{"title":"Republic City bombed: Ministry of Defense, Mint and western rail yards hit; the Congress hall spared","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Cruise missiles fired by high-flying Imperial bombers struck Republic City early yesterday, hitting the Ministry of Defense, the Mint and the western rail yards, and killing about 410 people, the Office of the President said.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Congress's hall, the High Council's chambers and the President's residence were not hit. The President said the Republic &quot;has been shown what it cannot defend, and what was left alone.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Office of the President, Feb. 17, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;The Empire struck the capital of a republic at war with it and spared the building where its Congress sits. The Republic will draw its own conclusions from that choice.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The sirens\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The city's air-raid sirens sounded 4 minutes after the first missile. &quot;We heard it and did not know what it was,&quot; said Bao Lin, 33, a rail clerk.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Where from\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Ministry of Defense said it believed the bombers had flown from the Si Wong Desert, where it said the Empire's carrier aircraft, the CL1201, was hidden. It did not say how it knew.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily could not confirm the casualty figures or the Ministry's account of the aircraft's base.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"News","kick":"","deck":["About 410 dead; no alarm until the first missiles; the Ministry says the Empire’s aircraft flew from the Si Wong Desert"],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":181,"m":2,"d":17,"time":"5:50 a.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"About 410 dead; no alarm until the first missiles; the Ministry says the Empire’s aircraft flew from the Si Wong Desert","src":"{j} Cruise missiles fired by high-flying Imperial bombers struck Republic City early yesterday, hitting the Ministry of Defense, the Mint and the western rail yards, and killing about 410 people, the Office of the President said.\n\nThe Congress's hall, the High Council's chambers and the President's residence were not hit. The President said the Republic \"has been shown what it cannot defend, and what was left alone.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Office of the President, Feb. 17, 181 AG (abridged): \"The Empire struck the capital of a republic at war with it and spared the building where its Congress sits. The Republic will draw its own conclusions from that choice.\"\n\n## The sirens\n\nThe city's air-raid sirens sounded 4 minutes after the first missile. \"We heard it and did not know what it was,\" said Bao Lin, 33, a rail clerk.\n\n## Where from\n\nThe Ministry of Defense said it believed the bombers had flown from the Si Wong Desert, where it said the Empire's carrier aircraft, the CL1201, was hidden. It did not say how it knew.\n\n//The Daily could not confirm the casualty figures or the Ministry's account of the aircraft's base.//"},"t":1793744467537},"republic_city_also_bombed_navy_says_empire_is_striking_the_capitals_one_at_a_time":{"title":"Republic City also bombed; Navy says Empire \"is striking the capitals one at a time\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Earth Empire bombed Republic City on Tuesday, two weeks after Caldera City, and the Ministry of the Navy told the Diet it believed the Empire was &quot;striking the capitals one at a time.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Foreign Minister Hayato Mori said Tajisaki had offered the Republic 40 of its new interceptors. Ishikawa Reiko asked whether Gaoling would be next. The Minister said the Ministry &quot;cannot say that it will not.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of the Navy, Feb. 18, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;The Empire's aircraft appear to come from the same place each time. The Navy believes it is the desert.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Scire could not confirm the Ministry's account.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Foreign Minister Mori says Tajisaki has offered the Republic its interceptors; Diet asks whether Gaoling is next"],"by":"By Mika Tsuneda","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":181,"m":2,"d":17,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Foreign Minister Mori says Tajisaki has offered the Republic its interceptors; Diet asks whether Gaoling is next","src":"{j} The Earth Empire bombed Republic City on Tuesday, two weeks after Caldera City, and the Ministry of the Navy told the Diet it believed the Empire was \"striking the capitals one at a time.\"\n\nForeign Minister Hayato Mori said Tajisaki had offered the Republic 40 of its new interceptors. Ishikawa Reiko asked whether Gaoling would be next. The Minister said the Ministry \"cannot say that it will not.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of the Navy, Feb. 18, 181 AG (abridged): \"The Empire's aircraft appear to come from the same place each time. The Navy believes it is the desert.\"\n\n//The Scire could not confirm the Ministry's account.//"},"t":1793744468537},"gaoling_bombed_federal_defense_office_says_the_parliament_building_was_spared":{"title":"Gaoling bombed; Federal Defense Office says the Parliament building was spared","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Imperial bombers struck Gaoling before dawn on March 4, hitting the Federal Defense Office and the city airfield and killing about 230 people, the Defense Office said.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Parliament building was not hit. The Federal Council met in session by 9 a.m.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Federal Defense Office, Mar. 5, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;The Empire has now struck all three capitals from the same desert. The Office has asked the Council for authority to find where.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Zaofu\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Suyin Beifong's office said the Beifong Foundation would &quot;lend its engineers and its aircraft to the search.&quot; It said the Foundation's airships had begun flights over the western Si Wong on March 1.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Qianchan Report could not confirm the casualty figures.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"qr","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["About 230 dead at the Defense Office and the airfield; the Council meets in session under guard"],"by":"By the Politics Desk","cr":"","cty":"QIANCHAN","y":181,"m":3,"d":5,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"About 230 dead at the Defense Office and the airfield; the Council meets in session under guard","src":"{j} Imperial bombers struck Gaoling before dawn on March 4, hitting the Federal Defense Office and the city airfield and killing about 230 people, the Defense Office said.\n\nThe Parliament building was not hit. The Federal Council met in session by 9 a.m.\n\n**Official statement.** Federal Defense Office, Mar. 5, 181 AG (abridged): \"The Empire has now struck all three capitals from the same desert. The Office has asked the Council for authority to find where.\"\n\n## Zaofu\n\nSuyin Beifong's office said the Beifong Foundation would \"lend its engineers and its aircraft to the search.\" It said the Foundation's airships had begun flights over the western Si Wong on March 1.\n\n//The Qianchan Report could not confirm the casualty figures.//"},"t":1793744469537},"federal_defense_office_gaoling_struck_mar_4_parliament_sits":{"title":"Federal Defense Office: Gaoling Struck Mar. 4; Parliament Sits","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>The Federal Defense Office stated on Mar. 5 that Imperial aircraft had struck Gaoling on Mar. 4, hitting the Federal Defense Office and the airfield, and that about 230 persons had been killed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Office of the Speaker stated that Parliament had met on Mar. 4 at 10 o'clock and had voted to authorize the Defense Office to search the western Si Wong.\u003c/p>\u003cp>* * *\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Federal Defense Office stated that the Imperial aircraft were believed to have flown from the Si Wong Desert, and that the Office would not state how it knew.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"gaozu","t":"report","sec":"Defense","kick":"","deck":["Two Hundred Thirty Killed; Parliament Building Not Hit; Council Authorizes Search of Western Desert"],"by":"By Lin Shaoyu","cr":"","cty":"Gaoling","y":181,"m":3,"d":5,"time":"8:30 a.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Two Hundred Thirty Killed; Parliament Building Not Hit; Council Authorizes Search of Western Desert","src":"The Federal Defense Office stated on Mar. 5 that Imperial aircraft had struck Gaoling on Mar. 4, hitting the Federal Defense Office and the airfield, and that about 230 persons had been killed.\n\nThe Office of the Speaker stated that Parliament had met on Mar. 4 at 10 o'clock and had voted to authorize the Defense Office to search the western Si Wong.\n\n* * *\n\nThe Federal Defense Office stated that the Imperial aircraft were believed to have flown from the Si Wong Desert, and that the Office would not state how it knew."},"t":1793744470537},"zaofu_airships_find_the_empire_s_desert_base_strip_empty_carrier_gone":{"title":"Zaofu airships find the Empire’s desert base; strip empty, carrier gone","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Airships of the Beifong Foundation found the Earth Empire's hidden base in the deep Si Wong Desert on June 2, and the Federal Defense Office said Wednesday that the carrier aircraft the Empire used to strike the three capitals had left it days earlier.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The base is a dry-lake strip and a ring of buried hangars. The Office said the airships found fuel dumps, a reactor service bay and cradles for three B70 bombers, and the ruts of &quot;a very large aircraft.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Federal Defense Office, June 3, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;The base was used until about the end of May. The aircraft is not there. The Office does not know where it is.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The search\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Office said the search began after the strike on Gaoling on March 4. The Foundation's airships flew over the western Si Wong 63 times. Suyin Beifong's office said an airship pilot had seen &quot;the shadow of the hangar roofs at noon.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">What is not known\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Office would not say whether the CL1201 had been seen leaving. It said no allied radar had tracked it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Qianchan Report could not confirm the Office's account of what was found.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"qr","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["Beifong Foundation craft found a dry-lake strip and hangars on June 2; the Defense Office says the CL1201 left about the end of May"],"by":"By the Politics Desk","cr":"","cty":"QIANCHAN","y":181,"m":6,"d":3,"time":"5:10 p.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Beifong Foundation craft found a dry-lake strip and hangars on June 2; the Defense Office says the CL1201 left about the end of May","src":"{j} Airships of the Beifong Foundation found the Earth Empire's hidden base in the deep Si Wong Desert on June 2, and the Federal Defense Office said Wednesday that the carrier aircraft the Empire used to strike the three capitals had left it days earlier.\n\nThe base is a dry-lake strip and a ring of buried hangars. The Office said the airships found fuel dumps, a reactor service bay and cradles for three B70 bombers, and the ruts of \"a very large aircraft.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Federal Defense Office, June 3, 181 AG (abridged): \"The base was used until about the end of May. The aircraft is not there. The Office does not know where it is.\"\n\n## The search\n\nThe Office said the search began after the strike on Gaoling on March 4. The Foundation's airships flew over the western Si Wong 63 times. Suyin Beifong's office said an airship pilot had seen \"the shadow of the hangar roofs at noon.\"\n\n## What is not known\n\nThe Office would not say whether the CL1201 had been seen leaving. It said no allied radar had tracked it.\n\n//The Qianchan Report could not confirm the Office's account of what was found.//"},"t":1793745517187},"federal_defense_office_imperial_desert_base_found_carrier_aircraft_absent":{"title":"Federal Defense Office: Imperial Desert Base Found; Carrier Aircraft Absent","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>The Federal Defense Office stated on June 3 that the base used by the Earth Empire's carrier aircraft had been found in the Si Wong Desert on June 2 by airships of the Beifong Foundation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Office stated that the base had been empty. It stated that three bomber cradles and a reactor service bay had been found, and that the aircraft had left about the end of May.\u003c/p>\u003cp>* * *\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Federal Council was informed on June 3. The Council voted to ask the Concord of Nations for a standing watch on the western desert.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"gaozu","t":"report","sec":"Defense","kick":"","deck":["Strip Found June 2 by Beifong Airships; Fuel and Hangars Taken; Federal Council Told Aircraft Is Elsewhere"],"by":"By Lin Shaoyu","cr":"","cty":"Gaoling","y":181,"m":6,"d":3,"time":"8:15 a.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Strip Found June 2 by Beifong Airships; Fuel and Hangars Taken; Federal Council Told Aircraft Is Elsewhere","src":"The Federal Defense Office stated on June 3 that the base used by the Earth Empire's carrier aircraft had been found in the Si Wong Desert on June 2 by airships of the Beifong Foundation.\n\nThe Office stated that the base had been empty. It stated that three bomber cradles and a reactor service bay had been found, and that the aircraft had left about the end of May.\n\n* * *\n\nThe Federal Council was informed on June 3. The Council voted to ask the Concord of Nations for a standing watch on the western desert."},"t":1793745518187},"republic_s_army_reports_heavy_losses_in_the_south_dunhua_besieged_for_the_22nd_day":{"title":"Republic’s army reports heavy losses in the south; Dunhua besieged for the 22nd day","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Empire's army has besieged Dunhua for 22 days, the Ministry of Defense said Tuesday, and the Republic has counted about 22,000 dead in the south since July.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Ministry said six Imperial divisions had come through the South Gate, the corridor at the desert's south-west edge, which the Republic had held since the war began. The Ministry said the Republic's air arm could not stop them because the Empire's guns were under the mountains' lee.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Defense, Aug. 13, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;Dunhua will be held. The Republic's reserves are moving. The militia of the south is in the line.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The siege\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Refugees from Dunhua said the Imperial guns fired from the east at night. &quot;We could hear the shells from the hills and not the guns,&quot; said Lin Mu, 40, a teacher who left on Aug. 8.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily could not confirm the casualty figures.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"News","kick":"","deck":["The Ministry of Defense says the Empire has moved six divisions through the South Gate; casualties are mounting"],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":181,"m":8,"d":13,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Ministry of Defense says the Empire has moved six divisions through the South Gate; casualties are mounting","src":"{j} The Empire's army has besieged Dunhua for 22 days, the Ministry of Defense said Tuesday, and the Republic has counted about 22,000 dead in the south since July.\n\nThe Ministry said six Imperial divisions had come through the South Gate, the corridor at the desert's south-west edge, which the Republic had held since the war began. The Ministry said the Republic's air arm could not stop them because the Empire's guns were under the mountains' lee.\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Defense, Aug. 13, 181 AG (abridged): \"Dunhua will be held. The Republic's reserves are moving. The militia of the south is in the line.\"\n\n## The siege\n\nRefugees from Dunhua said the Imperial guns fired from the east at night. \"We could hear the shells from the hills and not the guns,\" said Lin Mu, 40, a teacher who left on Aug. 8.\n\n//The Daily could not confirm the casualty figures.//"},"t":1793745519187},"dunhua_falls_after_41_days_ministry_says_18_000_dead":{"title":"Dunhua falls after 41 days; Ministry says 41,000 dead","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Imperial forces entered Dunhua on Sept. 22 after a 41-day siege, the Ministry of Defense said, and the Republic has lost about 41,000 soldiers and civilians in the battle.\u003c/p>\u003cp>It is the first Republic city taken since the war began in December.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Office of the President, Sept. 23, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;Dunhua is lost. The Republic will take it back. The President has asked the Congress for the second mobilization.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Congress\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The House voted 233 to 40 for the second mobilization. A High Council member who voted for it said afterward that the Council had &quot;not been told what the Empire has at Dunhua.&quot; The Daily could not reach him again.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily could not confirm the Ministry's figures.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"News","kick":"","deck":["The Empire holds the first Republic city since the war began; the President asks the Congress for the second mobilization"],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":181,"m":9,"d":23,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Empire holds the first Republic city since the war began; the President asks the Congress for the second mobilization","src":"{j} Imperial forces entered Dunhua on Sept. 22 after a 41-day siege, the Ministry of Defense said, and the Republic has lost about 41,000 soldiers and civilians in the battle.\n\nIt is the first Republic city taken since the war began in December.\n\n**Official statement.** Office of the President, Sept. 23, 181 AG (abridged): \"Dunhua is lost. The Republic will take it back. The President has asked the Congress for the second mobilization.\"\n\n## The Congress\n\nThe House voted 233 to 40 for the second mobilization. A High Council member who voted for it said afterward that the Council had \"not been told what the Empire has at Dunhua.\" The Daily could not reach him again.\n\n//The Daily could not confirm the Ministry's figures.//"},"t":1793745520187},"imperial_army_enters_dunhua_the_ministry_says_the_first_fruit_of_the_south":{"title":"Imperial army enters Dunhua; the Ministry says \"the first fruit of the south\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Imperial Ministry of Defense announced on Sept. 23 that the army of the Empire had entered Dunhua and &quot;holds the first fruit of the south.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Imperial Ministry of Defense, Sept. 23, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;The Republic's resistance is broken at Dunhua. The army will rest and continue.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Times has not been permitted to send a correspondent to Dunhua. It has been told by the Ministry of Information that the Empress &quot;has been pleased.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Under the Press Order, the Times may print no casualty figures for the Empire's army.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"bst","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Times is permitted to say that the Empire holds a city of the Republic; it has no account from inside"],"by":"By the Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"BA SING SE","y":181,"m":9,"d":24,"time":"11:20 p.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Times is permitted to say that the Empire holds a city of the Republic; it has no account from inside","src":"{j} The Imperial Ministry of Defense announced on Sept. 23 that the army of the Empire had entered Dunhua and \"holds the first fruit of the south.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Imperial Ministry of Defense, Sept. 23, 181 AG (abridged): \"The Republic's resistance is broken at Dunhua. The army will rest and continue.\"\n\nThe Times has not been permitted to send a correspondent to Dunhua. It has been told by the Ministry of Information that the Empress \"has been pleased.\"\n\nUnder the Press Order, the Times may print no casualty figures for the Empire's army."},"t":1793745521187},"republic_loses_dunhua_tajisaki_sends_the_fire_navy_s_northern_squadron_to_the_southern_coast":{"title":"Republic loses Dunhua; Tajisaki sends the Fire Navy’s northern squadron to the southern coast","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The fall of Dunhua on Sept. 22 has become the first reverse of the war for the allies, Foreign Minister Hayato Mori told the Diet on Wednesday, and the Navy has sent its northern squadron to the Republic's southern coast.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Diet voted 380 to 30 for further aid to the Republic. Daichi Mori said the Empire had shown that &quot;nothing the Concord promised us can be counted on, not even its silence.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Sept. 25, 181 AG (abridged): &quot;The Kingdom of Tajisaki stands with the Republic. It will not stand idle.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Scire could not confirm the Republic's casualty figures.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Foreign Minister Mori says the war \"has become a land war after all\"; the Diet votes 380 to 30 for more aid"],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":181,"m":9,"d":25,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Foreign Minister Mori says the war \"has become a land war after all\"; the Diet votes 380 to 30 for more aid","src":"{j} The fall of Dunhua on Sept. 22 has become the first reverse of the war for the allies, Foreign Minister Hayato Mori told the Diet on Wednesday, and the Navy has sent its northern squadron to the Republic's southern coast.\n\nThe Diet voted 380 to 30 for further aid to the Republic. Daichi Mori said the Empire had shown that \"nothing the Concord promised us can be counted on, not even its silence.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Sept. 25, 181 AG (abridged): \"The Kingdom of Tajisaki stands with the Republic. It will not stand idle.\"\n\n//The Scire could not confirm the Republic's casualty figures.//"},"t":1793745522187},"republic_retakes_dunhua_at_a_cost_of_31_000_the_city_is_rubble":{"title":"Republic retakes Dunhua at a cost of 31,000; the city \"is rubble\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Republic forces entered Dunhua on Feb. 14, five months after the Empire took it, and the Ministry of Defense said the Republic had lost about 31,000 soldiers in the counter-offensive.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Ministry said the Empire's army had withdrawn to the South Gate. The city, the Ministry said, &quot;is rubble.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Defense, Feb. 15, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;Dunhua is taken. The Republic will not advance beyond the gate.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The toll\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Ministry has counted about 150,000 Republic dead since December 180. It said the Empire's losses had been &quot;less than half that,&quot; and that the Republic had been &quot;fighting on its own ground, under its own bombs.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily could not confirm the Ministry's figures.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"News","kick":"","deck":["The counter-offensive reached the city on the 14th; the Ministry says the Empire’s army has withdrawn to the South Gate"],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"REPUBLIC CITY","y":182,"m":2,"d":15,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The counter-offensive reached the city on the 14th; the Ministry says the Empire’s army has withdrawn to the South Gate","src":"{j} Republic forces entered Dunhua on Feb. 14, five months after the Empire took it, and the Ministry of Defense said the Republic had lost about 31,000 soldiers in the counter-offensive.\n\nThe Ministry said the Empire's army had withdrawn to the South Gate. The city, the Ministry said, \"is rubble.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Defense, Feb. 15, 182 AG (abridged): \"Dunhua is taken. The Republic will not advance beyond the gate.\"\n\n## The toll\n\nThe Ministry has counted about 150,000 Republic dead since December 180. It said the Empire's losses had been \"less than half that,\" and that the Republic had been \"fighting on its own ground, under its own bombs.\"\n\n//The Daily could not confirm the Ministry's figures.//"},"t":1793745523187},"kuvira_dead_imperial_ministry_announces_the_empress_is_no_longer_with_us_armistice_rumored":{"title":"Imperial radio says Empress dead, then retracts: Ministry calls statement \"premature\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Imperial Ministry of Information announced on Sept. 4 that the Empress Kuvira had died in the Western Agricultural Sector and that the army &quot;stands to arms,&quot; and withdrew the statement on Sept. 5, saying it was &quot;premature.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Imperial Ministry of Information, Sept. 4, 182 AG (full text): &quot;The Empress is no longer with us. The army awaits orders. The people are asked to remain calm.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Times has no named source for the cause of death or for the events that led to it. It was told by the Ministry that the Press Order &quot;remains in force.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Persons in Ba Sing Se told the Times that the radio had played a hymn for six hours.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"bst","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Times prints the Ministry’s one-line statement; it has no further source; the Press Order is \"under review\""],"by":"By the Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"BA SING SE","y":182,"m":9,"d":5,"time":"9:50 p.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Times prints the Ministry’s one-line statement; it has no further source; the Press Order is \"under review\"","src":"{j} The Imperial Ministry of Information announced on Sept. 4 that the Empress Kuvira had died in the Western Agricultural Sector and that the army \"stands to arms,\" and withdrew the statement on Sept. 5, saying it was \"premature.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Imperial Ministry of Information, Sept. 4, 182 AG (full text): \"The Empress is no longer with us. The army awaits orders. The people are asked to remain calm.\"\n\nThe Times has no named source for the cause of death or for the events that led to it. It was told by the Ministry that the Press Order \"remains in force.\"\n\nPersons in Ba Sing Se told the Times that the radio had played a hymn for six hours."},"t":1793745524187},"giant_bomb_hits_republic_city_s_congress_hall_government_moves_to_yu_dao":{"title":"Giant bomb hits Republic City’s Congress hall; government moves to Yu Dao","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">A single bomb, the largest conventional weapon the Republic has seen, destroyed the Congress hall in Republic City on Nov. 24 and killed about 4,300 people, and the government has moved to Yu Dao, the Office of the President said.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Congress and the Cabinet had left the capital on Nov. 22. The Office said the bomb was dropped from a B70. It said the blast was &quot;of a size that the Ministry of Defense would have called an atomic bomb, if it were one.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Office of the President, Nov. 25, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Empire has aimed at the Republic's Congress for the first time. It had spared it for two years. The Republic's government continues at Yu Dao.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The bomb\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Witnesses said the bomb fell near noon and left a crater 300 meters across. &quot;It was white, and then there was a wind that took the doors off the street,&quot; said Hao Jin, 47, a printer who was a kilometer away.\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The government\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Congress will sit at Yu Dao from Dec. 1. The Daily moved its offices on Nov. 23.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily could not confirm the casualty figure.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"News","kick":"","deck":["About 4,300 dead; the Congress and Cabinet had left; the Ministry says the weapon was dropped from a B70"],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"YU DAO","y":182,"m":11,"d":25,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"About 4,300 dead; the Congress and Cabinet had left; the Ministry says the weapon was dropped from a B70","src":"{j} A single bomb, the largest conventional weapon the Republic has seen, destroyed the Congress hall in Republic City on Nov. 24 and killed about 4,300 people, and the government has moved to Yu Dao, the Office of the President said.\n\nThe Congress and the Cabinet had left the capital on Nov. 22. The Office said the bomb was dropped from a B70. It said the blast was \"of a size that the Ministry of Defense would have called an atomic bomb, if it were one.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Office of the President, Nov. 25, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Empire has aimed at the Republic's Congress for the first time. It had spared it for two years. The Republic's government continues at Yu Dao.\"\n\n## The bomb\n\nWitnesses said the bomb fell near noon and left a crater 300 meters across. \"It was white, and then there was a wind that took the doors off the street,\" said Hao Jin, 47, a printer who was a kilometer away.\n\n## The government\n\nThe Congress will sit at Yu Dao from Dec. 1. The Daily moved its offices on Nov. 23.\n\n//The Daily could not confirm the casualty figure.//"},"t":1793748375108},"republic_s_capital_bombed_with_a_weapon_the_navy_compares_to_an_atomic_bomb_palace_says_caldera_is_ready":{"title":"Republic’s capital bombed with a weapon the Navy compares to an atomic bomb; Palace says Caldera is \"ready\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Earth Empire destroyed the Congress hall of the Republic of Nations with a single bomb on Nov. 24, and the Ministry of the Navy told the Diet on Wednesday that the Fire Navy's new radar net &quot;is now watching the southern approaches of the Si Wong.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Ministry said the bomb was the largest conventional weapon known. Daichi Mori asked the Minister whether it was &quot;a rehearsal.&quot; The Minister said, &quot;We do not know what it is a rehearsal for.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Palace, Nov. 26, 182 AG (printed in full): &quot;The Kingdom of Tajisaki stands with the Republic in its exile. Caldera City is ready.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Scire could not confirm the Republic's figures.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Navy says the radar net built after the Night of No Sirens is now watching the southern desert approaches"],"by":"By Mika Tsuneda","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":182,"m":11,"d":26,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Navy says the radar net built after the Night of No Sirens is now watching the southern desert approaches","src":"{j} The Earth Empire destroyed the Congress hall of the Republic of Nations with a single bomb on Nov. 24, and the Ministry of the Navy told the Diet on Wednesday that the Fire Navy's new radar net \"is now watching the southern approaches of the Si Wong.\"\n\nThe Ministry said the bomb was the largest conventional weapon known. Daichi Mori asked the Minister whether it was \"a rehearsal.\" The Minister said, \"We do not know what it is a rehearsal for.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Palace, Nov. 26, 182 AG (printed in full): \"The Kingdom of Tajisaki stands with the Republic in its exile. Caldera City is ready.\"\n\n//The Scire could not confirm the Republic's figures.//"},"t":1793748376108},"imperial_army_holds_the_capital_of_the_republic_ministry_names_the_bomb_the_hammer_of_the_empress":{"title":"Imperial army \"holds the capital of the Republic\"; Ministry names the bomb the \"Hammer of the Empress\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Imperial Ministry of Defense said on Dec. 1 that the army of the Empire &quot;holds Republic City&quot; and that its capture followed the destruction of &quot;the Republic's House of Talk&quot; by the &quot;Hammer of the Empress&quot; on Nov. 24.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Imperial Ministry of Defense, Dec. 1, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The capital of the Republic is the Empire's. The Republic's government has fled to Yu Dao. It will be followed.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Times has not been able to send a correspondent to Republic City or to Yu Dao. It has been told that the Press Order &quot;remains in force.&quot;\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"bst","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Times prints the Ministry’s communiqué; it has no access to Republic City"],"by":"By the Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"BA SING SE","y":182,"m":12,"d":2,"time":"11:05 p.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Times prints the Ministry’s communiqué; it has no access to Republic City","src":"{j} The Imperial Ministry of Defense said on Dec. 1 that the army of the Empire \"holds Republic City\" and that its capture followed the destruction of \"the Republic's House of Talk\" by the \"Hammer of the Empress\" on Nov. 24.\n\n**Official statement.** Imperial Ministry of Defense, Dec. 1, 182 AG (abridged): \"The capital of the Republic is the Empire's. The Republic's government has fled to Yu Dao. It will be followed.\"\n\nThe Times has not been able to send a correspondent to Republic City or to Yu Dao. It has been told that the Press Order \"remains in force.\""},"t":1793748377108},"republic_city_falls_paratroops_and_armor_enter_after_the_bombing_ministry_says_50_000_dead":{"title":"Republic City falls; paratroops and armor enter after the bombing; Ministry says 50,000 dead","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Imperial paratroops and armor entered Republic City on Dec. 1 and the city has fallen, the Ministry of Defense said, after nine days of fighting in which about 50,000 people were killed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Congress met at Yu Dao on Dec. 2 for its first sitting outside the capital.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Office of the President, Dec. 3, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Republic has lost its capital. It has not lost its government or its people. The Republic will not surrender a city twice.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The sitting\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Congress voted 201 to 18 to continue the war. A member of the High Council said afterward that the Council had been told the Empire's army &quot;has not yet reached Yu Dao.&quot; The Daily could not confirm that.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily could not confirm the casualty figure.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"News","kick":"","deck":["The Republic has lost its capital; the Congress sits at Yu Dao; the President says the Republic \"will not surrender a city twice\""],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"YU DAO","y":182,"m":12,"d":3,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Republic has lost its capital; the Congress sits at Yu Dao; the President says the Republic \"will not surrender a city twice\"","src":"{j} Imperial paratroops and armor entered Republic City on Dec. 1 and the city has fallen, the Ministry of Defense said, after nine days of fighting in which about 50,000 people were killed.\n\nThe Congress met at Yu Dao on Dec. 2 for its first sitting outside the capital.\n\n**Official statement.** Office of the President, Dec. 3, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Republic has lost its capital. It has not lost its government or its people. The Republic will not surrender a city twice.\"\n\n## The sitting\n\nThe Congress voted 201 to 18 to continue the war. A member of the High Council said afterward that the Council had been told the Empire's army \"has not yet reached Yu Dao.\" The Daily could not confirm that.\n\n//The Daily could not confirm the casualty figure.//"},"t":1793748378108},"reports_say_kuvira_dead_at_the_hands_of_the_avatar_beifong_office_cannot_confirm":{"title":"Reports say Kuvira dead at the hands of the Avatar; Beifong office \"cannot confirm\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Reports from the Earth Empire's western provinces said Kuvira was dead at the hands of the Avatar, Korra, and Suyin Beifong's office said on Friday that it &quot;cannot confirm it.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Federal Defense Office said Imperial radio had been silent for three days. It said an Imperial order of battle had been intercepted that named a &quot;Xinan command&quot; as the &quot;authority of the army.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Federal Defense Office, Dec. 7, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Office has no confirmation of the Empress's death. The Office has noted that the Imperial command has changed.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Avatar\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Avatar's office in the Southern Water Tribe said Korra had &quot;not made a statement.&quot; A spokesman for Zaofu said Suyin Beifong &quot;has not heard from Korra in three weeks.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Qianchan Report could not confirm any of the reports.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"qr","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["The Empire has not commented; the Federal Defense Office says Imperial radio has been silent for three days"],"by":"By the Politics Desk","cr":"","cty":"QIANCHAN","y":182,"m":12,"d":7,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Empire has not commented; the Federal Defense Office says Imperial radio has been silent for three days","src":"{j} Reports from the Earth Empire's western provinces said Kuvira was dead at the hands of the Avatar, Korra, and Suyin Beifong's office said on Friday that it \"cannot confirm it.\"\n\nThe Federal Defense Office said Imperial radio had been silent for three days. It said an Imperial order of battle had been intercepted that named a \"Xinan command\" as the \"authority of the army.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Federal Defense Office, Dec. 7, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Office has no confirmation of the Empress's death. The Office has noted that the Imperial command has changed.\"\n\n## The Avatar\n\nThe Avatar's office in the Southern Water Tribe said Korra had \"not made a statement.\" A spokesman for Zaofu said Suyin Beifong \"has not heard from Korra in three weeks.\"\n\n//The Qianchan Report could not confirm any of the reports.//"},"t":1793748379108},"unconfirmed_kuvira_dead_fire_lord_s_office_says_no_comment_on_the_avatar_s_whereabouts":{"title":"Unconfirmed: Kuvira dead; Fire Lord’s office says \"no comment on the Avatar’s whereabouts\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Palace said on Monday it had no comment on reports from the Earth lands that Kuvira had died at the hands of the Avatar, and Foreign Minister Hayato Mori told the Diet that the Concord's staff had been told the Empire's forces were now under a &quot;Xinan command.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Mr. Mori said the report was &quot;unconfirmed and should be treated that way.&quot; Ishikawa Reiko asked whether a new command would end the war or widen it. The Minister said he did not know.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Dec. 8, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Kingdom of Tajisaki has not been informed of any change of ruler in the Earth Empire.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Scire could not confirm the report.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Foreign Minister Mori says the Concord has been told the Empire’s forces are now under a \"Xinan command\""],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":182,"m":12,"d":8,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Foreign Minister Mori says the Concord has been told the Empire’s forces are now under a \"Xinan command\"","src":"{j} The Palace said on Monday it had no comment on reports from the Earth lands that Kuvira had died at the hands of the Avatar, and Foreign Minister Hayato Mori told the Diet that the Concord's staff had been told the Empire's forces were now under a \"Xinan command.\"\n\nMr. Mori said the report was \"unconfirmed and should be treated that way.\" Ishikawa Reiko asked whether a new command would end the war or widen it. The Minister said he did not know.\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Dec. 8, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Kingdom of Tajisaki has not been informed of any change of ruler in the Earth Empire.\"\n\n//The Scire could not confirm the report.//"},"t":1793748380108},"xinan_command_says_it_holds_the_empire_s_powers_ministry_says_the_war_will_end_this_month":{"title":"\"Xinan command\" says it holds the Empire’s powers; Ministry says the war \"will end this month\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">A &quot;Xinan command&quot; announced on Dec. 10 that it held the powers of the Earth Empire and that the war &quot;will end this month.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Xinan command, Dec. 10, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Empire's army has a command. The war will end by the end of this month, by the means the Empire holds.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Times has no information on the Empress. The Ministry of Information has said only that &quot;the Empress's affairs are with the command.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Times was told to print the communiqué without comment.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"bst","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Times has no information on the Empress; the Ministry of Information says only that the army \"has a command\""],"by":"By the Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"BA SING SE","y":182,"m":12,"d":11,"time":"10:00 p.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Times has no information on the Empress; the Ministry of Information says only that the army \"has a command\"","src":"{j} A \"Xinan command\" announced on Dec. 10 that it held the powers of the Earth Empire and that the war \"will end this month.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Xinan command, Dec. 10, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Empire's army has a command. The war will end by the end of this month, by the means the Empire holds.\"\n\nThe Times has no information on the Empress. The Ministry of Information has said only that \"the Empress's affairs are with the command.\"\n\nThe Times was told to print the communiqué without comment."},"t":1793748381108},"atomic_bomb_destroys_yu_dao_in_an_air_burst_zaofu_and_gaoling_struck_on_the_20th":{"title":"Atomic bomb destroys Yu Dao in an air burst; Zaofu and Gaoling struck on the 20th","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Atomic bombs destroyed Yu Dao on Dec. 19, and Zaofu and Gaoling a day later, and the Fire Navy said on Tuesday that its radar net had shot down an atomic bomber over the gulf that was headed for Caldera City.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Republic's Congress and Cabinet were sitting at Yu Dao. The Palace said the government &quot;has been lost,&quot; and that the Republic's survivors were being counted. The Concord's relief agency said about 210,000 had died at Yu Dao, 160,000 at Zaofu and 190,000 at Gaoling in the first count.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Palace, Dec. 21, 182 AG (printed in full): &quot;The Earth Empire has used atomic weapons on three cities. The Fire Lord has ordered the Navy to defend Caldera. The Fire Lord asks the Concord to act today.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The bomber\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Ministry of the Navy said the bomber was a B70 and that the Navy had shot it down 90 kilometers from the coast with the radar net built after the strike of February 181. It would not say how it knew the bomb was aboard.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Scire could not confirm the casualty figures. Concord staff said they were &quot;preliminary.&quot;\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Tajisaki’s radar net shot down an atomic bomber over the gulf; the Palace says the Republic’s government \"has been lost\""],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":182,"m":12,"d":21,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Tajisaki’s radar net shot down an atomic bomber over the gulf; the Palace says the Republic’s government \"has been lost\"","src":"{j} Atomic bombs destroyed Yu Dao on Dec. 19, and Zaofu and Gaoling a day later, and the Fire Navy said on Tuesday that its radar net had shot down an atomic bomber over the gulf that was headed for Caldera City.\n\nThe Republic's Congress and Cabinet were sitting at Yu Dao. The Palace said the government \"has been lost,\" and that the Republic's survivors were being counted. The Concord's relief agency said about 210,000 had died at Yu Dao, 160,000 at Zaofu and 190,000 at Gaoling in the first count.\n\n**Official statement.** Palace, Dec. 21, 182 AG (printed in full): \"The Earth Empire has used atomic weapons on three cities. The Fire Lord has ordered the Navy to defend Caldera. The Fire Lord asks the Concord to act today.\"\n\n## The bomber\n\nThe Ministry of the Navy said the bomber was a B70 and that the Navy had shot it down 90 kilometers from the coast with the radar net built after the strike of February 181. It would not say how it knew the bomb was aboard.\n\n//The Scire could not confirm the casualty figures. Concord staff said they were \"preliminary.\"//"},"t":1793748382108},"zaofu_and_gaoling_hit_by_atomic_bombs_federal_council_votes_13_to_11_against_surrender":{"title":"Zaofu and Gaoling hit by atomic bombs; Federal Council votes 16 to 8, two-thirds, against surrender","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Federal Council of Qianchan voted 16 to 8 on Dec. 20, two-thirds of its 24 members, against surrender, hours after atomic bombs destroyed Zaofu and Gaoling, the Federal Defense Office said.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Council sat in a field outside Tongyuan. The Office said the first counts were about 160,000 dead at Zaofu and 190,000 at Gaoling.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Federal Defense Office, Dec. 22, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Federal Council has voted to continue. The Council has also voted to accept any Concord ceasefire that the Empire's command accepts.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Zaofu\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Suyin Beifong, who was in the Beifong Foundation's airship over the desert, said the Foundation's engineers &quot;have no words.&quot; She said Zaofu's metal domes &quot;stood and did not matter.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">Gaoling\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Parliament had been moved to the field the day before the strike. The Office said that was why &quot;any of the government is left.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Qianchan Report could not confirm the casualty figures.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"qr","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["About 160,000 dead at Zaofu and 190,000 at Gaoling; the Council sat in a field outside Tongyuan"],"by":"By the Politics Desk","cr":"","cty":"QIANCHAN","y":182,"m":12,"d":22,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"About 160,000 dead at Zaofu and 190,000 at Gaoling; the Council sat in a field outside Tongyuan","src":"{j} The Federal Council of Qianchan voted 16 to 8 on Dec. 20, two-thirds of its 24 members, against surrender, hours after atomic bombs destroyed Zaofu and Gaoling, the Federal Defense Office said.\n\nThe Council sat in a field outside Tongyuan. The Office said the first counts were about 160,000 dead at Zaofu and 190,000 at Gaoling.\n\n**Official statement.** Federal Defense Office, Dec. 22, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Federal Council has voted to continue. The Council has also voted to accept any Concord ceasefire that the Empire's command accepts.\"\n\n## Zaofu\n\nSuyin Beifong, who was in the Beifong Foundation's airship over the desert, said the Foundation's engineers \"have no words.\" She said Zaofu's metal domes \"stood and did not matter.\"\n\n## Gaoling\n\nParliament had been moved to the field the day before the strike. The Office said that was why \"any of the government is left.\"\n\n//The Qianchan Report could not confirm the casualty figures.//"},"t":1793748383108},"republic_s_congress_counts_its_dead_after_yu_dao_vice_president_sworn_in_at_tongyuan":{"title":"Republic’s Congress counts its dead after Yu Dao; Vice President sworn in at Tongyuan","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Republic of Nations' Vice President was sworn in at Tongyuan on Dec. 26 after the President and most of the Cabinet were killed at Yu Dao on Dec. 19, the Office of the Vice President said.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Daily's offices at Yu Dao were destroyed. This edition was set in a Qianchan print shop.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Office of the Vice President, Dec. 26, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Republic has lost its capital and its government in the same month. It has not lost the Republic. The Vice President accepts the ceasefire of Dec. 24.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Restoration\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Second Constitutional Reform Act, passed on Dec. 15, has not been put to referendum. The Vice President said it would be &quot;put to the survivors&quot; within a year.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily could not confirm the number of Cabinet members killed.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"News","kick":"","deck":["The Daily’s offices at Yu Dao were destroyed; this edition was set in a Qianchan print shop"],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"TONGYUAN","y":182,"m":12,"d":28,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Daily’s offices at Yu Dao were destroyed; this edition was set in a Qianchan print shop","src":"{j} The Republic of Nations' Vice President was sworn in at Tongyuan on Dec. 26 after the President and most of the Cabinet were killed at Yu Dao on Dec. 19, the Office of the Vice President said.\n\nThe Daily's offices at Yu Dao were destroyed. This edition was set in a Qianchan print shop.\n\n**Official statement.** Office of the Vice President, Dec. 26, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Republic has lost its capital and its government in the same month. It has not lost the Republic. The Vice President accepts the ceasefire of Dec. 24.\"\n\n## The Restoration\n\nThe Second Constitutional Reform Act, passed on Dec. 15, has not been put to referendum. The Vice President said it would be \"put to the survivors\" within a year.\n\n//The Daily could not confirm the number of Cabinet members killed.//"},"t":1793748384108},"concord_ceasefire_holds_war_between_empire_and_republic_ends_on_dec_31":{"title":"Concord ceasefire holds; war between Empire and Republic ends on Dec. 31","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The war that began on Dec. 2, 180 AG ended today, with the Earth Empire's Xinan command and the three allied governments signing a ceasefire arranged by the Concord of Nations.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The ceasefire took effect on Dec. 24. The Xinan command signed it because the Empire’s army had mutinied and its western coast and north had risen in the first weeks of December, and said it had &quot;no more weapons of that kind ready.&quot; The allies said they did not believe it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Concord of Nations, Dec. 31, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The war is over. The Concord will inventory the Empire's arms. The Concord has not been able to account for the aircraft the Empire calls the CL1201.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The toll\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The allies counted about 1.1 million dead on their side, most of them at Yu Dao, Zaofu, Gaoling and Republic City. The Xinan command said the Empire had lost about 120,000. All figures are provisional.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Scire could not confirm the casualty figures.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Xinan command accepted a Concord ceasefire on Dec. 24 and said it had no more weapons \"of that kind\" ready"],"by":"By Mika Tsuneda","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":182,"m":12,"d":31,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Xinan command accepted a Concord ceasefire on Dec. 24 and said it had no more weapons \"of that kind\" ready","src":"{j} The war that began on Dec. 2, 180 AG ended today, with the Earth Empire's Xinan command and the three allied governments signing a ceasefire arranged by the Concord of Nations.\n\nThe ceasefire took effect on Dec. 24. The Xinan command signed it because the Empire’s army had mutinied and its western coast and north had risen in the first weeks of December, and said it had \"no more weapons of that kind ready.\" The allies said they did not believe it.\n\n**Official statement.** Concord of Nations, Dec. 31, 182 AG (abridged): \"The war is over. The Concord will inventory the Empire's arms. The Concord has not been able to account for the aircraft the Empire calls the CL1201.\"\n\n## The toll\n\nThe allies counted about 1.1 million dead on their side, most of them at Yu Dao, Zaofu, Gaoling and Republic City. The Xinan command said the Empire had lost about 120,000. All figures are provisional.\n\n//The Scire could not confirm the casualty figures.//"},"t":1793748385108},"ministry_disturbances_on_the_western_coast_press_order_extended_to_cover_rumors":{"title":"Ministry: \"disturbances\" on the western coast; Press Order extended to cover \"rumors\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Imperial Ministry of Information said on Dec. 8 that there had been &quot;disturbances&quot; at ports on the western coast and that the army &quot;is steady.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Imperial Ministry of Information, Dec. 8, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;Rumors of the Empress's affairs and of the coast are the work of the Empire's enemies. The Press Order is extended to cover rumors.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Times has been told that persons who print the word &quot;mutiny&quot; will be &quot;dealt with as the Order allows.&quot;&quot;\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"bst","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["The Times has been told the army \"is steady\"; it has no correspondent on the coast"],"by":"By the Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"BA SING SE","y":182,"m":12,"d":9,"time":"10:30 p.m.","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Times has been told the army \"is steady\"; it has no correspondent on the coast","src":"{j} The Imperial Ministry of Information said on Dec. 8 that there had been \"disturbances\" at ports on the western coast and that the army \"is steady.\"\n\n**Official statement.** Imperial Ministry of Information, Dec. 8, 182 AG (abridged): \"Rumors of the Empress's affairs and of the coast are the work of the Empire's enemies. The Press Order is extended to cover rumors.\"\n\nThe Times has been told that persons who print the word \"mutiny\" will be \"dealt with as the Order allows.\"\""},"t":1793749189943},"western_coast_in_revolt_garrisons_at_xilan_and_cape_hengshan_join_committees_ports_taken":{"title":"Western coast in revolt: garrisons at Xilan and Cape Hengshan join committees, ports taken","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Imperial garrisons at Xilan and Cape Hengshan, the bases attacked in the false flags of two years ago, have joined committees on the western coast and taken the ports, the Federal Defense Office said on Dec. 12.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Office said &quot;tens of thousands&quot; of conscripts and townspeople were in the streets. It said the Hanyuan Strait ports had begun to follow.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Federal Defense Office, Dec. 12, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Empire's coast is no longer under its command. The Office does not know who commands it.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The bases\u003c/h3>\u003cp>A Qianchan officer who spoke with a Xilan garrison on the radio said the soldiers had named the false flags &quot;the lie.&quot; &quot;They said they had been told the Republic killed 800 of their friends,&quot; he said. &quot;They had been burying them for two years.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Qianchan Report could not confirm the Office's account.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"qr","t":"report","sec":"Politics","kick":"","deck":["The Federal Defense Office says Imperial garrisons on the coast have refused orders from the \"Xinan command\"; tens of thousands rising"],"by":"By the Politics Desk","cr":"","cty":"QIANCHAN","y":182,"m":12,"d":12,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"The Federal Defense Office says Imperial garrisons on the coast have refused orders from the \"Xinan command\"; tens of thousands rising","src":"{j} Imperial garrisons at Xilan and Cape Hengshan, the bases attacked in the false flags of two years ago, have joined committees on the western coast and taken the ports, the Federal Defense Office said on Dec. 12.\n\nThe Office said \"tens of thousands\" of conscripts and townspeople were in the streets. It said the Hanyuan Strait ports had begun to follow.\n\n**Official statement.** Federal Defense Office, Dec. 12, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Empire's coast is no longer under its command. The Office does not know who commands it.\"\n\n## The bases\n\nA Qianchan officer who spoke with a Xilan garrison on the radio said the soldiers had named the false flags \"the lie.\" \"They said they had been told the Republic killed 800 of their friends,\" he said. \"They had been burying them for two years.\"\n\n//The Qianchan Report could not confirm the Office's account.//"},"t":1793749190943},"northern_army_and_provinces_revolt_against_xinan_command_beiyuan_garrison_declares_for_the_committees":{"title":"Northern army and provinces revolt against Xinan command; Beiyuan garrison declares for the committees","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">The Earth Empire's northern army and the northern provinces revolted on Dec. 14, Foreign Minister Hayato Mori told the Diet on Monday, and the garrison at Beiyuan declared for the committees.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The northern rail line has stopped sending grain south. Mr. Mori said the Concord's staff believed the Empire's commanders had been arrested by their own men at at least four garrisons.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Dec. 15, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Kingdom of Tajisaki understands that the Earth Empire has no army in the north. Tajisaki will not cross a border to find out.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Diet\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Ishikawa Reiko asked whether the Empire's collapse made the Republic's war &quot;won.&quot; The Minister said, &quot;It makes it shorter. It does not make it safe.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Scire could not confirm the number of garrisons.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"cs","t":"report","sec":"World","kick":"","deck":["Grain trains stopped on the northern line; Foreign Minister Mori says the Empire \"has no army in the north\""],"by":"By Ayame Fujimoto, Foreign Desk","cr":"","cty":"CALDERA CITY","y":182,"m":12,"d":15,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Grain trains stopped on the northern line; Foreign Minister Mori says the Empire \"has no army in the north\"","src":"{j} The Earth Empire's northern army and the northern provinces revolted on Dec. 14, Foreign Minister Hayato Mori told the Diet on Monday, and the garrison at Beiyuan declared for the committees.\n\nThe northern rail line has stopped sending grain south. Mr. Mori said the Concord's staff believed the Empire's commanders had been arrested by their own men at at least four garrisons.\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Dec. 15, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Kingdom of Tajisaki understands that the Earth Empire has no army in the north. Tajisaki will not cross a border to find out.\"\n\n## The Diet\n\nIshikawa Reiko asked whether the Empire's collapse made the Republic's war \"won.\" The Minister said, \"It makes it shorter. It does not make it safe.\"\n\n//The Scire could not confirm the number of garrisons.//"},"t":1793749191943},"imperial_divisions_at_the_south_gate_surrender_to_republic_lines_ministry_says_the_army_is_dissolving":{"title":"Imperial divisions at the South Gate surrender to Republic lines; Ministry says \"the army is dissolving\"","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp class=\"x-j\">Three Imperial divisions at the South Gate surrendered to Republic lines on Dec. 17, the Ministry of Defense said, and the Ministry said the Empire's army &quot;is dissolving.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The divisions walked in with their officers under arrest. The Ministry said no orders from the Xinan command had reached them in a week.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Official statement.\u003c/b> Ministry of Defense, Dec. 18, 182 AG (abridged): &quot;The Empire's army in the south is not fighting. The Republic accepts its surrender under the laws of war.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003ch3 class=\"xh\">The Congress\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The Congress at Yu Dao said it had received word that the Xinan command &quot;still holds weapons that can be used on cities.&quot; A member of the High Council said the Congress had &quot;no way of knowing where.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ci>The Daily could not confirm the number of men in the three divisions.\u003c/i>\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"rd","t":"report","sec":"News","kick":"","deck":["Three divisions walked in with their officers under arrest; the Ministry says no orders have come from the Xinan command in a week"],"by":"By Daily staff","cr":"","cty":"YU DAO","y":182,"m":12,"d":18,"time":"","ph":"","pc":"","pcr":"","pp":"top","ps":"m","pr":"0","hs":"m","dc":"0","tg":"","rw":"","kf":"","cn":"","lv":"0","sub":"Three divisions walked in with their officers under arrest; the Ministry says no orders have come from the Xinan command in a week","src":"{j} Three Imperial divisions at the South Gate surrendered to Republic lines on Dec. 17, the Ministry of Defense said, and the Ministry said the Empire's army \"is dissolving.\"\n\nThe divisions walked in with their officers under arrest. The Ministry said no orders from the Xinan command had reached them in a week.\n\n**Official statement.** Ministry of Defense, Dec. 18, 182 AG (abridged): \"The Empire's army in the south is not fighting. The Republic accepts its surrender under the laws of war.\"\n\n## The Congress\n\nThe Congress at Yu Dao said it had received word that the Xinan command \"still holds weapons that can be used on cities.\" A member of the High Council said the Congress had \"no way of knowing where.\"\n\n//The Daily could not confirm the number of men in the three divisions.//"},"t":1793749192943},"concord_summit_markets_republic_city":{"title":"Markets Slip in Republic City After Concord Summit Orders New Sanctions","cat":"News","html":"\u003cp>Grain and oil futures opened lower in Republic City, the first full trading day after the Concord summit closed on Oct. 5 with a sanctions package against the Earth Union and the Xinan Dominion, the Republic City Exchange Office reported.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Brokers said the package, agreed by 21 of the Concord's members, bars both states from the Concord clearing house and from insured shipping in member ports. The Exchange Office said insurance quotes on cargoes for the Earth Union rose by about a fifth by the close. Marine underwriters at the Harbor Ward said the figure was &quot;provisional.&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>The summit, held from Oct. 1 to Oct. 5, also called on the Union and the Dominion to cease hostilities over the Si Wong oil fields. Neither state has said it will comply. The Dominion's embassy told reporters that sanctions &quot;do not bind a sovereign house.&quot; The Union's trade office did not respond.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The oil price in Republic City rose 4 percent on the Concord's call for a ceasefire and fell back by the afternoon, the Exchange Office said. Millers and shipping agents said they expected grain from the Earth lands to arrive late. The United Republic's Customs Office said it had not yet received the list of goods covered by the sanctions.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"orn\">* * *\u003c/p>\u003cp>On the floor, the larger worry was the plan to unite the Earth Republic and King Wu's kingdom, which the summit's final statement noted as &quot;under way.&quot; Under the plan, which the delegations from both sides described in outline, the united state would become a constitutional monarchy. Wu has lived in Republic City since 171 AG. No date for the union was given.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A trade adviser to the Earth Republic's delegation said tariffs on Earth grain exports would be &quot;reviewed once the constitution is settled.&quot; The United Republic's Treasury said it would not change its own tariff schedule before the Senate sits in November.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The Exchange Office said trading would continue until the evening bell.\u003c/p>","news":{"o":"gaozu","t":"report","sec":"Port & Trade","kick":"","deck":["Brokers Cite Sanctions on Earth Union and Xinan Dominion; Earth Republic and Wu’s Kingdom Move Toward a Constitutional Monarchy"],"by":"By Mina Mikanazu","cr":"","cty":"Republic City","y":190,"m":10,"d":7,"time":"6:30 p.m.","sub":"Brokers Cite Sanctions on Earth Union and Xinan Dominion; Earth Republic and Wu’s Kingdom Move Toward a Constitutional Monarchy","src":"Grain and oil futures opened lower in Republic City, the first full trading day after the Concord summit closed on Oct. 5 with a sanctions package against the Earth Union and the Xinan Dominion, the Republic City Exchange Office reported.\n\nBrokers said the package, agreed by 21 of the Concord's members, bars both states from the Concord clearing house and from insured shipping in member ports. The Exchange Office said insurance quotes on cargoes for the Earth Union rose by about a fifth by the close. Marine underwriters at the Harbor Ward said the figure was \"provisional.\"\n\nThe summit, held from Oct. 1 to Oct. 5, also called on the Union and the Dominion to cease hostilities over the Si Wong oil fields. Neither state has said it will comply. The Dominion's embassy told reporters that sanctions \"do not bind a sovereign house.\" The Union's trade office did not respond.\n\nThe oil price in Republic City rose 4 percent on the Concord's call for a ceasefire and fell back by the afternoon, the Exchange Office said. Millers and shipping agents said they expected grain from the Earth lands to arrive late. The United Republic's Customs Office said it had not yet received the list of goods covered by the sanctions.\n\n* * *\n\nOn the floor, the larger worry was the plan to unite the Earth Republic and King Wu's kingdom, which the summit's final statement noted as \"under way.\" Under the plan, which the delegations from both sides described in outline, the united state would become a constitutional monarchy. Wu has lived in Republic City since 171 AG. No date for the union was given.\n\nA trade adviser to the Earth Republic's delegation said tariffs on Earth grain exports would be \"reviewed once the constitution is settled.\" The United Republic's Treasury said it would not change its own tariff schedule before the Senate sits in November.\n\nThe Exchange Office said trading would continue until the evening bell."},"t":1793608050205}};
